\id JER - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.25
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Jeremiah
\toc1 Jeremiah
\toc2 Jeremiah
\mt1 Jeremiah
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Introduction
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add These are\add* the messages from Hilkiyah’s son, Jeremiah—one of the priests who were in Anatot in Benjamin’s territory.
\v 2 Yahweh’s messages \add started\add* coming to him when Judah’s King Josiah Amon’s son) was in the thirteenth year of his reign.
\v 3 It continued through the reign of Josiah’s son, King Yehoyakim until the end of the eleventh year of \add the reign\add* of Josiah’s son, King Hezekiah—Jerusalem was exiled in the fifth month \add of that year\add*.
\s1 Jeremiah’s calling
\q1
\v 4 Now Yahweh’s message came to me:
\q1
\v 5 “Before I formed you in your mother, I \add already\add* knew you,
\q1 and before you came out of the womb, I had a plan for you.
\q1 I appointed you as a prophet to the nations.”
\p
\v 6 But I said, “Oh no, Yahweh, my master. Listen, I don’t know how to speak, because I’m \add just\add* a young lad.”
\p
\v 7 but Yahweh told me, “Don’t say that you’re only a child, because you must go to everyone I send you to and you must tell them everything that I command you to say.
\v 8 Don’t be afraid of them, because I’ll be with you to rescue you.”
\m That’s Yahweh’s message.
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh stretched out his hand and touched my mouth and said, “Listen, I’ve put my messages into your mouth.
\v 10 I’m appointing you today over the nations and kingdoms—to uproot and to break down, to destroy and to pull down, to build and to plant.”
\s1 Yahweh encourages Jeremiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 11 Another time, Yahweh’s message came to me, saying, “Jeremiah, what do you see?”
\p “I see a branch from an almond tree,” I replied.
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh told me, “You’ve done well to see that, because I keep watch\f + \fr 1:12 \ft There’s a play on words in the original here, as the Hebrew word for ‘keeping watch’ has the same consonants as the word for ‘almond’.\f* on my message to make sure it happens.”
\p
\v 13 Then Yahweh asked me a second time what I was seeing, and I replied, “I see a pot full of boiling water and it’s tipping towards the north.”
\p
\v 14 “\add Yes,\add* disaster will come from the north \add and\add* be released onto all the inhabitants of this region,” Yahweh continued.
\v 15 “Listen, I’m about to summon all the clans of the northern kingdoms, and they’ll come and \add attack\add* the walls around Jerusalem and all of Judah’s \add other\add* cities, then each king will place his throne at the entrance to Jerusalem’s gates.” That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 16 “\add That’s how\add* I’ll judge my people for their wickedness as they’ve abandoned me and sacrificed to other gods and bowed down to \add idols\add* that they made with their own hands.
\v 17 So Jeremiah, get yourself ready and go and tell them everything that I’ll tell you. Don’t be scared in front of them or else I’ll scare you \add right there\add* in front of them!
\v 18 And listen, today I’ve you \add as strong as\add* a fortified city and an iron pillar and bronze walls, as far as the kings of Judah \add are concerned\add*, along with their officials and priests, and the general population.
\v 19 They’ll oppose you but they won’t \add be able to\add* defeat you because I’ll be with you to rescue you,” says Yahweh.
\c 2
\s1 Israel has deviated from Yahweh
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh gave me a message
\v 2 to declare to Jerusalem’s ears:
\q1 “Yahweh says: I remember your loyal commitment when you were all young
\q1 and your love when you were committed to me.
\q1 You all followed after me in the wilderness where no crops were planted.
\q1
\v 3 Israel was set apart for Yahweh—the firstfruits of his harvest.
\q1 All those who devoured it became guilty—calamity came to them.
\q1 That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 Israel’s ancestors turned away
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 4 Listen to Yahweh’s message you descendants of Jacob,
\q1 and all you clans that are part of Israel.
\q1
\v 5 Yahweh asks: what injustices did you ancestors find in me,
\q1 that \add caused them\add* to become distant from me.
\q1 They chased after vanity and became vain.
\q1
\v 6 They didn’t try to find out where Yahweh was—
\q1 \add the one\add* brought led them out of Egypt
\q1 then led them through the wilderness with its pits and desert areas.
\q1 \add It was\add* a region of deep darkness without water—
\q1 an area that no one passes through, let alone lives in.
\q1
\v 7 Then I took you to the Carmel region
\q1 to eat its fruit and \add other\add* good things.
\q1 However you came and defiled my land
\q1 and made the inheritance that I gave you into something detestable.
\q1
\v 8 The priests didn’t ask where Yahweh was,
\q1 and the handlers of the law didn’t know me.
\q1 The shepherds rebelled against me,
\q1 and the prophets prophesied by Baal
\q1 and they went after \add things\add* that wouldn’t profit them.
\s1 Yahweh’s case against his people
\s4 Both kingdoms
\q1
\v 9 Yahweh declares: I will bring a case against you all,
\q1 and I’ll contend against your \add children and\add* your grandchildren.
\q1
\v 10 If you all cross over \add west\add* to the coasts of Cyprus \add island\add* and look,
\q1 or head \add east\add* to Kedar, ask around and pay careful attention
\q1 to see if something like this has \add ever\add* happened \add before\add*.
\q1
\v 11 Has a country ever swapped out its gods (not that they were \add actual\add* gods)?
\q1 But my people have exchanged their greatness for something of no benefit.
\q1
\v 12 You heavens, be appalled and shudder.
\q1 Become utterly desolate.
\q1 That’s what Yahweh says
\q1
\v 13 because my people have done two evil things:
\q1 1. They’ve abandoned the spring of living water
\q1 \add and 2. They’ve gone and\add* dug wells for themselves—broken cisterns that can’t hold any water.
\s1 Israel’s punishment for abandonment
\s4 Both kingdoms
\q1
\v 14 Is Israel a slave? Was it born into slavery?
\q1 Why has it \add been carried off as\add* plunder?
\q1
\v 15 Young lions roared at it—
\q1 they’ve made their voice heard.
\q1 They’ve made the land into wasteland—
\q1 its cities have become ruins without any inhabitants.
\q1
\v 16 Also the people from Memphis and Tahfanhes have shaved your heads.
\q1
\v 17 Didn’t you bring this on yourself by abandoning your god, Yahweh
\q1 at the time when he was leading you along \add the right\add* path.
\q1
\v 18 So now, what’s the point of travelling to Egypt to drink from Shihor’s waters?
\q1 And why bother taking a trip to Assyria to drink the river water?
\q1
\v 19 Your \add own\add* wickedness will punish you
\q1 and your turning away will rebuke you.
\q1 So see and know that it’s evil and bitter to abandon your god Yahweh,
\q1 and you no longer have respect for me.
\m That’s Commander-in-chief Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 Israel refuses to worship Yahweh
\s4 Both kingdoms
\q1
\v 20 I broke off your yoke from long ago.
\q1 I tore your chains to pieces and you said that you wouldn’t disobey,
\q1 because you’d been lying down with prostitutes on every high hill and under every leafy tree.
\q1
\v 21 I myself had planted you as a choice vine from a reliable seed,
\q1 but how you’ve changed yourself into some degenerate, wild vine,
\q1
\v 22 because even if you wash yourself with bleach or use heaps of soap,
\q1 your guilt \add remains\add* as a stain in front of me.
\m That is the declaration of my master, Yahweh.
\q1
\v 23 How can you say, ‘I’m not defiled—I haven’t worshipped the Baals’?
\q1 Consider your conduct in the valley.
\q1 Recognise what you’ve done:
\q1 \add behaving like\add* a young, female camel that flits here and there.
\q1
\v 24 You’re like a wild, female donkey, accustomed to the wilderness in its desire for the wind.
\q1 Thebes one can hold her back when she’s in heat.
\q1 All those searching for her won’t give up trying to find her at mating time.
\q1
\v 25 Keep your feet from becoming bare, and your throat from thirst,
\q1 but you’ve said that it’s hopeless,
\q1 as you love foreign gods and feel obliged to follow them.
\s1 Israel deserves their punishment
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 26 It’s like the shame \add felt\add* by a thief who gets caught.
\q1 So \add too,\add* \add you\add* Israelites will be shamed:
\q1 the people, their kings and officials, and their priests and prophets
\q1
\v 27 who say to the tree, ‘You’re my father,’
\q1 and to the \add standing\add* stone, ‘You gave birth to me.’
\q1 Yes, they show their backs to me, not their faces,
\q1 but when calamity comes, they’ll say, ‘Do something and rescue us!’
\b
\q1
\v 28 So where \add then\add* are the gods that you made for yourself?
\q1 Let them take action if they can save you at the time of your calamity,
\q1 because you have as many gods and you have cities in Judah.
\q1
\v 29 Why do you all complain to me, \add when\add* all of you have rebelled against me?
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 30 It was a waste of time when I beat your sons—they wouldn’t accept discipline.
\q1 You’ve all killed your prophets like a destructive lion.
\q1
\v 31 \add You in this\add* generation, pay attention to Yahweh’s message.
\q1 Have I been a wilderness to Israel, or a land of thick darkness?
\q1 Why would my people say that they’ve now gone their own ways
\q1 →\add and so\add* won’t come back to me again?
\q1
\v 32 Would a young woman forget \add to wear\add* her jewelry,
\q1 or a bride forget to wear her \add wedding\add* sashes?
\q1 However my people have forgotten me for such a long time.
\q1
\v 33 You’ve become very good at finding love—
\q1 you \add could\add* even teach prostitutes a thing or two!
\q1
\v 34 The blood of innocent, poor people was even found on your clothes—
\q1 it wasn’t because you caught them breaking in, but you did it anyway
\q1
\v 35 then you said, ‘But I’m innocent—surely he’s not angry at me.’
\q1 Listen, I’m about to judge you because you said that you didn’t sin.
\q1
\v 36 Why are you constantly changing your alliances?
\q1 You’ll be disappointed by Egypt just like you were disappointed by Assyria.
\q1
\v 37 What’s more, you’ll be removed from this place will your hands on your head,
\q1 because Yahweh has rejected the ones that you put your confidence in
\q1 →and they won’t be of any help to you.
\c 3
\s1 Israel is unfaithful
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 1 Listen, if a man divorced his wife and she left \add and married\add* another man,
\q1 surely he wouldn’t move in with her again?
\q1 Wouldn’t the country end up totally immoral?
\q1 Now you’ve acted like a prostitute with many partners—
\q1 would you then return to me?
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 2 Look up at the barren hilltops and see.
\q1 Is there anywhere \add there\add* where you haven’t given yourself?
\q1 You sat by the paths \add waiting\add* for them like an Aravi \add waiting\add* in the wilderness,
\q1 →and \add so\add* you’ve polluted the nation by your \add acts of\add* prostitution and wickedness.
\q1
\v 3 That’s why the showers have been withheld
\q1 and the late rains never came,
\q1 yet you have the look of a prostitute who refuses to be ashamed.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Even now you say that I’m your father,
\q1 \add or that\add* I’m your close friend from since you were young.
\q1
\v 5 Will he be angry forever?
\q1 Or permanently keep watch?
\q1 Listen, you say that, but you do \add all\add* the wicked things that you can.
\s1 Judah is also unfaithful
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 6 At the time when Josiah was king \add of Judah\add*, Yahweh asked me, “Do you see what faithless Israel has done? She herself has walked up every high hill, and has been a prostitute underneath every green tree.
\v 7 However I thought that after she’d done all those things, she’d return to me, but she hasn’t, and her treacherous sister, Judah, saw her.
\v 8 So I saw that because of all those situations where faithless Israel had committed adultery, I had given her divorce notice to her and sent her away. But her sister Judah, acting treacherously, wasn’t worried and she went out and also prostituted herself.
\v 9 Because she took her prostitution lightly, the nation became immoral and committed adultery with \add standing\add* stones and trees.
\v 10 Then \add after\add* all that, that treacherous sister Judah didn’t return to me with sincerity, \add but\add* with deception. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 Hope for unfaithful Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh told me: Unfaithful Israel has \add actually\add* been more upright than treacherous Judah!
\v 12 Go up north and tell them:
\q1 Unfaithful Israel, Yahweh declares:
\q1 Return \add to me\add*—
\q1 I won’t continue to be angry at you, because I’m faithful.
\m That’s Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 13 However you must recognise your disobedience
\q1 because you’ve rebelled against your god, Yahweh,
\q1 and you’ve extended your connections to foreign \add gods\add* under every leafy tree,
\q1 and you haven’t listened to my voice.
\m That’s Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 14 Return \add you\add* unfaithful children, Yahweh declares,
\q1 because I’m your husband.
\q1 Then I’ll take you all—one from a city and a couple from a clan—and I’ll bring you to Zion,
\q1
\v 15 and I’ll give you shepherds who’ll be faithful to me,
\q1 and they will guide you with knowledge and insight.
\q1
\v 16 Then when you’re fruitful and multiply in the land, Yahweh says,
\q1 they’ll no longer talk about having the chest \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement.
\q1 They won’t call it to mind or remember it,
\q1 and they won’t miss it or want a new one made again.
\q1
\v 17 At that time, they’ll call Jerusalem Yahweh’s throne,
\q1 and they’ll be gathered there from all the nations \add to honour\add* Yahweh’s name,
\q1 and they won’t want to behave again according to the evil in their own stubborn minds.
\q1
\v 18 In those days, the people of Judah will join the people of Israel,
\q1 coming from the northern lands to the land that I gave your ancestors as an inheritance.
\s1 The sinning of Israel
\s4 Both kingdoms
\q1
\v 19 Then I thought to myself \add about\add* how I’d accept you to be my children,
\q1 and give you a desirable land—an inheritance that all nations consider very beautiful.
\q1 I decided that you’ll all call me ‘my father’,
\q1 →and then you all won’t turn away from me.
\q1
\v 20 However, like a woman who deals treacherously with her companion,
\q1 so too you’ve all dealt treacherously with me, \add you\add* Israelite people.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\b
\q1
\v 21 A sound of weeping is heard on the barren hilltops—
\q1 the Israelite people begging for mercy
\q1 because they’ve followed paths of disobedience
\q1 and they’ve forgotten their god, Yahweh.
\q1
\v 22 Come back, \add you\add* unfaithful children—I will cure your faithlessness.
\q1 \add Say,\add* look, we’re coming back to you because you’re our god, Yahweh.
\q1
\v 23 Deception definitely comes from the hills—a commotion of mountains.
\q1 Our god Yahweh is certainly Israel’s salvation.
\q1
\v 24 From our youth, that shameful \add idol worship\add* has consumed our ancestors’ \add hard\add* work—
\q1 their sheep and cattle, and their sons and daughters.
\q1
\v 25 Let us lie down in our shame, and let our humiliation cover us,
\q1 because we’ve disobeyed our god, Yahweh.
\q1 Our ancestors and \add then\add* we ourselves, haven’t listened to the voice of our god, Yahweh.
\c 4
\s1 A call to turn back
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Yahweh says:
\q1 If you’ll return, Israel,
\q1 \add yes,\add* if you’ll return to me
\q1 and remove those detestable idols away from me without wavering,
\q1
\v 2 and if you promise as Yahweh lives, in truth, justice, and righteousness,
\q1 →then the nations will bless themselves in him,
\q1 and it’s him that they’ll boast about.
\p
\v 3 Yes, tells the people from Jerusalem and \add across all\add* Judah:
\q1 Plough your unploughed ground, but don’t sow thornbushes.
\q1
\v 4 Circumcise yourselves to Yahweh by removing the foreskins from your hearts
\q1 \add you\add* people of Jerusalem and Judah,
\q1 →so that my rage doesn’t burst out and burn like fire,
\q1 because there wouldn’t be anyone to extinguish it due to the wickedness of your behaviour.
\s1 The coming punishment of Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 5 Announce in Jerusalem and declare in Judah to blow the horn in the land.
\q1 Call out loudly to gather together and enter the fortified cities.
\q1
\v 6 Lift up a banner towards Zion and get to safety.
\q1 Don’t stand still because I’m about to bring calamity from the north with horrific destruction.
\q1
\v 7 A lion has come up from its thicket,
\q1 and a destroyer of nations has set out.
\q1 He’s come out from his place to make your country into a wasteland.
\q1 Your cities will be reduced to ruins with no inhabitants.
\q1
\v 8 So put on \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth—mourn and wail
\q1 because Yahweh’s burning anger hasn’t turned away from us.
\p
\v 9 Yahweh says that at that time,
\q1 the king and his officials will lose heart
\q1 and the priests and prophets will be astonished.
\p
\v 10 Then I replied, “Oh now! My master Yahweh,
\q1 you’ve certainly completely deceived Jerusalem and \add also\add* this people,
\q1 saying, ‘It will be well for you all.’ Yet the sword will come for our throats.”
\p
\v 11 When that happens, Jerusalem and this people will be told:
\q1 A scorching wind will come from the bare hills in the wilderness.
\q1 It won’t be coming \add just\add* to winnow the grain and blow away the chaff—
\q1
\v 12 it’ll be a strong blast from me.
\q1 Then I’ll speak judgements \add against\add* them.
\q1
\v 13 Listen, he goes up like the clouds,
\q1 and his chariots are like the storm wind.
\q1 His horses are faster than eagles.
\q1 →We won’t end well because we’ll be devastated.
\q1
\v 14 Jerusalem, wash the evil from your heart so that you can be saved.
\q1 How much longer will you hold onto your wicked thoughts for,
\q1
\v 15 because a voice from Dan declares trouble
\q1 and disaster is declared from Ephraim’s hill?
\q1
\v 16 Remind the nations—
\q1 listen, announce to Jerusalem:
\q1 They’re coming from a distant land to lay siege \add against you\add*,
\q1 and they raise their voice against Judah’s cities.
\q1
\v 17 They’ll surround Jerusalem like the watchmen around a field because it rebelled against me.
\m That’s Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 18 Your conduct and your actions have brought that to you, \add Jerusalem\add*.
\q1 That’s your punishment—it’s bitter and it’ll pierce to your heart.
\s1 Jeremiah’s sorrow for the country
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 19 My inward parts, my insides!
\q1 I writhe with chest pains.
\q1 My heart is pounding—I won’t stay silent,
\q1 →because my soul, you hear the sound of the trumpet—an alarm for battle.
\q1
\v 20 Destruction \add followed by more\add* destruction has been declared,
\q1 because all the land has been devastated.
\q1 Suddenly my tents are devastated—
\q1 my tent fabrics \add gone\add* in a moment.
\q1
\v 21 How long do I have to watch \add the enemy’s\add* banner for?
\q1 How long will that trumpet sound go on for?
\q1
\v 22 Yes, my people are foolish—they don’t know me.
\q1 They’re like stupid children who don’t understand anything.
\q1 They’ve very clever at \add doing what’s\add* evil
\q1 and they don’t know how to do what’s good.
\s1 Jeremiah’s vision about destruction
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 23 I saw the land, and wow, it was formless and empty,
\q1 and the skies didn’t have any light.
\q1
\v 24 I saw the mountains, and wow, they were quaking,
\q1 and all the hills shook back-and-forth.
\q1
\v 25 I looked, and wow, there were no more people,
\q1 and all the birds had flown away.
\q1
\v 26 I saw, and wow, the orchard had turned into a wilderness,
\q1 and all the cities had been pulled down out of Yahweh’s sight
\q1 due to his burning anger,
\p
\v 27 because Yahweh says this:
\q1 The entire country will become desolate,
\q1 ^but I won’t completely destroy it.
\q1
\v 28 The land will mourn after that,
\q1 and the skies will darken from above,
\q1 because I’ve revealed my resolution,
\q1 ^and I won’t change my mind or turn back from it.
\q1
\v 29 The city people will flee from the sound of \add approaching\add* horsemen and archers.
\q1 They’ll head into the thickets and up onto the cliffs.
\q1 →Every city will be abandoned without anyone living in them.
\q1
\v 30 So you,\f + \fr 4:30 \ft The Hebrew has a female \fq you \ft pronoun (as used for the city), which doesn’t match the male form of \fq devastated\ft . Many modern versions have \fqa What will you do, devastated one? \ft despite the gender mismatch.\f* what will you do \add for\add* the devastated one?
\q1 You dress in scarlet, adorning yourself with gold jewelry.
\q1 You make your eyes look bigger with makeup, but it won’t help—
\q1 your lovers reject you \add and\add* they’re coming for your life.
\q1
\v 31 Yes, I hear a sound like a woman in labour—
\q1 distress like a woman giving birth to her first child—
\q1 the sound of Zion’s daughter \add as\add* she gasps for breath.
\q1 She spreads out her hands \add saying\add*,
\q1 ‘I fear for my life because of those murderers.’
\c 5
\s1 Jerusalem’s corruption
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 1 Roam around Jerusalem’s streets and look and observe,
\q1 and search around its plazas.
\q1 If you can find one person there who values justice and strives to be faithful,
\q1 →then I’ll forgive the city.
\q1
\v 2 Even when they make an oath in Yahweh’s name,
\q1 it’ll \add still\add* be a lie.
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh, don’t you look for honesty?
\q1 You struck them but they don’t even react.
\q1 You brought them to an end, \add but\add* they refuse to accept correction.
\q1 They make their faces harder than rock \add and\add* refuse to change.
\q1
\v 4 Then I thought that \add maybe\add* they’re just poor people?
\q1 They act foolishly because they’re not educated about Yahweh’s way
\q2 or what their god requires of them?
\q1
\v 5 So I’ll go to the leaders and speak to them,
\q1 because they know Yahweh’s way and what their god requires.
\q1 ^However, all of them have broken out from under his yoke,
\q2 and torn off the chains.
\q1
\v 6 Because of that, a lion from a thicket will attack them.
\q1 A wolf from the wilderness will devastate them.
\q1 A leopard lies in wait outside their cities—
\q1 →everyone who goes out from them will be torn to pieces
\q1 because their disobedience is extensive
\q1 and their disloyalty is substantial.
\q1
\v 7 How could I forgive you for that?
\q1 Your children have abandoned me and have made oaths by \add things that are\add* not gods.
\q1 I satisfied them, but they committed adultery
\q1 and \add ritually\add* cut themselves\f + \fr 5:7 \ft Many translations follow the Septuagint here rather than the Hebrew, and end up with something like \fqa gathered together\ft . (Of course, \fq the prostitute \ft here refers to idol worship.)\f* at the prostitute’s house.
\q1
\v 8 They were well-fed, lusty horses,
\q1 each man neighing at his neighbour’s wife.
\p
\v 9 Yahweh asks:
\q1 Shouldn’t I punish them for these things?
\q1 Shouldn’t I avenge myself on a nation like that?
\q1
\v 10 Go along the rows in the vineyard and destroy them,
\q1 but don’t completely destroy them—
\q1 break off their shoots because they don’t belong to Yahweh.
\q1
\v 11 Thebes, Israel’s and Judah’s people had indeed dealt treacherously with me.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 Yahweh rejects Israel
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 12 They’ve denied Yahweh and said:
\q1 He doesn’t exist and nothing bad will happen us,
\q1 and we’ll never experience war or famine.
\q1
\v 13 The prophets are full of air and don’t have any message.
\q1 →So let what they say, happen to them.
\p
\v 14 So Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 Because you’ve spoken those words,
\q1 I’ll put my messages in your mouth as a fire
\q1 and this people \add group\add* will be the wood that it consumes.
\q1
\v 15 Listen you Israelites to Yahweh’s declaration:
\q1 I’m about to bring to you all a nation from far away—
\q1 it’s an enduring nation that’s been around for a long time.
\q1 You won’t know their language or understand what they say.
\q1
\v 16 Its quiver is like an open tomb.
\q1 All of them are warriors.
\q1
\v 17 It will consume your grain and your food.
\q1 They’ll devour your sons and daughters, and your flocks and your cattle.
\q1 They’ll strip your grapevines and your fig trees.
\q1 With their swords, they’ll shatter your fortified cities that you were trusting in.
\p
\v 18 But Yahweh says:
\q1 Even in those days, I won’t completely destroy you all.
\q1
\v 19 When the people ask why Yahweh’s doing that, you’ll tell them:
\q1 You rejected me and served foreign gods in your own country,
\q1 →so you’ll all serve strangers in a land that doesn’t belong to you.
\s1 Yahweh warns his people
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 20 Declare this to Jacob’s descendants,
\q1 and declare it in Judah:
\q1
\v 21 Please listen to this, \add you\add* foolish, heartless people.
\q1 They have eyes but do not see,
\q1 and they have ears, but do not hear.
\q1
\v 22 Yahweh declares:
\q1 Should you all respect and strive to obey me,
\q1 and shouldn’t you tremble in front of me?
\q1 It was me who designated the sand as the boundary for the ocean—
\q1 it’s a permanent \add line\add* and the sea doesn’t go past it.
\q1 The waters surge but get no further,
\q1 and the waves roar, but can’t get past it.
\q1
\v 23 But this group of people has a stubborn and rebellious heart.
\q1 They’ve turned aside and gone away.
\q1
\v 24 They don’t say to themselves:
\q1 Please, let’s strive to obey our god Yahweh—
\q1 the one who gives the autumn and spring rains at the appropriate time.
\q1 He decides on the harvest weeks for us.
\q1
\v 25 Your disobedience has diverted those things,
\q1 and your sins have withheld goodness from you all
\q1
\v 26 because there are wicked influencers among my people.
\q1 They lie in wait like a bird-catcher.
\q1 They set a trap \add and\add* catch people.
\q1
\v 27 Their homes are full of deceit like a cage full of birds.
\q1 →so that’s how they’ve become rich and powerful.
\q1
\v 28 They’ve become fat and slick.
\q1 They also disobey by doing evil.
\q1 They don’t plead the cause of \add those who are\add* fatherless,
\q1 and they’re successful, yet they haven’t insisted on justice for the poor.
\p
\v 29 Yahweh says:
\q1 Why shouldn’t I punish them for all that behaviour?
\q1 Why shouldn’t I avenge myself on a nation like this one?
\q1
\v 30 Something horrible and shocking has happened in the country:
\q1
\v 31 The prophets prophesy with lies,
\q1 and the priests rule by their own authority,
\q1 and my people love it this way,
\q1 but what will you all do \add when it comes to its\add* end?
\c 6
\s1 Jerusalem’s last warning
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Get out of Jerusalem \add and get\add* yourselves to safety, \add you\add* people of Benjamin,
\q1 and blow a trumpet in Tekoa.
\q1 Send up a signal from Beyt-Hakerem
\q1 because disaster and terrible destruction looms from the north.
\q1
\v 2 Although it’s beautiful and delicate,
\q1 I will destroy Zion’s daughter.
\q1
\v 3 Shepherds and their flocks will go and pitch their tents all around it.\f + \fr 6:3 \ft Many English translations interpret these ‘shepherds’ with their ‘flocks’ to be leaders of foreign nations with their armies implementing the destruction mentioned in the previous verse, i.e., the ones battling in the next verse. However, we’ve chosen to interpret the flocks grazing the city surrounds as the follow-on \+em after\+em* the destruction, before the next verse goes \+em back in time\+em* to give more details of how the destruction will occur.\f*
\q1 Each one will graze their own area.
\p
\v 4 Prepare for battle.
\q1 Take action and let’s attack at midday.
\q1 It won’t be good for us if the day is ending
\q1 because the evening shadows get longer.
\q1
\v 5 Take action and attack at night
\q1 and destroy its palaces.
\q1
\v 6 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says:
\q1 Cut down \add trees for\add* timber
\q1 and mound up a ramp against Jerusalem.
\q1 →That city must be punished as it’s full of oppression.
\q1
\v 7 Just as a well keeps its water fresh,
\q1 so too she keeps her evil fresh.
\q1 Violence and oppression can be heard there.
\q1 I always see sickness and deadly diseases.
\q1
\v 8 Take note of my warning Jerusalem
\q1 in case I turn away from you—
\q1 in case I make you desolate—
\q1 a land that’s not inhabited.
\s1 Israel’s disobedience
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 9 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 Those who remain in Israel
\q1 \add will be picked over\add* like gleaning \add the last few grapes off\add* a vine.
\q1 Return your hand over the branches like a grape-picker.
\q1
\v 10 Who should I speak to and warn so they can list?
\q1 Wow, their ears are so closed that they can’t pay attention.
\q1 Wow, Yahweh’s message has become offensive to them—
\q1 they don’t enjoy \add hearing\add* it.
\q1
\v 11 Now I’m filled with Yahweh’s rage.
\q1 I’m tired of holding it in.
\p Pour it out on the children in the street
\q1 and on the young men who gather together,
\q1 because a husband will be captured with his wife—
\q1 the very elderly as well.
\q1
\v 12 Then their houses will be turned over to others,
\q1 along with their fields and wives
\q1 because I’ll stretch out my hand
\q1 against the land’s inhabitants.
\m That’s Yahweh’s declaration
\q1
\v 13 because from the smallest to the greatest,
\q1 they’re all greedy for dishonest gain.
\q1 From a prophet to the priest, everyone deals in lies.
\q1
\v 14 They’ve lightly bandaged my people’s wound
\q1 by saying, ‘Peace, peace,’ but there wasn’t \add any\add* peace.
\q1
\v 15 Were they ashamed when they did something disgusting?
\q1 Thebes, they weren’t ashamed—
\q1 they didn’t even know how to be humiliated
\q1 →so therefore they’ll fall among the fallen.
\q1 At the time when I punish them, they’ll stumble.
\m \add That’s what\add* Yahweh says.
\s1 Israel rejects Yahweh’s messages
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 16 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Stand at the \add cross\add*roads and look,
\q1 and ask about the ancient paths.
\q1 ‘Where is the path of the good?’ Then walk along it
\q1 and find rest for your spirit.
\p But they say, ‘We won’t walk \add on it\add*.’
\p
\v 17 I set watchmen over you.
\q1 Pay attention to the sound of the trumpet,
\q1 But they said, ‘We won’t pay attention.’
\p
\v 18 Therefore listen \add you\add* nations,
\q1 and \add you\add* congregation, know what will happen to them.
\q1
\v 19 Listen, \add you\add* earth.
\q1 Yes, I will bring disaster to this people—
\q1 the fruit of their own plans
\q1 because they paid no attention to my messages
\q1 and they rejected my instructions.
\q1
\v 20 What difference does it make to me if they burn frankincense that came from Sheba?
\q1 Or \add if they have\add* good cane from a distant land?
\q1 Your burnt offerings aren’t acceptable,
\q1 and your sacrifices don’t please me.
\q1
\v 21 So Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, I’ll give this \add group of\add* people a stumbling block,
\q1 and they’ll trip over it—
\q1 fathers and sons together,
\q1 \add both\add* a neighbour and his friend.
\s1 The coming, northern army
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 22 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, an army is about to come from a northern country,
\q1 and a powerful nation will be roused from the remotest parts of the world.
\q1
\v 23 They carry bows and spears.
\q1 It’s cruel and they have no mercy.
\q1 They make a noise like the sea roaring
\q1 and they ride in battle formation on horses
\q1 against you, Zion’s daughter.
\p
\v 24 We felt weak when we heard the reports about them.
\q1 Distress took hold of us and we’re in agony like a woman about to give birth.
\q1
\v 25 Don’t go out to the countryside,
\q1 and don’t walk on the road
\q1 because of the enemy’s sword.
\q1 There’s terror all around.
\p
\v 26 My people’s daughter, dress yourself in \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth
\q1 and roll in ashes.
\q1 Go into mourning as if you’d lost an only child—
\q1 \add a time\add* of bitter wailing
\q1 because the destroyer will suddenly come \add against\add* us.
\q1
\v 27 I’ve appointed you to examine my people like ore,
\q1 →so you can observe their conduct, and test \add them\add*.
\q1
\v 28 They’re all stubborn rebels who go around slandering others.
\q1 They’re all \add like\add* bronze and iron, behaving corruptly.
\q1
\v 29 The bellows fan the fire
\q1 ^\add but after\add* the thorough refining, the lead remains.
\q1 The evil people haven’t been separated out.
\q1
\v 30 They’ll be called ‘rejected silver’
\q1 because Yahweh has rejected them.
\c 7
\s1 Jeremiah’s preaching in the temple
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Yahweh’s message came to Jeremiah:
\q1
\v 2 Stand at the gate at Yahweh’s temple and declare this message, saying:
\q1 Listen to Yahweh’s message,
\q1 all you \add people of\add* Judah who enter these gates to worship Yahweh.
\q1
\v 3 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says that
\q1 you all must improve your ways,
\q1 and make them right
\q1 →so that I’ll allow you all to live in this land.
\q1
\v 4 Don’t trust in deceptive words that say,
\q1 ‘Yahweh’s temple \add is here\add*.’
\q1 ‘Yahweh’s temple, Yahweh’s temple.’
\p
\v 5 Yes, if you all really make your behaviour good,
\q1 and everyone really acts fairly towards their neighbours,
\q1
\v 6 \add and\add* you all don’t oppress strangers, orphans, or widows,
\q1 and don’t shed innocent blood in this place
\q1 or follow after other gods that will harm you,
\q1
\v 7 then I’ll allow you all to live in this place in the land that I gave to your ancestors from ancient times and for the future to come.
\p
\v 8 Listen, you’re all relying on deceptive messages which can’t profit.
\v 9 Will you all \add keep\add* stealing and murdering, committing adultery and telling lies in court, offering incense to Baal and following other gods that you didn’t previously know.
\v 10 Then you all come \add here\add* and stand in front of this temple that’s associated with my name, and say that you’re saved while you all \add continue to\add* do all those disgusting things.
\v 11 This house which bears my name has become a robbers’ den in your eyes. Listen, I’ve also seen it. That is Yahweh’s utterance.
\v 12 But please go to my place in Shiloh where I had previously \add had my tent and\add* my name resided, and see what I did to \add destroy\add* that place due to the wickedness of my people, Israel.
\v 13 So now Yahweh declares that because you’ve all been doing those things, and I’ve spoken to you all over and over again but you wouldn’t listen, and I called you all but you wouldn’t answer,
\v 14 then I’ll do what I did to Shiloh, to this house that’s called by my name—the temple that you’ve all trusted in, and the place that I gave to you and your ancestors.
\v 15 I’ll throw you all away where I can’t see you just as I threw out all your relatives, all of Israel’s descendants, \add from the northern kingdom\add*.
\s1 Judah’s consistent disobedience
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 16 And you, \add Jeremiah\add*, don’t pray on behalf of this \add group of\add* people, and don’t cry out for help, or pray for them, or petition me about them, because I won’t listen to you.
\v 17 Don’t you see what they do in Judah’s cities and in Jerusalem’s streets?
\v 18 The sons are gathering firewood, and the fathers are kindling the fire, and the women are kneading dough to make cakes for ‘the queen of the heavens’ and pouring out drink offerings for other gods so as to provoke me to anger.
\v 19 Yahweh declares that it’s not me they’re provoking, but rather it’s themselves that they’re hurting to their own shame.
\v 20 So my master Yahweh, says this: “Listen, my anger and rage will gush out onto this place—onto people and animals, and trees in the countryside and crops in the ground. It will burn and not be extinguished.”
\p
\v 21 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says: “Instead of \add wasting time\add* burning up your sacrifices, \add just\add* eat the meat \add yourselves\add*,
\v 22 because on the day when I brought your ancestors out from Egypt, I didn’t \add immediately\add* give them orders about burnt offerings and sacrifices.
\v 23 What I told them was, ‘Listen to my voice, and I’ll be your god, and you all will be my people. Follow all the instructions that I’ll give you so that everything will go well for you all.’
\v 24 But they didn’t turn their heads towards him and listen, and they enacted schemes from the stubbornness of their own evil hearts—going backwards instead of forwards
\v 25 from the day that their ancestors left Egypt even until today. Then I sent my servants the prophets to you all—rising early each day and sending them.
\v 26 However, they didn’t listen to me, and they stiffened their necks and turned their heads—doing even more evil than their ancestors.
\p
\v 27 So pass all these messages on to them, even though they won’t listen to you.
\q1 You’ll call to them, but they won’t answer you.
\v 28 Tell them that this is a nation that doesn’t listen to the voice of its god Yahweh, and doesn’t accept discipline.
\s1 ‘The Slaughter Valley’
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 29 Shave your head \add in mourning\add* and throw the hair away.
\q1 Sing a mourning song on the barren heights
\q1 because Yahweh has rejected and abandoned the generation who made him angry.
\p
\v 30 Yes, Yahweh says that Judah’s people have done what I consider evil—putting disgusting things in the temple that’s associated with my name, and so have defiled it.
\v 31 They’ve built hilltop shrines to Tofet in Hinom valley \add outside Jerusalem\add* to burn their sons and daughters in fire—something I’ve never commanded or even considered.
\v 32 So listen, Yahweh says, that days are coming because of that that, when it’ll no longer be called ‘Tofet’ or ‘Hinom valley’, but rather, ‘The Slaughter Valley’, because so many people will be buried in Tofet that they’ll run out of space.
\v 33 Then the people’s corpses will become food for the vultures and the wild animals, and there’ll be no one \add left\add* to frighten them \add away\add*.
\v 34 Then in Judah’s cities and Jerusalem’s streets, I’ll halt the sounds of joy and gladness, even wedding celebrations, because the region will become a wasteland.
\c 8
\p
\v 1 Yahweh declares that they will bring the bones of Judah’s kings out from their graves at that time, as well as the bones of their leaders, priests, and prophets, along with the bones of Jerusalem’s inhabitants.
\v 2 Then they’ll spread them out under the sun, moon, and stars—the heavenly bodies that they loved and served, and that they followed after and searched for, and that they bowed down to. Those bones won’t be collected up, and they won’t be buried again. They’ll be like dung across the surface of the land.
\v 3 The remnant of that evil group of people in all those places where I banished them, will choose death rather than life. That is Commander-in-chief Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 Sin and punishment
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 4 Then Yahweh says to tell them:
\q1 Don’t people get back up when they fall down?
\q1 Don’t they come back after they’ve turned the wrong way?
\q1
\v 5 \add So\add* Why do these people from Jerusalem turn and away, then continue to be unfaithful?
\q1 They keep hold of deceitfulness and refuse to come back.
\q1
\v 6 I’ve listened carefully but they don’t say the right things.
\q1 None of them wants to give up their wickedness.
\q1 ^All of them carry on their own ways, like a horse rushing \add forward\add* in battle.
\q1
\v 7 Even the stork as it flies, knows the seasons.
\q1 The dove, swallow, and thrush stick to their migration times,
\q1 ^yet my people do know what Yahweh expects of them.
\q1
\v 8 How can you all say that you’re wise and that you have Yahwehs instructions?
\q1 Honestly, the quills of deceitful scribes have made it into a lie.
\q1
\v 9 The wise will be put to shame—
\q1 they will be dismayed and trapped.
\q1 Yes, they’ve rejected Yahweh’s message.
\q1 What wisdom do they really have?
\q1
\v 10 Because of that, I’ll give their wives to others,
\q1 and their fields to those who dispossess them,
\q1 because they’re all prospering by taking advantage of others—
\q1 prophets and priests are all behaving deceptively.
\q1
\v 11 They lightly treated the fracture of my people’s daughter,
\q1 saying, ‘Peace, peace,’ when there’s no peace.
\q1
\v 12 Were they ashamed when they did something disgusting?
\q1 Even when confronted, they weren’t ashamed—
\q1 they didn’t know how to be humiliated
\q1 →so they’ll fall like the others.
\q1 They’ll stumble at the time of their punishment says Yahweh.
\p
\v 13 I’ll bring their harvests to an end, says Yahweh.
\q1 There won’t be any grapes on their vines, or figs on their trees.
\q1 Even the leaves will wither,
\q1 and what I gave them, will rot away.
\p
\v 14 Why are we just sitting here?
\q1 →Let’s go into the fortified cities together so we can die there,
\q1 because our god Yahweh is going to cause us to perish,
\q1 and he’ll make us drink poisoned water because we’ve sinned against him.
\q1
\v 15 We had hoped for peace, but nothing good \add came\add*.
\q1 We hoped for a time of healing, but wow, got terror.
\q1
\v 16 The snorting of his stallions could be heard from Dan.
\q1 The whole earth shakes from the sound of the neighing of his powerful horses.
\q1 They are coming to destroy the land and everything in it,
\q1 \add and\add* the city and everyone living in it.
\p
\v 17 Yes, listen, I’ll send snakes\f + \fr 8:17 \ft This could likely be referring to enemy warriors, not literal snakes.\f* among you all—
\q1 vipers that can’t be charmed,
\q1 →and they’ll bite you all, says Yahweh.
\s1 Jeremiah’s sorrow about Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 18 My sorrow is beyond comfort.
\q1 My heart is faint within me.
\q1
\v 19 Listen, it’s the sound of a cry for help from my people’s daughter from a far away land.
\q1 Isn’t Yahweh in Zion?
\q1 Is its king no longer there?
\q1 Why have they provoked me to anger with their idols and with useless foreign gods?
\q1
\v 20 The harvest has passed, summer has ended,
\q1 and we haven’t been rescued.
\b
\q1
\v 21 I’m broken because of the brokenness of my people’s daughter.
\q1 I’m in mourning \add and\add* overwhelmed by depression.
\q1
\v 22 Is there no medicine in Gilead?
\q1 Isn’t there a doctor there?
\q1 Why isn’t my people’s daughter getting healed?
\c 9
\q1
\v 1 If only my head was water, and my eyes were a fountain of tears,
\q1 →then I’d weep day and night for my people’s slain daughter.
\q1
\v 2 Who will give me a lodging place for travellers in the desert?
\q1 →Then I would leave my people and walk away from them,
\q1 because all of them are adulterers—
\q1 a treacherous group of people.
\q1
\v 3 They bend their tongues \add like\add* bows—
\q1 \add chasing\add* lies rather than faithfulness.
\q1 They grow strong in the land
\q1 because they go from evil to evil and they don’t know me.
\m That’s Yahweh’s declaration.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Let each person beware of his neighbour,
\q1 and don’t rely on any relative,
\q1 because brothers consistently betray,
\q1 and neighbours go around slandering.
\q1
\v 5 Every one will deceive their neighbour,
\q1 and they won’t tell the truth—
\q1 they teach their tongues to tell lies.
\q1 →They wear themselves out being disobedient.
\q1
\v 6 Your residence is in the middle of deceit—
\q1 \add because of\add* deceit they refuse to know me.
\m That’s Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 7 Because of that, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says,
\q1 I’m about to refine them and test them,
\q1 because how \add else\add* should I act towards my people’s daughter.
\q1
\v 8 Their tongue is a deadly arrow—
\q1 it speaks out deceit.
\q1 With his mouth, a man speaks peace with his neighbours,
\q1 while in his mind, he’s planning his ambush.
\q1
\v 9 Shouldn’t I punish them for those things? Yahweh says.
\q1 Shouldn’t I avenge myself against a nation like that?
\b
\q1
\v 10 On the mountains I’ll take up weeping and wailing,
\q1 and a funeral song out in the wilderness pastures,
\q1 because they’re left desolate as no one passes through \add there\add*.
\q1 They don’t hear the sound of cattle or birds or \add wild\add* animals—
\q1 they’ve fled \add and\add* gone away.
\b
\q1
\v 11 Then I’ll make Jerusalem into piles of ruins—
\q1 a place for jackals to live in—
\q1 and I’ll make Judah’s cities into uninhabited wastelands.
\p
\v 12 Who is the person that’s wise and understands this?
\q1 Who has Yahweh’s mouth spoken to so that they can declare it—
\q1 concerning why the nation has perished and been destroyed
\q1 like the wilderness with no one passing through it?
\p
\v 13 Then Yahweh said, “Because they abandoned my instructions that I gave to them,
\q1 and they don’t listen to my voice or follow what say,
\q1
\v 14 and they’re guided by their own stubborn minds,
\q1 and follow after the Baal that their ancestors taught them about,
\q1
\v 15 then Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, I’m about to feed them bitter food
\q1 and give them poison water to drink.
\q1
\v 16 Then I’ll scatter them among nations that neither they nor their ancestors knew anything about,
\q1 and I’ll send an army after them until I’ve finished them off.
\s1 The requesting of people of Jerusalem
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 17 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 Consider that and summon funeral wailers to come—
\q1 get the most skillful women to come.
\p
\v 18 Let them hurry and start wailing about us,
\q1 →so our eyes have tears running down them,
\q1 and our eyelids flow with water,
\p
\v 19 because the sound of mourning can be heard from Zion:
\q1 ‘We’ve been so devastated.
\q1 We’re terribly ashamed, because we’ve left the land
\q1 because they tore down our houses.’
\p
\v 20 So now hear Yahweh’s message, \add you\add* women.
\q1 Let your ear receive the message from his mouth,
\q1 and teach your daughters a mourning song,
\q1 and each woman teach her neighbour a funeral song,
\q1
\v 21 because death has come up into our windows.
\q1 It enters into our fortresses,
\q1 cutting off children from the street
\q1 and young men from the open plazas.
\q1
\v 22 Yahweh says to pass this on:
\q1 Human corpses will fall like dung across the ground’s surface
\q1 and like fallen grain after the harvesters \add have finished\add*,
\q1 and there’s no one to collect them.
\p
\v 23 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Don’t let the wise person boast about their wisdom,
\q1 and don’t let the warrior boast about his strength.
\q1 Don’t let the rich person boast about their riches,
\q1
\v 24 because if they want to boast, let them boast about this:
\q1 Understanding and knowing me because I’m Yahweh,
\q1 demonstrating loyal commitment, justice, and righteousness on the earth
\q1 because I take pleasure in those things.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 25 Listen, Yahweh declares that there’s a time coming
\q1 when I will punish all those who’ve been physically circumcised:
\q1
\v 26 the Egyptians, Jews, Edomites, Ammonites, and Moabites,
\q1 and all those who trim the corners \add of their beards\add*, those living in the wilderness,
\q1 because all the uncircumcised nations and all the Israelites have uncircumcised hearts.
\c 10
\s1 Idolatry versus true worship
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Listen to the message that Yahweh is telling you all, \add you\add* Israelites.
\v 2 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 Don’t imitate the ways of \add other\add* nations,
\q1 and don’t be dismayed by signs in the skies,
\q1 \add just\add* because \add those other\add* nations are,
\q1
\v 3 because the customs of \add those groups of\add* people are worthless.
\q1 Yes, they cut down a tree from a forest,
\q1 \add and\add* a craftsman does his handywork with his tools,
\q1
\v 4 then they overlay it with gold and silver.
\q1 They use hammer and nails to securely fasten it
\q1 so that it won’t topple over.
\q1
\v 5 Those idols are like a scarecrow in a cucumber field,
\q1 not being able to speak.
\q1 \add What’s more,\add* they have to be carried because they can’t walk.
\q1 Don’t be afraid of them because they can’t harm anyone,
\q1 but at the same time, there’s no good in them \add either\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 6 There’s no one like you, Yahweh.
\q1 You’re powerful and have a reputation for your strength.
\q1
\v 7 Who wouldn’t respect and obey you, king of the nations
\q1 because that’s what you deserve?
\q1 Yes, among all the wise people of the word,
\q1 and among all their kingdoms,
\q1 there’s no one like you.
\q1
\v 8 Those ‘wise’ people are \add both\add* stupid and foolish all at once—
\q1 instructed by an idol that’s \add just a piece of\add* wood.
\q1
\v 9 They brought hammered silver from Tarshish and gold from Ufaz—
\q1 the work of a craftsman and from the hands of the refiners.
\q1 \add Then they put\add* violet and purple clothing \add on them\add*—
\q1 all the work of skillful people.
\q1
\v 10 However, Yahweh is the true god.
\q1 He is the living god and permanent king.
\q1 \add When\add* he’s angry, the earth shakes,
\q1 and the nations can’t endure his indignation.
\p
\v 11 You need to tell them this:
\q1 The gods that didn’t make the heavens and earth
\q1 will perish from the earth and from under these skies.
\s1 Praising Yahweh
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 12 Yahweh made the earth using his power.
\q1 He established the world by his wisdom,
\q1 and with his understanding, he stretched out the skies.
\q1
\v 13 When he projects his voice, there’s a rush of waters in the skies,
\q1 and he brings clouds up from the ends of the earth.
\q1 He made lightning for the rain,
\q1 and sends out wind from his storehouse.
\q1
\v 14 Every person has become stupid from knowledge.
\q1 Every metalworker is put to shame by idols
\q1 because his molten image is a deception and there’s no life in them.
\q1
\v 15 They’re worthless—a work of mockery.
\q1 They’ll perish at the time for their punishment.
\q1
\v 16 Jacob’s inheritance isn’t like that
\q1 because he’s the one who made everything,
\q1 and the Israelites are his inheritance.
\q1 His name is Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\s1 Coming destruction for Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 17 Gather your stuff and leave the land,
\q1 you who are living under siege
\q1
\v 18 because Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, I’m flinging out the inhabitants of the land at this time,
\q1 and I’ll cause them distress so that they can find me.
\p
\v 19 Oh dear, poor me. My fracture, my wound is severe,
\q1 but I said that this is a sickness and I will endure it.
\q1
\v 20 My tent has been devastated, and all of my tent ropes have been snapped.
\q1 My sons have gone off and left me.
\q1 There’s no one to pitch my tent again,
\q1 or to hang up my tent curtains.
\b
\q1
\v 21 Yes, the shepherds are stupid and haven’t asked Yahweh for direction
\q1 →so therefore they haven’t prospered, and all their flock has been scattered.
\q1
\v 22 Listen, it sounds like news has arrived
\q1 plus there’s a loud rumble from a land in the north
\q1 coming to make Judah’s cities into deserted places for jackals to live in.
\b
\q1
\v 23 Yahweh, I know that a person’s \add life\add* pathway isn’t their own—
\q1 no one who walks, directs their own steps.
\q1
\v 24 Discipline me, Yahweh, only in justice and not in anger
\q1 in case you reduce me to nothing.
\q1
\v 25 Pour your fury out on the nations that don’t know you.
\q1 and on the families that don’t call out to you to help them,
\q1 because they’ve consumed Jacob.
\q1 Yes, they’ve completely destroyed him and ruined his farmland.
\c 11
\s1 The broken agreement
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 1 Yahweh’s message came to Jeremiah to say:
\v 2 Listen to the words of this agreement and tell them to everyone in Judah, including those who live in Jerusalem
\v 3 and tell them, “Israel’s god Yahweh says that anyone who won’t listen to the words of this agreement will be cursed.
\v 4 \add It’s the same agreement\add* that I commanded for your ancestors at the time when I brought them out of the iron furnace \add that was\add* Egypt, telling them: ‘Listen to my voice and follow the instructions that I’ve given you all, and then you’ll be my people, and I’ll become your god
\v 5 in order to fulfill the promise that I made to your ancestors to give them a very fertile land as it is right now.’ ”
\p “So be it, Yahweh,” I answered.
\p
\v 6 Then Yahweh told me, “Declare all these things in Judah’s cities, including in Jerusalem’s streets. Tell them to listen to the words of this agreement and carry them out
\v 7 because I solemnly warned your ancestors at the time when I brought them out of Egypt until today, repeatedly warning them to listen to my voice.
\v 8 However, they didn’t listen or even pay any attention to me, and each one of them stubbornly followed their own evil desires, so I ordered all the consequences from not obeying the agreement, to come to them.”
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh told me, “A conspiracy has been found across Judah, and including Jerusalem’s inhabitants.
\v 10 They’ve returned to the evil ways of their ancestors from the past who refused to listen to my messages, and then they followed after other gods to serve them. The people of \add both\add* Israel and Judah broke the agreement that I made with the ancestors.
\v 11 So now, listen: Yahweh says that I’m about to bring calamity to them which they won’t be able to avoid, and they’ll cry out to me \add for help\add* but I won’t listen to them.
\v 12 Then Judah’s cities, including those living in Jerusalem, will go and cry out to the gods for help that they’ve been burning incense to, but those gods won’t be able to rescue them from their calamity
\v 13 because there are now as many gods in Judah as you all have cities, and as many incense altars for Baal in Jerusalem as you all have streets.
\v 14 Jeremiah, don’t pray for those people, and don’t plead with me to rescue them, because I won’t be listening when they call out to me \add for help\add* during their difficulties.
\p
\v 15 “My dear \add Judah\add*, what \add right do you have\add* to be in my temple after doing so much evil?
\q1 You offer holy sacrifices then go and happily do wicked things.
\q1
\v 16 Yahweh called you a luxuriant olive tree with beautiful, good-looking fruit.
\q1 ^\add Now\add* he’ll kindle a fire with a loud roar on its branches that have gone bad.
\p
\v 17 Commander-in-chief Yahweh who planted you, has decided that you’ll experience evil because of the evil done by the peoples of Israel and Judah when they’ve provoked me to anger by offering incense to Baal.
\s1 A plot against Jeremiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 18 Then Yahweh revealed something to me which I understood once he showed me their preparations.
\v 19 I was like a tame lamb being led to be slaughtered, and I didn’t know that they had made plans against me, \add saying,\add* ‘Let’s destroy the tree with its fruit, and cut him off from the land of the living. Then his name won’t be remembered any longer.’
\p
\v 20 \add Then I prayed,\add* “Commander-in-chief Yahweh who \add always\add* judges fairly, examining our inner thoughts and motives, let me see your vengeance on them, because I’ve presented my case to you.”
\p
\v 21 So Yahweh said it was \add some\add* men of Anatot\f + \fr 11:21 \ft Jeremiah’s home town—see 1:1.\f* who were wanting to kill me, saying that I mustn’t prophesy in Yahweh’s name or else they’d make sure that I die.
\v 22 Therefore Commander-in-chief Yahweh says, “Listen, I’m about to punish them. Their young men will die by the sword, and their children will die from famine.
\v 23 There won’t be any of them left, because I will bring calamity to the people from Anatot at the time of their punishment.”
\c 12
\s1 Jeremiah queries Yahweh
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 1 Yahweh, you do what’s right when I bring you a case.
\q1 →Nevertheless, I \add want to\add* talk with you about justice:
\q1 Why do wicked people prosper?
\q1 Why are treacherous people able to relax?
\q1
\v 2 You planted them \add like trees\add* and they’ve taken root,
\q1 \add then\add* they’ve grown and produced fruit.
\q1 They’re full of talk about you,
\q1 but they’re really far from you.
\q1
\v 3 but Yahweh, you know me.
\q1 You see me, and test my heart toward you.
\q1 Drag them away like sheep \add doing\add* to slaughter,
\q1 and reserve a time for them to be slaughtered.
\q1
\v 4 How much longer will the land be dry for
\q1 with the plants in the countryside withering up?
\q1 Even the wild animals and birds have diminished in number,
\q1 because people have said that he won’t see what will happen to us.
\s2 Yahweh’s response to Jeremiah
\p
\v 5 If you’ve run with foot soldiers and they’ve worn you out,
\q1 how will you compete with horses?
\q1 If you’re falling to the ground in a safe region,
\q1 how will you do in the thickets by the Jordan \add river\add*?
\q1
\v 6 Even your brothers and other family members are working against you.
\q1 They speak out against you.
\q1 Don’t trust them when they say good things \add about you\add*.
\s1 Yahweh’s distress about his people
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 7 I’ve abandoned my country.
\q1 I’ve walked away from my inheritance.
\q1 I’ve given my dearly loved one into the hand of its enemies.
\q1
\v 8 My inheritance become like a lion in a forest for me.
\q1 That country always speaks against me so I hate it.
\q1
\v 9 My inheritance is like a speckled bird of prey to me,
\q1 with \add other\add* birds of prey all around it.
\q1 Go \add and\add* get all the wild animals from the countryside to come and eat.
\q1
\v 10 Many shepherds \add (foreign rulers)\add* have ruined my vineyard—
\q1 they’ve trampled my area.
\q1 They’ve made my desirable region into a desolate wasteland.
\q1
\v 11 They’ve made it into a wasteland—it’s mourning to me.
\q1 All the land has been made desolate because there’s no one who cares about it.
\q1
\v 12 Destroyers have come to all the bare hilltops in the wilderness,
\q1 because a sword is devouring for Yahweh, from each end of the land.
\q1 There’s no peace for any living thing.
\q1
\v 13 People have sown wheat and harvested thorns.
\q1 They’ve wearied themselves but not profited,
\q1 and they were ashamed by your low production
\q1 \add resulting\add* from Yahweh’s fierce anger.
\s1 The what was said of God concerning the nations surrounding Israel
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 14 Yahweh says this: All my evil neighbours who touch the inheritance that I gave to my Israelite people, listen, I’ll uproot them from the land, and I’ll uproot Judah’s descendants from among them.
\v 15 Then after I’ve uprooted them, I’ll have compassion on them again, and I’ll bring them back—each one back to their own inheritance and their own land.
\v 16 If they’ll properly learn the ways of my people, to make promises using my name saying, ‘As Yahweh lives,’ instead of how my people were taught to make promises by Baal, then they’ll prosper and be my people \add again\add*.
\v 17 However if they won’t listen when I uproot the nation, then I’ll uproot it to destroy it. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\c 13
\s1 Jeremiah buries a loincloth
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Yahweh told me this: Go and buy yourself a linen loincloth and put it on, but don’t let it get near water.
\v 2 So I bought a loincloth as Yahweh had told me to, and I put it on.
\v 3 Then Yahweh \add gave\add* me a second instruction, saying:
\v 4 Take the loincloth that you bought and you’re wearing, and pack up and go to Euphrates\f + \fr 13:4 \ft About 6km away from Anatot. It’s possible that the Euphrates river is being referred to here, although less likely as that was a trip of over a thousand kilometres.\f* and bury it in a crack in the rock.
\v 5 So I went and hid it at Euphrates just as Yahweh had told me to.
\p
\v 6 Then after many days, Yahweh told me to get ready and go to Euphrates to retrieve the loincloth that he’d told me to bury there.
\v 7 So I went to Euphrates and dug the loincloth out from the crack that I’d hidden it in, but wow, it was ruined and totally worthless.
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh gave me this message:
\v 9 Yahweh says that I’ll rot away the pride of Judah, including the pride of the great Jerusalem.
\v 10 That wicked nation that refused to listen to my messages, who are following their own stubborn desires and have followed other god by serving them and bowing down to them, let that nation become like that loincloth that’s no good for anything.
\v 11 Yahweh declares that just like a loincloth is hitched to someone’s hips, so too I’ve made all those from Israel and Judah cling to me, to be my people who would bring me fame, praise, and honour, but they didn’t listen.
\s1 Filled with wine
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 12 So you must pass this message along to them: Israel’s god Yahweh says that every jar will be filled with wine, and they’ll tell you, ‘Of course we know that every jar will be filled with wine.’
\v 13 So tell them that Yahweh says this: Listen, I’m about to fill all the inhabitants of this land with drunkenness—the kings that sit of David’s throne, the priests and prophets, and all of Jerusalem’s inhabitants.
\v 14 Then Yahweh declares: I’ll cause a drunken brawl among brothers, parents, and children. I won’t pity you or have compassion, and I won’t spare them from destruction.
\s1 Jeremiah warns against pride
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 15 Listen and pay attention.
\q1 Don’t be arrogant, because Yahweh has spoken.
\q1
\v 16 Give honour to your god Yahweh before he brings darkness,
\q1 and before he causes you all to stumble on the mountains at twilight,
\q1 because you’re hoping for light, but he’ll turn the place into deep darkness—into a dark cloud.
\q1
\v 17 But if you all won’t listen,
\q1 I’ll weep in private because of your arrogance.
\q1 Tears will run down my eyes because Yahweh’s flock has been taken captive.
\p
\v 18 Tell the king and the queen mother to take a low seat
\q1 because their splendid crowns have been removed from their heads.
\q1
\v 19 The cities of the Negev have been shut up,
\q1 and there’s no one to open them \add again\add*.
\q1 Judah has been taken into exile—
\q1 they’ve all been taken far away.
\p
\v 20 Look up and see them coming from the north.
\q1 Where’s the flock that was given to you—those beautiful sheep?
\q1
\v 21 What will you say when the allies that you taught, he sets over you?
\q1 Won’t labour pains hit you like a woman giving birth?
\q1
\v 22 Then \add as a country\add* you’ll ask why those things have happened to you.
\q1 It’s the magnitude of your disobedience that’s caused your skirt to be lifted
\q1 and your body to be treated with violence.
\q1
\v 23 An Ethiopian person can’t change their skin \add colour\add*
\q1 and a leopard can’t change its spots.
\q1 Similarly you all aren’t able to do good
\q1 as you’re all so accustomed to doing evil.
\q1
\v 24 I’ll scatter them like straw that’s blown across the wilderness by the wind.
\q1
\v 25 Yahweh declares that that’s your part of your inheritance
\q1 as you’ve forgotten me and trusted in lies.
\q1
\v 26 So I’ll also pull your skirt up over your face
\q1 →and then your shame will become visible.
\q1
\v 27 I’ve seen your adultery and neighing—
\q1 the wickedness of your prostitution on the hills and across the countryside.
\q1 I’ve seen your detestable behaviour.
\q1 You won’t end well, Jerusalem.
\q1 How long will you remain ‘unclean’?
\c 14
\s1 The terrible drought
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 This is Yahweh’s message to Jeremiah regarding droughts:
\q1
\v 2 Judah is in mourning and its gates are neglected.
\q1 They’re mourning for the land
\q1 and Jerusalem is crying out \add in distress\add*.
\q1
\v 3 The leading citizens send their servants out for water,
\q1 ^\add but\add* when they get to the wells, they didn’t find any.
\q1 They return with empty containers—
\q1 ashamed and humiliated, and with their heads covered \add in grief\add*.
\q1
\v 4 The ground is cracked open because there hasn’t been any rain.
\q1 The farmers are ashamed—they’ve covered their heads \add in grief\add*,
\q1
\v 5 because even the doe in the countryside abandoned her newborn fawn as there was no grass.
\q1
\v 6 Wild donkeys stand on bare hills and pant for breath like jackals—
\q1 their eyes have failed because there’s no vegetation.
\q1
\v 7 Even though our transgressions testify against us,
\q1 do something, Yahweh, for the sake of your reputation
\q1 because we sinned against you with our frequent rebellion.
\q1
\v 8 \add You\add* are Israel’s hope—the one who rescues it at a time of distress.
\q1 Why would you act like a stranger in the land—
\q1 like a traveller who’s stopped in to stay the night.
\q1
\v 9 Why are you like someone just standing there with their mouth open—
\q1 like a warrior who’s powerless to save someone?
\q1 You’re \add here\add* among us, Yahweh, and we’ve been calling out to you \add to rescue us\add*.
\q1 Don’t abandon us.
\p
\v 10 This is what Yahweh says about this nation:
\q1 They love to wander, so they’ve gone wherever their feet take them,
\q1 →but Yahweh hasn’t accepted them.
\q1 Now he’ll remember their wickedness
\q1 and he’ll punish them for their disobedience.
\p
\v 11 Yahweh told me, “Don’t pray for good \add to come to\add* this nation
\v 12 because \add even\add* if they fast, I won’t listen to their petition, and when they offer up a burnt offering and a grain offering, I won’t be accepting them, because I’m bringing an end to them by the sword, and famine, and disease.”
\p
\v 13 Then I said, “Oh no, my master Yahweh. Listen, the prophets are telling the people that they won’t experience war or famine because you’ll give them ongoing peace in this place.”
\p
\v 14 “The prophets use my name but speak deceitfully,” Yahweh answered. “I didn’t appoint them and I didn’t send them, and I didn’t give them a false vision. The worthless prophecies that they’re declaring to you all \add come\add* from their own minds.”
\v 15 Therefore Yahweh says, “\add Now\add* those prophets who I never sent but who are using my name and say that no war or famine will happen in this region, \add well\add* those prophets will meet their \add own\add* ends through war and famine.
\v 16 Then the people that they prophesied to will be thrown out into Jerusalem’s streets because of famine and war. Thebes one will be there to bury them or their wives, sons, or daughters, as I’ll pour their wickedness out onto them.
\p
\v 17 Then you’ll pass this message onto them:
\q1 Day and night the tears run down my eyes.
\q1 Don’t let them stop, because of the terrible damage
\q1 as my virgin daughter has been shattered—
\q1 my people have \add received\add* a very severe wound.
\q1
\v 18 If I go out into the countryside, wow, I see those who were killed by the sword.
\q1 Then if I go into the city, wow, there’s diseases caused by famine,
\q1 because both prophets and priests have gone into a land that they weren’t familiar with.
\s1 Pleading for mercy
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 19 Have you completely rejected Judah?
\q1 Do you really hate Zion?
\q1 Why have you struck us when there’s no healing for us?
\q1 We eagerly hoped for peace, but there was nothing good.
\q1 We hoped for a time of healing, but wow, we got terror!
\q1
\v 20 Yahweh, we acknowledge our wickedness—
\q1 the transgression of our ancestors.
\q1 Yes, we’ve sinned against you.
\q1
\v 21 For the sake of your reputation, don’t reject us.
\q1 Don’t treat \add the place hosting\add* your wonderful throne with contempt.
\q1 Remember your agreement with us, and don’t break it.
\q1
\v 22 Do those useless \add idols\add* from other countries send rain?
\q1 Do they cause showers \add to fall\add*?
\q1 Isn’t it you, our god Yahweh, who does that?
\q1 We eagerly wait for you, because you’re the one who does all those things.
\c 15
\s1 Judah’s coming destruction
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told me, “Even if Moses or Samuel were standing in front of me, I still wouldn’t be in favour of this \add group of\add* people. Send them out away from me, and let them go away.
\v 2 If anyone comes to ask you where they should go, tell them that this is what Yahweh says:
\q1 Those who are \add destined\add* for death, will die.
\q1 Those for the sword, will die in war.
\q1 Those for famine, will suffer starvation.
\q1 Those for exile, will be taken away in captivity.
\p
\v 3 Yahweh declares that he’ll assign them to four groups:
\li1 1. killed by the sword,
\li1 2. dragged away by \add wild\add* dogs,
\li1 3. assigned to the birds \add of prey\add*, and
\li1 4. assigned to be devoured and destroyed by \add wild\add* animals.
\m
\v 4 Because of what Judah’s King Manasseh did in Jerusalem, I’ll make people in all the world’s nations be horrified by them.
\p
\v 5 Yes, who will feel sorry for you, Jerusalem,
\q1 and who will show sympathy for you?
\q1 Who will go out of their way to ask about your welfare?
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh declares: You’ve forsaken me—
\q1 you’ve turned your back on me
\q1 so I’ve used my power against you to destroy you.
\q1 I’m tired of letting you off.
\q1
\v 7 So I’ll winnow them with a pitchfork at the country’s gates.
\q1 I’ll make them childless, \add thus\add* destroying my people
\q1 as they haven’t turned from their \add own\add* ways.
\q1
\v 8 I’ll make more widows for them than sand \add grains\add* on the beach.
\q1 I’ll send a destroyer against the mothers of their young men at midday.
\q1 I’ll make shock and horror fall on them suddenly.
\q1
\v 9 The mother who has borne seven children will waste away.
\q1 She’ll gasp—her sun will set while it is still daytime.
\q1 She’ll be ashamed and embarrassed, because I will give those who remain
\q1 to \add be killed by\add* the sword in their enemies’ presence.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 Jeremiah’s complaint to Yahweh
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 10 Poor me, mother, that you gave birth to me—
\q1 a man of strife and contention throughout the entire region.
\q1 I haven’t lent money or borrowed it
\q1 ^\add yet\add* they all curse me.
\p
\v 11 Yahweh said,
\q1 Won’t I rescue you for good?
\q1 Won’t I make your enemies plead with you
\q1 in a time of evil and distress?
\q1
\v 12 Can \add anyone\add* break iron—
\q1 iron from the north, or bronze?
\p
\v 13 I’ll give your wealth and treasures to your enemies as plunder without them having to pay
\q1 as the price for all your sins committed across all your territories.
\q1
\v 14 Then I’ll make you serve your enemies in a land that you hadn’t been to before
\q1 because the fire of my anger towards you all has been kindled \add and\add* will burn.
\p
\v 15 Yahweh, you know \add my situation\add*.
\q1 Take note of my situation and bring your vengeance to those harassing me.
\q1 Don’t be patient with them and let them take me away.
\q1 Know that I suffer reproach for your sake.
\q1
\v 16 Your messages were found and I consumed them.
\q1 Your message to me made me happy and gave me cause for celebration
\q1 because I bear your name, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, god of armies.
\q1
\v 17 I didn’t sit among the gathering of the merry makers,
\q1 but rather, I enjoyed feeling your presence.
\q1 I sat alone because you’d filled me with indignation.
\q1
\v 18 Why is my pain ongoing and my wound incurable, refusing to be healed?
\q1 Will you be like a spring that lets me down when its supply is unreliable?
\p
\v 19 So Yahweh said this:
\q1 If you return \add to me\add*, then I’ll restore you and you’ll stand in front of me
\q1 because if you separate out the foolish things from the precious things,
\q1 you will be like my mouth. The people will come back to you,
\q1 but you yourself mustn’t go back to them.
\q1
\v 20 I’ll make you like an impenetrable bronze wall to this people,
\q1 and they’ll fight against you.
\q1 However, they won’t defeat you,
\q1 because I’ll be with you to save you, and to rescue you.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 21 Then I’ll rescue you from the plots of wicked people
\q1 and I’ll buy your freedom from the ruthless people.
\c 16
\s1 Jeremiah advised not to marry
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh gave me a message, telling me:
\v 2 Don’t get married and don’t have children in this place
\v 3 because Yahweh says this to the children born in this place and to the parents who brought them into this world:
\q1
\v 4 They’ll die from disease and not be mourned or buried—
\q1 they’ll be like dung on the ground.
\q1 They’ll come to their ends through war and famine,
\q1 and their corpses will become food for the birds and the wild animals.
\p
\v 5 Yes, Yahweh says this:
\q1 Don’t enter a home where there’s a funeral.
\q1 Don’t go to mourn or to show sympathy to them,
\q1 because I’ve withdrawn my peace from this people,
\q1 \add as well as\add* my compassion and loyal commitment.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 6 Both the important people and the ordinary people will die in this land.
\q1 They won’t be buried and no one will mourn for them,
\q1 or cut themselves or shave off their hair \add to show grief\add* for them.
\q1
\v 7 Thebes one must share any food to comfort those in mourning because of the deaths,
\q1 and no one must give a comforting cup to his father or mother.
\q1
\v 8 Don’t go to a dining establishment to sit with them to eat and drink
\q1
\v 9 because Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, \add right\add* in front of your eyes, in your lifetime and in this place,
\q1 I’m about to make the sounds of happiness and gladness cease—
\q1 \add including\add* the sounds of brides and grooms.
\p
\v 10 Then when you pass on all these messages to the people, they’ll ask you:
\q1 Why has Yahweh decreed all this terrible calamity against us,
\q1 and what wickedness or sin have we committed against our god Yahweh?
\p
\v 11 So give them this declaration from Yahweh:
\q1 It’s because your ancestors followed after foreign gods and served them and bowed down to them.
\q1 They abandoned me and didn’t follow my instructions.
\q1
\v 12 Then you yourselves have done \add even\add* more wickedness than your ancestors,
\q1 and everyone of you is walking by the stubbornness of their wicked heart—
\q1 not listening to me.
\q1
\v 13 So I will throw you all out of this land,
\q1 to a land that neither you or your ancestors have been to.
\s1 Return from exile
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 14 Now listen, Yahweh declares:
\q1 Because of that the days are coming when they’ll no longer say,
\q1 ‘As Yahweh, the one who brought the Israelites out of Egypt, lives.’
\q1
\v 15 Instead they’ll say:
\q1 ‘As Yahweh lives, the one who brought the Israelites back from the land of the north
\q1 and from the lands where he had driven them,’
\q1 because I’ll bring them back to the land that I gave to their ancestors.
\s1 The coming punishment
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 16 Listen, Yahweh declares:
\q1 I’ll send for many fishermen to fish the people out.
\q1 After that, I’ll send for many hunters
\q1 to hunt them from under every mountain and hill, and from the crevices in the rocks
\q1
\v 17 because I’m watching everything they do
\q1 and their wicked behaviour can’t be hidden from me.
\q1
\v 18 Firstly, I’ll pay back double for their disobedience and wickedness,
\q1 because of their polluting my land with their detestable carvings,
\q1 and because they filled my inheritance with their disgusting idols.”
\s1 Jeremiah thanks for safety
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 19 Yahweh, you’re my fortress and my refuge—
\q1 my place of safety in any distressing time.
\q1 The nations will go to you from the ends of the earth and say,
\q1 “Surely our ancestors inherited lies—
\q1 Those idols are a waste of time and there’s no profit in them.
\q1
\v 20 Can people make their own gods?
\q1 Well, they’re not gods \add at all\add*!
\p
\v 21 So listen, I’m about to demonstrate that at this time—
\q1 I’ll reveal my power and activity to them
\q1 →and then they’ll know that my name is Yahweh.
\c 17
\s1 Judah’s sin and punishment
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Judah’s sin is written with an iron stylus that has a diamond point.
\q1 It’s engraved on the tablet which is their hearts, and on the projecting corners of your altars.
\q1
\v 2 Even their children remember their altars and their Asherah poles
\q1 that were beside the luxuriant trees and on the high hills—
\q1
\v 3 \add even on\add* my mountain in the countryside.
\q1 I’ll give your wealth and all your treasures to be plundered \add by others\add*
\q1 \add because\add* of your sin on your hilltop shrines throughout all your territories.
\q1
\v 4 You’ll have to let go the inheritance that I gave you.
\q1 I’ll make your serve your enemies in a land that you’ve never been to,
\q1 because you’ve ignited a fire in my anger which will burn forever.
\s1 Various sayings
\p
\v 5 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Anyone who trusts in humankind will be cursed—
\q1 trusting in their own physical strength turns their hearts away from Yahweh.
\q1
\v 6 They’re like shrubs in the desert, unable to see any good coming,
\q1 and they’ll live in dry parts of the wilderness
\q1 that’s salty and with no one \add else\add* living there.
\b
\q1
\v 7 The person who trusts in Yahweh will be blessed,
\q1 as they put their confidence in him.
\q1
\v 8 That person will be like a tree planted by a stream
\q1 that sends its roots out to the water.
\q1 It has nothing to fear when the heat comes and its leaves stay green.
\q1 It has nothing to be afraid of in a drought year
\q1 and it won’t stop producing fruit.
\b
\q1
\v 9 Human motives are the most deceitful of all.
\q1 They can’t be cured—who can determine them?
\q1
\v 10 I am Yahweh who searches the mind.
\q1 I test the motives.
\q1 I give to everyone according to the results of their actions.
\b
\q1
\v 11 A partridge hatches an egg that it didn’t lay.
\q1 Someone might get rich by unjust means
\q1 ^\add but right\add* in the middle of their life it’ll leave them
\q1 and in the end, that person will be a fool.
\s1 Jeremiah’s request to Yahweh
\p
\v 12 The place of our sanctuary from the beginning has been \add like\add* a splendid throne \add on\add* a high place.
\q1
\v 13 \add You,\add* Yahweh are the one that Israel puts their hope in.
\q1 All who abandon you will be put to shame.
\q1 Those who turn away from you will be written on the ground,
\q1 because they’ve forsaken Yahweh—the fountain of living waters.
\q1
\v 14 Heal me, Yahweh, and I’ll be healed.
\q1 Rescue me, and I’ll be rescued,
\q1 because you are the one I praise.
\q1
\v 15 Listen, they’re asking me,
\q1 Where is Yahweh’s message? Let it come.
\q1
\v 16 As for me, I didn’t run from being a shepherd following you.
\q1 I didn’t long for the day of disaster.
\q1 You know everything I said—it was said in your presence.
\q1
\v 17 Don’t become a terror to me.
\q1 You’re my place of safety when disaster comes.
\q1
\v 18 Let my pursuers be ashamed, but don’t let me be ashamed.
\q1 Let them be dismayed, but don’t let me be dismayed.
\q1 Send the day of disaster against them
\q1 and shatter them with a double share of destruction.
\s1 Observing the rest day
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 19 Yahweh told me this:
\q1 Go and stand in at gate of the people where Judah’s kings come and go,
\q1 then in all Jerusalem’s \add other\add* gates.
\q1
\v 20 Tell the people:
\q1 Listen to Yahweh’s message, \add you\add* kings of Judah and all you \add people of\add* Judah,
\q1 and all of Jerusalem’s residents who go in through those gates.
\q1
\v 21 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Be careful \add for the sake of\add* your lives
\q1 and on the rest day, don’t carry a load to bring it to Jerusalem’s gates.
\q1
\v 22 Don’t bring a load out from your homes on the rest day.
\q1 Don’t do any work, but set apart the rest day,
\q1 just as I instructed your ancestors to do.
\q1
\v 23 They did not listen or pay attention,
\q1 but stubbornly refused to listen to me, nor did they accept discipline.
\p
\v 24 Yahweh declares that if you carefully listen to me
\q1 and don’t bring a load to this city’s gates on the rest day
\q1 ^but instead set apart the rest day for Yahweh, and don’t do any work on it,
\q1
\v 25 then kings, princes, and those who sit on David’s throne
\q1 will come to this city’s gates in chariots and on horses.
\q1 They’ll come along with their leaders from Judah including Jerusalem’s residents,
\q1 and this city will be inhabited forever.
\q1
\v 26 They’ll come from Judah’s cities including from all around Jerusalem,
\q1 from the Benjamin region and the foothills,
\q1 from the mountains, and from the Negev \add desert\add*,
\q1 bringing burnt offerings and sacrifices, grain offerings and frankincense,
\q1 as well as those who bring thank offerings to Yahweh’s residence.
\q1
\v 27 But if you don’t listen to me
\q1 and don’t set apart the rest day
\q1 and don’t carry heavy loads to enter Jerusalem’s gates on the rest day,
\q1 →then I’ll light a fire at its gates that will incinerate Jerusalem’s fortresses,
\q1 and that won’t be able to be put out.
\c 18
\s1 Jeremiah visits the potter
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Yahweh’s message came to Jeremiah to say,
\v 2 “Get ready and go down to the potter’s house, and I’ll give you my messages there.”
\p
\v 3 So I went down to pottery factory, and wow, he was on his potter’s wheel.
\v 4 However, the container that he was making with his hand on the clay was ruined, so he started again and make it into another container that he was pleased with.
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh gave me this message:
\v 6 Like that potter, shouldn’t I be able to do what I want with you Israelites? Yahweh declares that you Israelites are in my hand like that clay in the potter’s hand.
\v 7 One time, I might declare to a nation or a kingdom that it will be uprooted, torn down, and destroyed.
\v 8 and \add later on,\add* that nation that I spoke to might turn away from its evil, and then I’ll cancel the destruction that I’d planned for them.
\v 9 Another time, I might declare that a nation or kingdom will be established, or strengthened,
\v 10 however if I see it doing evil and not listening to my voice, then I’ll cancel the good that I’d said I’d do to it.
\v 11 So now, speak to the people of Judah including Jerusalem’s residents and tell them that Yahweh says, “Listen, I’m about to devise a plan against you all and prepare calamity for you. Each of you needs to turn back from their evil activities and improve your behaviour and your activities.”
\v 12 However, they’ll say, “Don’t waste your time because we’ll continue with our plans, and each of us will do \add whatever\add* wicked things that we want!”
\s1 Israel deserts Yahweh
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 13 Because of that, Yahweh says this:
\q1 Ask the nations if they’ve ever heard anything like this?
\q2 The virgin Israel has done something extremely horrible.
\q1
\v 14 Does the snow in Lebanon ever disappear off its rocky slopes?
\q1 Do the cool waters stop flowing?
\q1
\v 15 Yet my people have forgotten me.
\q1 They offer incense to useless idols
\q1 that have made them stumble on the ancient paths
\q1 that haven’t had any maintenance.
\q1
\v 16 Their land will become a wasteland—forever mocked at.
\q1 Everyone who passes by will be appalled
\q1 and will wag their heads.
\q1
\v 17 I’ll scatter them before their enemies like the east wind \add from the desert\add* does.
\q1 I won’t look at them but will turn my back when their disaster comes.
\s1 A plot against Jeremiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\sp The people
\p
\v 18 Then the people said,
\q1 Come on, let’s decide what to do about Jeremiah,
\q1 because we have plenty of instructions from the priests,
\q1 advice from the wise men,
\q1 and messages from the prophets.
\sp Jeremiah
\p
\v 19 Yahweh, pay attention to me
\q1 and listen to the what my enemies are saying.
\q1
\v 20 Should good be repaid with evil,
\q1 because they’re digging a pit for me?
\q1 Remember how I stood in front of you to tell you their good points—
\q1 to turn back your rage from them.
\q1
\v 21 Therefore, hand their children over to famine,
\q1 and send war to punish them.
\q1 Let their wives be childless and become widowed,
\q1 and let their husbands die from disease—
\q1 their young men killed in battle.
\q1
\v 22 Let a cry of distress be heard from their houses
\q1 when you send raiders to surprise them,
\q1 because they’ve dug a pit to capture me in
\q1 and have hidden traps for me to step into.
\q1
\v 23 But you, Yahweh, know all their deadly plots against me.
\q1 Don’t forgive their wickedness.
\q1 Don’t erase their disobedience from your sight.
\q1 Let them be overthrown as you watch
\q1 at the time when you deal with them in your anger.
\c 19
\s1 The shattered flask
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh said this:
\q1 Take some of the elders and some of the priests,
\q1 and go and buy a clay flask from the potter.
\q1
\v 2 Then go out to the Ben-Hinom valley by the Broken Pottery gate,
\q1 and then declare the words that I’ll tell you there.
\q1
\v 3 Tell them:
\q1 You kings of Judah and \add you\add* residents of Jerusalem,
\q1 listen to Yahweh’s message.
\q1 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says,
\q1 Listen, I’m about to send calamity to this place,
\q1 and the ears of everyone who hears about it will tingle.
\q1
\v 4 I’m doing that because they’ve abandoned me and defiled this place—
\q1 they’ve burnt incense to other ‘gods’,
\q1 that neither they or their ancestors or Judah’s kings used to know.
\q1 Then they’ve filled this place with the blood of the innocent.
\q1
\v 5 They built hilltop shrines to burn their children in the fire as offerings to Baal,
\q1 something I never instructed or talked about, nor did it \add even\add* enter my mind.
\p
\v 6 So Yahweh declares that the time is coming when this place will no longer be called Tofet or the Ben-Hinom valley, but \add rather\add*, the ‘Slaughter Valley’.
\v 7 Then I’ll confound Judah and Jerusalem’s plans in this place, and I’ll have them struck down by their enemies’ swords and by those who want to kill them, and I’ll give their dead bodies to the birds and the wild animals for food.
\v 8 Then I’ll make that city into a wasteland that’s something to scoff at—everyone who passes by will be appalled and will mock it because of all its disasters.
\v 9 I’ll cause them to eat the flesh of their children and of their neighbours during the distress caused by the siege imposed by the enemies who want to kill them.
\p
\v 10 Then as the men who went with you watch, you must break that pottery flask
\v 11 and tell them that Commander-in_chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 I’ll shatter this nation and this city, just like someone shatters a piece of pottery that can never be repaired again. They’ll bury the dead in Tofet until they run out of room.
\v 12 Yahweh declares that I’ll do that to this place and its residents—making this city like Tofet
\v 13 and Jerusalem’s houses including the houses of Judah’s kings will be defiled like that place, Tofet—all the houses on whose rooftops they burnt incense to all the constellations and poured out drink offerings to other ‘gods’.
\p
\v 14 Then Jeremiah returned from Tofet where Yahweh had sent him to prophesy, and he stood in the temple courtyard and declared to all the people,
\v 15 “This is what Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, says: ‘Listen, every disaster that I’ve pronounced against these people, I’m about to send to this city and to all the villages around it, because they’ve been stubborn and failed to take notice of my messages.’ ”
\c 20
\s1 Jeremiah’s conflict with Pashhur
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 The priest, Pashhur (Imer’s son) who was a supervisor, heard Jeremiah prophesying those words at Yahweh’s house,
\v 2 and he struck the prophet Jeremiah. Then he placed him in the stocks that were at the upper Benjamin gate hear Yahweh’s house.
\v 3 The next day, when Pashhur released Jeremiah from the stocks, Jeremiah said to him, “Yahweh won’t call you ‘Pashhur’, but ‘Magor-Misaviv’ \add (which means ‘surrounded by terror’)\add*,
\v 4 because Yahweh says this: Listen, I’ll make you a terror to yourself and to all your friends. They’ll fall by their enemies’ swords before your \add very\add* eyes, and then I’ll hand Judah over to the Babylonian king, and he’ll carry them away to Babylon and put them to death.
\v 5 I’ll give away all the wealth of this city—all its products and valuables, and all the treasures of Judah’s kings—to their enemies. They’ll plunder it, seize it, and carry it off to Babylon.
\v 6 Then you, Pashhur will be taken into captivity along with all the others in your home. You’ll be taken to Babylon, and you’ll die there and be buried—you and all your friends who you’ve prophesied these lies to.
\s1 Jeremiah’s complaint to Yahweh
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 7 Yahweh, you’ve deceived me, and I was deceived.
\q1 You’ve overcome me and prevailed.
\q1 I’m laughed at all day long—
\q1 everyone mocks me.
\b
\q1
\v 8 Yes, whenever I speak, I call out loudly.
\q1 I declare violence and destruction
\q1 because Yahweh’s message has become a reproach and derision for me, all day \add long\add*.
\q1
\v 9 If I say that I won’t mention him
\q1 and won’t speak in his name anymore,
\q1 →then his message becomes a fire burning in my heart—
\q1 shut up in my bones.
\q1 →Then I get worn out holding it in,
\q1 and I can’t continue \add to do it\add*.
\q1
\v 10 Yes, I’ve heard many people whispering
\q1 that terror is all around.
\q1 They want to publicly denounce me.
\q1 All my trusted friends watch for my fall:
\q1 “Perhaps he can be deceived
\q1 →so that we may prevail against him
\q1 and take out our vengeance on him.”
\q1
\v 11 However Yahweh is like a fearsome warrior with me,
\q1 →therefore my persecutors will stumble and won’t prevail.
\q1 Because they won’t succeed, they’ll be totally put to shame—
\q1 an everlasting disgrace that’ll never be forgotten.
\q1
\v 12 Commander-in-chief Yahweh, who examines the righteous people,
\q1 who sees the thoughts and motives,
\q1 let me see your vengeance on them,
\q1 →because I’ve made my case known to you.
\q1
\v 13 Sing to Yahweh.
\q1 Praise Yahweh because he’s rescued the lives of those who are oppressed from the clutch of those who do evil.
\b
\q1
\v 14 Let the day when I was born be cursed.
\q1 Don’t let the day that my mother gave birth to me be blessed.
\q1
\v 15 Let the man who brought the news to my father be cursed—
\q1 The one who said, ‘You now have a son,’ causing great celebration.
\q1
\v 16 Let that man be like the cities that Yahweh overthrew without having compassion on them.
\q1 Let him hear a cry for help in the dawn and a battle cry at noontime,
\q1
\v 17 because he didn’t kill me in the womb—
\q1 →making my mother become my tomb—her womb forever pregnant.
\q1
\v 18 Why did I come out from the womb to see trouble and sorrow,
\q1 and to end my days in shame?
\c 21
\s1 Jerusalem’s future destruction
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh gave Jeremiah a message when King Zedekiah sent Malkiyah’s son Pashhur and Ma’aseyah’s son Zephaniah the priest to him, and they said,
\v 2 “Please request Yahweh’s advice on our behalf, because the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar is attacking us. Perhaps Yahweh will do miracles for us like \add he has in the past\add*, and will make him stop attacking us and leave.”
\p
\v 3 So Jeremiah told them to tell \add King\add* Zedekiah that
\v 4 Israel’s god Yahweh says this:
\m Listen, I’m about to make your own weapons ineffective that you’re all using to fight the Babylonian king and the Chaldeans who are laying siege to you from outside the wall, and I’ll gather them\f + \fr 21:4 \ft This last part of the sentence is left quite literal, as it’s ambiguous in the Hebrew, what or who the ‘them’ is referring to here.\f* in the middle of this city.
\v 5 Then I myself will fight you all with all my strength and power in my anger and rage and terrible fury,
\v 6 and I’ll kill this city’s inhabitants—people and animals will all die from terrible, deadly diseases.
\v 7 Then Yahweh declares that after that I’ll hand over Judah’s King Zedekiah along with his officers, and the people in this city who survive the deadly diseases and sword and famine, to the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar, and to their enemies who want to kill them. He’ll kill them with the sword—he won’t spare them or show pity or compassion.
\p
\v 8 Then you must tell this to the people:
\m Yahweh says that I’m about to give you the choice between the path to life and the path to death.
\v 9 Anyone who stays in this city will die from war, famine, or deadly diseases, but anyone who goes out and falls to their knees in front of the Babylonians who’ve surrounded you all, will live They’ll escape with their lives \add that way\add*
\v 10 because I’ve already decided to harm this city rather than doing good. Yahweh declares that this city will be handed over to the Babylonian king and he’ll burn it \add down\add*.
\s1 Yahweh’s punishment for Judah’s kings
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 11 As for Judah’s king, listen to this message from Yahweh
\v 12 for David’s descendants:
\q1 Administer justice \add every\add* morning
\q1 and rescue anyone who’s been robbed, from their oppressor,
\q1 otherwise my fury will go out like fire
\q1 and burn, but no one will be able to extinguish it
\q1 because of your wicked activities.
\q1
\v 13 Yahweh declares: listen you inhabitants from the valley
\q1 \add and you\add* on the rocky plain.
\q1 You all say \add that no one\add* can swoop down on you,
\q1 or \add be able to\add* enter your hiding places,
\q1
\v 14 however I will punish you all according to the results of your actions.
\q1 Yahweh declares:
\q1 I’ll start a fire in your forest
\q1 and it’ll consume everything around it.
\c 22
\s1 The message for Judah’s king
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Yahweh told me to go down to Judah’s palace and declare this message:
\v 2 Listen to Yahweh’s message for Judah’s king who sits on David’s throne, along with your officials and your people who enter these doorways.
\v 3 Yahweh says \add that you must\add* administer justice and do what’s right. Rescue anyone who’s been robbed, from their oppressor. Don’t mistreat any foreigner or orphan or widow. Don’t be violent, and don’t shed innocent blood in this place,
\v 4 because if you carefully do those things, then the kings who sit on David’s throne will enter through the gates of this palace riding in chariots or on horses, along with their officials and their people.
\v 5 However, if you take no notice of those instructions, then Yahweh solemnly promises that this palace will become a pile of rubble,
\v 6 because this is what Yahweh says about the palace of Judah’s king:
\q1 To me, you’re like the Gilead \add forests\add* or like Lebanon’s summit.
\q1 ^\add However,\add* I’ll make you into a wilderness—
\q1 cities that aren’t inhabited.
\q1
\v 7 I’ll appoint destroyers against you—
\q1 each \add warrior\add* with his weapons.
\q1 They’ll cut down your choicest cedars
\q1 and throw them onto the fire.
\p
\v 8 Then many nations will pass by this city and ask each other why Yahweh has done that to this great city?
\v 9 Someone else will answer that it’s because they abandoned the agreement with their god Yahweh, and bowed down to their gods and served them.
\s1 Jeremiah’s message about King Josiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 10 Don’t weep for King Josiah who was killed
\q1 and don’t mourn \add because he died\add*.
\q1 ^\add Instead,\add* weep bitterly for his son, King Yehoahaz who’s about to be exiled
\q1 because he won’t return again and see his native land \add again\add*.
\p
\v 11 Yes, Yahweh says this about Josiah’s son Yehoahaz\f + \fr 22:11 \ft Called ‘Shallum’ here in the Hebrew.\f* who reigned instead of his father, who \add then\add* left this place. He won’t return there again
\v 12 because he’ll die in the place where they took him into exile, and he won’t \add ever\add* see this country again.
\s1 Jeremiah’s message about Yehoyakim
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 13 The king\f + \fr 22:13 \ft Referring here to Judah’s King Yehoyakim—see verse 24.\f* who built his house by doing what’s not right
\q1 and built his upper rooms by unjustly \add forcing people to work\add*,
\q1 →won’t end well.
\q1 He forced the citizens to work without getting paid—
\q1 not giving them their wages.
\q1
\v 14 He’d decided to build himself a large house with spacious upper rooms,
\q1 so he cut out large windows for it, and panelled it with cedar,
\q1 and painted it with red \add ochre\add*.
\q1
\v 15 Does having the best cedar make you a \add better\add* king?
\q1 Didn’t your father \add Josiah\add* eat and drink \add well\add*?
\q1 However, he administered justice and did what was right
\q1 →so \add things went\add* well for him.
\q1
\v 16 He took up the cause of the poor and needy,
\q1 →so \add things went\add* well for him.
\q1 Yahweh declares that that’s what it means to know me,
\q1
\v 17 but your desires and motives are \add only focused on\add* your dishonest gain,
\q1 on shedding innocent blood,
\q1 and on oppression and practicing extortion.
\p
\v 18 Therefore this is what Yahweh says about Yehoyakim, son of Judah’s King Josiah:
\q1 They won’t mourn for him \add saying,\add* ‘Oh no, my brother! Oh no, my sister!’
\q1 They won’t mourn for him \add saying,\add* ‘Oh no, my master! Oh no, his splendour!’
\q1
\v 19 He’ll be buried just like a donkey would be—
\q1 dragged away and thrown \add somewhere\add* outside Jerusalem’s gates.
\s1 Jeremiah’s message about Jerusalem’s fate
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 20 \add You people of Judah\add*, go up to Lebanon and cry out.
\q1 Shout in the Bashan \add region\add*.
\q1 Cry out from the Avarim \add mountains\add*,
\q1 because all your ‘lovers’ have been destroyed.
\q1
\v 21 I warned you when you were still secure—
\q1 you said that you wouldn’t listen.
\q1 That’s been your way since your youth,
\q1 that you haven’t taken any notice of my instructions.
\q1
\v 22 The wind will drive away all your ‘shepherds’,
\q1 and your ‘lovers’ will go into captivity.
\q1 →Then you’ll be ashamed and humiliated because of all your wickedness.
\q1
\v 23 You who live in Lebanon,
\q1 nested \add there\add* among the cedars,
\q1 ^\add but\add* how you’ll groan when your labour pains come to you,
\q1 like a woman giving birth.
\s1 Judgement for Yehoyakin
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 24 Yahweh declares by his life:
\m Even if \add you\add*, Yehoyakin,\f + \fr 22:24 \ft Called ‘Shallum’ here in the Hebrew and in verse 28.\f* son of Judah’s King Yehoyakim, were the signet ring on my right hand, I would tear you off
\v 25 because I’m handing you over to the ones who are wanting to kill you and to those that you’re afraid of—to Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar and his Chaldeans.
\v 26 I’ll hurl you and your mother who bore you, into another land—a country where you’re both foreigners and you’ll both die there.
\v 27 Although they’ll long to return to this land, they’ll never return here.
\p
\v 28 Is this man Yehoyakin a despised, shattered pot—
\q1 a jar that no one wants
\q1 Why will before hurled out along with his descendants,
\q1 and thrown into a country that they’re not familiar with?
\q1
\v 29 Land, land, land, Listen to Yahweh’s message.
\p
\v 30 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Record this man as being childless—
\q1 a man who won’t prosper in his lifetime.
\q1 None of his descendants will prosper
\q1 to sit on David’s throne
\q1 or to rule over Judah again.
\c 23
\s1 Returning under new shepherds
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Yahweh declares that the shepherds who destroy and scatter the flock from my pasture won’t end well.
\v 2 Therefore, this is what Israel’s god Yahweh says about the shepherds who tend My people: Yahweh declares that you’ve all scattered my flock and driven them away, and haven’t looked after them. Listen, I’m about to punish you all for your wicked actions.
\v 3 Then I myself will gather the remnant of my flock from all the countries that I’ve banished them to and I’ll return them to their pasture, where they’ll be fruitful and multiply.
\v 4 Yahweh declares that I’ll appoint shepherds over them who’ll look after them, and then they’ll no longer be afraid or dismayed, nor will any go missing.
\v 5 Listen, Yahweh declares that the time is coming
\q1 when I will appoint a righteous branch for David
\q1 and he will reign wisely as king
\q1 and will administer justice and righteousness in the land.
\q1
\v 6 At that time, Judah will be rescued and Israel will live in security,
\q1 and this is the name that he’ll be known by: Yahweh who does what is right for us.
\p
\v 7 Listen, Yahweh declares that the time is coming when they’ll no longer say, ‘As surely as Yahweh lives, who brought the Israelites out from Egypt.’
\v 8 Instead they’ll say, ‘As surely as Yahweh lives, who brought and led the Israelites out of the land in the north and all the other lands that he’d banished them to.’ Then they’ll live in their own land again.
\s1 Lying prophets
\s4 Southern kingdom
\q1
\v 9 As for the prophets,
\q1 I feel broken inside and all my bones tremble.
\q1 I’ve become like a drunkard—like a man overcome by wine,
\q1 because of Yahweh—because of his holy words.
\q1
\v 10 Yes, the land is full of adulterers—
\q1 the land mourns because of the curse,
\q1 and the pastures in the wilderness have dried up—
\q1 their course is evil and their power is misused.
\q1
\v 11 Yes, both prophet and priest are compromised—
\q1 even in my house I’ve discovered their wickedness.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 12 Therefore their path will become slippery.
\q1 They’ll be pushed into the darkness and will fall into it
\q1 because I’ll send them disaster in the year of their punishment.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 13 Among the prophets in Samaria, I’ve seen offensive behaviour:
\q1 they prophesied by Baal and led my people Israel astray.
\q1
\v 14 Also, among the prophets in Jerusalem, I’ve seen horrible things:
\q1 They commit adultery and walk in lies.
\q1 They empower those who do evil,
\q1 →so that no one turns their back on wickedness.
\q1 They’re all like Sodom to me.
\q1 The people in Jerusalem are like Gomorrah,
\q1
\v 15 therefore this is what Commander-in-chief Yahweh says about the prophets:
\q1 I’ll feed them wormwood
\q1 and give them poisoned water to drink,
\q1 because ungodliness has spread throughout the land
\q1 starting from Jerusalem’s prophets.
\p
\v 16 This is what Commander-in-chief Yahweh says:
\q1 Don’t listen to the words of the prophets who prophesy to you.
\q1 They are filling you all with false hopes.
\q1 They speak visions from their own minds,
\q1 not from Yahweh’s mouth.
\q1
\v 17 They keep telling those who despise me,
\q1 that Yahweh says that you’ll all have peace.
\q1 They tell everyone who practises their own stubborn ways
\q1 that no harm will come to them.
\p
\v 18 But which of them has attended Yahweh’s council \add meeting\add*
\q1 \add where he could\add* see \add what’s happening\add* and hear his message?
\q1 Who has paid attention to his message and obeyed it?
\q1
\v 19 Listen, the storm of Yahweh’s rage is going out,
\q1 and the storm is creating a whirlwind that’s whirling around the heads of those who are wicked.
\q1
\v 20 Yahweh’s anger won’t turn back until he’s carried out his plans and purposes.
\q1 In the final days, you’ll all understand it.
\p
\v 21 I didn’t send those prophets, yet they rushed \add to declare their messages\add*.
\q1 I didn’t speak to them, yet they \add still\add* prophesied.
\q1
\v 22 If they had \add in fact\add* attended my council \add meeting\add*,
\q1 they would have declared my messages to my people
\q1 and turned them back from their wickedness and their wicked activities.
\p
\v 23 Yahweh declares:
\q1 Am I a god who’s only nearby, but who isn’t \add also\add* a god from far away?
\q1
\v 24 Would someone hide themself in some place and \add think\add* that I couldn’t see them? Yahweh asks.
\q1 Don’t I fill the heavens and the earth? declares Yahweh.
\p
\v 25 Those prophets who prophesy lies in my name, I’ve heard them say, ‘I had a dream. I had a dream!’
\v 26 How long will this go on, with prophets who prophesy lies from their own minds, but who are prophesying from the deceitfulness of their own hearts.
\v 27 They’re planning on making my people forget my name with the dreams that they report, each one to his neighbour, just as their ancestors forgot my name in favour of Baal’s name.
\v 28 Let the prophet who has a dream, report that dream. However, the one who I’ve declared something to, let him declare my message truthfully. What does straw have to do with grain? That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 29 Isn’t my message like fire, and like a hammer that shatters rock? That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 30 So listen, Yahweh declares that I’m against the prophets who steal my messages from someone else.
\v 31 Yes, I’m against those prophets who speak their own messages by claim that the messages came from me.
\v 32 Yahweh declares that indeed, I’m against those who prophesy false dreams and retell them to lead my people astray with their reckless lies. It wasn’t me who sent or commanded them, and they’re of no benefit at all to those people. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 False prophets
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 33 Now when this people \add group\add* or a prophet or priest asks you, ‘What is Yahweh’s burden?’\f + \fr 23:33 \ft The Hebrew word translated ‘burden’ could alternatively mean ‘message of doom’ here and through to verse 40, except that it’s also used to describe the people.\f* you should tell them, ‘\add You are the\add* burden and I’ll abandon you.’ That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 34 As for the prophet or priest or anyone who claims that he knows Yahweh’s burden, I’ll punish that man and his household.
\v 35 This is what each of you should ask their friend or relative: ‘What did Yahweh answer?’ or ‘What has Yahweh said?’
\v 36 But don’t refer anymore to Yahweh’s burden, because each person’s message becomes the burden, so you all \add end up\add* perverting the words of the living god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, our god.
\v 37 This is what you should ask to the prophet, ‘What answer did Yahweh give you?’ or ‘What did Yahweh say?’
\v 38 However if you say, ‘Yahweh’s burden’, then this is what Yahweh says: Because you said those words, ‘Yahweh’s burden,’ after I sent you all \add a message\add* saying, ‘Don’t say, “Yahweh’s burden,’
\v 39 therefore listen, I’ll definitely forget you and will remove you all out of my presence—both you and your ancestors, and the city that I gave you.
\v 40 Then I’ll put permanent shame on you all, and continual insults that won’t be forgotten.
\c 24
\s1 Two baskets of figs
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 After the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar had carried away Yehoyakin, son of Judah’s King Yehoyakim, as well as Judah’s officials and Jerusalem’s craftsmen and metalsmiths, and had taken them to Babylon, Yahweh showed me two baskets of figs placed in front of his temple.
\v 2 One basket of figs was very good like early, ripe figs, but the figs in the other basket were so bad that they weren’t edible.
\v 3 Then Yahweh asked me, “Jeremiah, what do you see?”
\p “Figs,” I replied. “\add Some\add* figs that look very good, and others that are so bad they’re inedible.”
\p
\v 4 Then Yahweh gave me this message:
\v 5 Israel’s god, Yahweh, says this: I’ll look favourably at the exiles from Judah just like those good figs, the exiles that I’ve sent out from this place to Babylon.
\v 6 I’ll give them my favour and restore them to this land. I’ll build them up, and not tear them down. I’ll plant them, and not uproot them.
\v 7 Then I will give them a desire to know me, because I’m Yahweh. They’ll be my people and I will be their god, so they will turn to me wholeheartedly.
\p
\v 8 But Yahweh declares that \add just\add* like those bad figs that are inedible \add will be discarded\add*, I’ll act in that \add same\add* way towards Judah’s King Zedekiah, along with his officials and the rest of Jerusalem who remain in this land or go to stay in Egypt.
\v 9 I’ll turn them into something horrifying—a disaster, \add right there\add* in the sight of all the world’s kingdoms—a disgrace and a subject for proverbs, taunts, and curses in every place that I’ll drive them into.
\v 10 I’ll send armies, famine, and disease against them until they’re destroyed from the land that I gave to them and their ancestors.
\c 25
\s1 Exile to Babylon foretold
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 This is the message that came to Jeremiah concerning all the people of Judah in the fourth year of \add the reign of\add* Yehoyakim (son of Judah’s King Josiah, which was the first year of \add the reign of\add* the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar.
\v 2 The prophet Jeremiah declared this to all the people across Judah including all of Jerusalem’s inhabitants, saying:
\v 3 Yahweh’s messages have been coming to me for twenty-three years since the thirteenth year of the reign of Judah’s king Josiah (\add Josiah,\add* Amon’s son), and I’ve passed them on to you all over and over again, but you haven’t taken any notice \add of them\add*.
\v 4 Yahweh sent all his servants the prophets to you again and again, but you all haven’t listened or paid any attention.
\v 5 Those prophets said, ‘Let everyone turn from their wicked ways and their evil activities, and return to the land that Yahweh gave in ancient times to your ancestors and to all of you as a permanent \add inheritance\add*.
\v 6 So don’t walk after other gods to worship them or to bow down to them, and don’t provoke him with things you make with your hands, and then he won’t harm you all.’
\v 7 But Yahweh declares that you all haven’t listened to me, because with what you’ve made, you’ve provoked me to do harm to you.
\p
\v 8 So Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: Since all of you haven’t listen to my messages,
\v 9 listen, I’m about to send out a command to fetch all the northern peoples. Yahweh declares that I will bring my servant, the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar and \add his armies\add* against this land and its inhabitants, and against all the nations around you. I will mark them for destruction and turn them into a horror—something to be hissed at, and permanent ruins.
\v 10 I’ll put an end to the sounds of gladness and celebration, the sounds of brides and grooms, \add and\add* the sound of the millstones and the light of the lamp.
\v 11 Then all of this land will become a desolation and a horror, and these nations will serve the Babylonian king for seventy years.
\p
\v 12 Then after seventy years have passed, then I’ll punish the king of Babylon and that nation—the land of the Chaldeans—for their wickedness, and make it into a permanent wasteland. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 13 Then I’ll carry out all the messages that I’d spoken against that land, and everything written in this document that Jeremiah has prophesied against all the nations.
\v 14 Yes many other nations and great kings will also make slaves out of those nations. I’ll repay them for everything they’ve done.
\s1 The punishment of God of nations
\p
\v 15 Yes Israel’s god Yahweh told me, “Take this cup of the wine of fury from my hand, and all the nations that I’m sending you to, make them drink it.
\v 16 They’ll drink and then stagger around and act like mad people when they see the deadly army that I’m sending out to them.
\p
\v 17 So I took the cup from Yahweh’s hand and made all the nations that he’d sent me to, drink from it,
\v 18 \add as well as\add* Judah’s cities including Jerusalem and its king and officials—to turn them into ruins and something terrifying, and into something to hiss and curse at, as they are until this present day.
\v 19 \add Those nations included:\add*
\li1 Egypt’s King Pharaoh, his officials, his leaders, and all his people,
\li1
\v 20 all people of mixed heritage and all the kings of Uz,
\li1 all the kings of Philistia: Ashkelon, Gaza, Ekron, and the remnant from Ashdod,
\li1
\v 21 Edom, Moab, and the Ammonites,
\li1
\v 22 all the kings of Tyre and Sidon,
\li1 the kings from the coastlands across the sea,
\li1
\v 23 Dedan, Teyma, Buz,
\li1 and all who cut the corners \add of their hair\add*,
\li1
\v 24 all the Aravi kings,
\li1 and all the kings of the mixed tribes who live in the desert,
\li1
\v 25 all the kings from Zimri, Eylam, and Media,
\li1
\v 26 all the kings of the north, both near and far, one after another,
\li1 all the kingdoms around the world.
\p And after all of them, the Babylonian\f + \fr 25:26 \ft ‘Babylon’ here in the Hebrew is ‘Sheshak’, which is Hebrew for ‘Babylon’ with the letters substituted in a simple code.\f* king will drink \add it too\add*.
\p
\v 27 Then Yahweh told me to tell them, ‘Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, says this: Drink and become drunk, then vomit, fall down, and don’t get up \add again\add* until the deadly army arrives that I’m sending among you.’
\v 28 Then if any \add of the above\add* refuse to take the cup from your hand to drink, tell me, ‘Commander-in-chief Yahweh says that you certainly must drink it,
\v 29 because listen, I’m about to bring disaster to the city that is called by my name, and should you yourselves be free from punishment? You won’t be free, because I’m calling a deadly army against all the inhabitants of the land.’
\m That is Commander-in-chief Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 30 You must prophesy all these messages against them, telling them:
\q1 ‘Yahweh will roar from the heights
\q1 and he’ll shout with his voice from his sacred residence,
\q1 and he will roar mightily against his land,
\q1 and he will shout, like those who tread \add grapes in a wine-pressing pit\add*
\q1 against all those who live on the earth.
\q1
\v 31 The uproar will be heard even at the ends of the earth
\q1 because Yahweh brings a charge against the nations.
\q1 He will pass judgement on all humankind
\q1 and have the wicked killed in war.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 32 Commander-in-chief says this:
\q1 Listen, disaster is spreading from nation to nation.
\q1 A terrible storm is rising from the ends of the earth.
\m
\v 33 Then those killed that day by Yahweh will be spread from \add one\add* end of the earth to the other. They won’t be mourned \add over\add*, gathered, or buried—they’ll be like dung lying on the ground.
\p
\v 34 Wail, \add you\add* shepherds, and call out for help.
\q1 Roll around in the dust, \add you\add* leaders of the flock,
\q1 because the time has come for your slaughter.
\q1 You’ll all be scattered when you fall, like \add fragments of broken\add* pottery.
\q1
\v 35 There’s no safe place for the shepherds \add to go to\add*.
\q1 There’ll be no escape for the leaders of the flock.
\q1
\v 36 Hear the shepherds’ cries of distress
\q1 and the wails of the leaders of the flock,
\q1 →because Yahweh is destroying their pastures.
\q1
\v 37 So the peaceful pastures will be devastated
\q1 because of Yahweh’s fierce anger.
\q1
\v 38 He’s left his den like a young lion would,
\q1 because their land will become a horror
\q1 due to the oppressor’s anger and Yahweh’s fierce angry.
\c 26
\s1 Wanting a death sentence for Jeremiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Near the beginning of the reign of Yehoyakim (Josiah’s son), this message came from Yahweh:
\v 2 Yahweh says this: Stand in the courtyard of my temple and speak about all of Judah’s cities who come to worship at my residence. Declare all the messages that I’ve commanded you to say to them—don’t hold anything back.
\v 3 Maybe they’ll listen and everyone will turn from their wicked ways, and then I would turn back the disaster that I’m planning to send to them because of their wicked behaviour,
\v 4 so you \add need to\add* tell them that Yahweh says this:
\m If you don’t listen to me so as to follow my instructions that I’ve given to you all,
\v 5 if you don’t listen to the words of my servants the prophets that I’ve been \add regularly\add* sending to you, and \add indeed\add* you haven’t listened,
\v 6 then I’ll destroy this house like \add I did at\add* Shiloh and I’ll turn this city into a curse to/for all the nations in the world.
\p
\v 7 The priests, the prophets, and all the people heard Jeremiah declaring those words at Yahweh’s temple.
\v 8 Then as soon as Jeremiah had finished saying everything that Yahweh had commanded him to tell all the people, the priests and the prophets and all the people seized him and threatened to kill him, saying:
\v 9 Why have you prophesied in Yahweh’s name and said that this temple will be \add destroyed\add* like Shiloh, and that this city will become desolate, with no inhabitants?” Then all the people gathered together against Jeremiah at Yahweh’s residence.
\p
\v 10 Then Judah’s officials heard about what was going on, and they went up from the palace to the temple where they sat in the gateway at the New gate at Yahweh’s residence.
\v 11 Then the priests and the prophets spoke to the officials and to all the people, saying, “It’s right for this man to die, because he prophesied against this city, as you’ve all heard with your own ears.”
\p
\v 12 So Jeremiah spoke to all the officials and all the people and said, “Yahweh has sent me out to prophesy to say all the words that you have heard, against \add both\add* this temple and this city.
\v 13 So now, improve your ways and your practices, and listen to the voice of your god Yahweh so that he’ll relent concerning the disaster that he’s declared against you all.
\v 14 As for me, here I am in your hands—do to me whatever you think is right and proper.
\v 15 However, you must surely know that if you kill me, then you’re all bringing innocent blood on yourselves and on this city and its inhabitants, because Yahweh has truly sent me to you to declare all these messages for you all to hear.”
\p
\v 16 Then the officials and all the people said to the priests and to the prophets, “This man doesn’t deserve to be sentenced to death, because he’s spoken to us in the name of our god, Yahweh.”
\p
\v 17 Then some of \add Judah’s\add* elders stepped forward and spoke to all the people who were gathered there,
\v 18 “Micah (from Morashet) prophesied during the lifetime of Judah’s King Hezekiah, and he told all the people from Judah that Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 Zion, a field will be ploughed,
\q1 and Jerusalem will become heaps \add of ruins\add*,
\q1 and the temple hill will become an overgrown forest.
\m
\v 19 Did Judah’s King Hezekiah or anyone else in Judah put him to death? Didn’t Hezekiah respect and honour Yahweh and ask for his favour, and didn’t Yahweh relent from sending the disaster that he had pronounced against them? It seems that we’re about to bring great harm on ourselves.”
\p
\v 20 Now there was another man prophesying in Yahweh’s name of Yahweh: Uriah (son of Shemayah from Kiryat-Yearim) who prophesied against this city and against this land with similar messages to Jeremiah.
\v 21 When King Yehoyakim and all his warriors and all the officials heard his words, the king wanted to put him to death. However when Uriah heard about it, he became afraid, and he fled and went to Egypt.
\v 22 However, King Yehoyakim sent Akbor’s son Elnatan and several other men to Egypt \add to get Uriah\add*.
\v 23 They brought Uriah back from Egypt and took him to King Yehoyakim who ran him through with a sword and threw his corpse into the public graveyard.
\p
\v 24 However, Shafan’s son Ahikam supported Jeremiah and didn’t allow the people to kill him.
\c 27
\s1 Jeremiah wears a yoke
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 At the beginning of the reign of Judah’s King Yehoyakim (son of Josiah \add Josiah\add*), this message came to Jeremiah from Yahweh,
\v 2 telling him this:
\m Make yourself a \add wooden\add* \add ox\add* yoke with bars, and tie it to your neck.
\v 3 Use it to send messages to the kings of Edom, Moab, Amon, Tyre, and Sidon, through the ambassadors \add from those countries\add* who’ve come to Jerusalem to \add meet with\add* King Zedekiah
\v 4 to tell them that Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says to give their masters this message:
\v 5 I myself made the earth \add and\add* the people and animals that are on the earth, by my direct actions and my great strength, and I give it to whoever I see fit.
\v 6 Now I’ve personally handed over all these lands to my servant, the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar, and I’ve also given the animals in the countryside to him to serve him.
\v 7 All the nations will serve him and his son and then his grandson, until the time of his own land comes \add due\add*. Yes, it will, and then many nations and great kings will make him serve them.
\p
\v 8 Any nation or kingdom that won’t serve the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar and that won’t put its neck into his yoke, then I’ll punish that nation with war and famine and disease until I’ve used King Nebuchadnezzar to finish them \add off\add*. That is Yahweh’s declaration,
\v 9 so don’t you all keep listening to your ‘prophets’, and to your diviners and dreamers, and to your soothsayers and sorcerers—all those who keep telling you that you all won’t serve the Babylonian king,
\v 10 because they’re prophesying lies to you, so as to remove you all far away from on your land. Yes, I will drive you all out and you’ll die \add in a foreign land\add*.
\v 11 However, the nation that brings its neck into the Babylonian king’s yoke and serves him, I’ll let that nation remain on its land, and it will cultivate it and live there. That’s is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 12 Then to Judah’s King Zedekiah, I passed on those \add same\add* words, saying, “Put your necks into the yoke of the Babylonian king, and serve him and his people, and live.
\v 13 Why would you and your people die from war, famine, and disease, as Yahweh has said will come to any nation that doesn’t serve the Babylonian king?
\v 14 So don’t listen to the messages from the prophets who are speaking to you when they tell you all not to serve the king of Babylon as they’re prophesying lies to you
\v 15 because I didn’t send them. Yahweh declares that they’re using my name to prophesy lies so that I’ll drive you out, and you’ll all perish—\add both\add* you and the prophets who are prophesying to you.”
\v 16 Then I told the priests and all those people that Yahweh says: Don’t listen to the words of your prophets who are prophesying to you and saying that the utensils from Yahweh’s temple will soon be brought back from Babylon, because they’re prophesying lies to you.
\v 17 Don’t listen to them. Serve the Babylonian king and live. Why should this city become a ruin?
\v 18 If they were prophets who really have access to Yahweh’s message, then please let them intercede with Commander-in-chief Yahweh so that the utensils remaining in Yahweh’s temple and in Judah’s king’s palace and in Jerusalem don’t end up being taken to Babylon.
\p
\v 19 Yes, Commander-in-chief Yahweh has spoken about the \add bronze\add* pillars and the \add large, bronze\add* water basin, and concerning the \add bronze\add* stands and the rest of the utensils remaining in this city,
\v 20 which the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar didn’t take when he exiled Judah’s King Jeconiah (son of Yehoyakim) from Jerusalem to Babylon, along with all the leading citizens of Judah including Jerusalem.
\v 21 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says about the utensils remaining in Yahweh’s temple and in the palace of the king of Judah and Jerusalem:
\v 22 They’ll be taken to Babylon and they’ll remain there until the time when I punish those Babylonians, and then I’ll bring them back and restore them to this place. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\c 28
\s1 Jeremiah vs Prophet Hananyah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 In the fifth month of the fourth year of the reign of Judah’s King Zedekiah, Azur’s son Hananyah who was a ‘prophet’ from Gibeon, said to me in Yahweh’s temple as priests and all the people \add watched\add*, saying,
\v 2 “Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says: I’ve broken the yoke of the Babylonian king.
\v 3 Within two years, I, myself, will bring back here all the utensils from Yahweh’s residence that the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar got from this place and took to Babylon.
\v 4 I, myself, will also bring Jeconiah (the son of Judah’s King Yehoyakim) back to this place, and all the exiles from Judah that were taken to Babylon, because I’ll break the yoke of the Babylonian king. That is Yahweh’s declaration.”
\p
\v 5 Then the prophet Jeremiah spoke to the prophet Hananyah, as the priests and all the people standing in Yahweh’s temple watched,
\v 6 saying, “Let it be so—may Yahweh do all that. May Yahweh confirm your messages that you prophesied, to bring the temple utensils back to this place, along with all the exiles from Babylon.
\v 7 However, please listen to this message that I, myself, am speaking as you and all the people listen:
\v 8 The prophets who preceded \add both\add* me and you, prophesied long ago that many nations and powerful kingdoms would experience wars, terrible suffering, and deadly diseases.
\v 9 The prophet who prophesies about peace, that prophet will be known as ‘a truthful prophet sent by Yahweh’ \add only\add* when his message comes \add to pass\add*.”
\p
\v 10 Then the prophet Hananyah took the yoke bar off the prophet Jeremiah’s neck, and broke it.
\v 11 Then as all the people watched, he said, “This is what Yahweh says: Just like that, I’ll break the yoke of the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar off the neck of all the nations within two more years.”
\p Then the prophet Jeremiah left the gathering.
\p
\v 12 After the prophet Hananyah broke the yoke bar that had been on the prophet Jeremiah’s neck, Yahweh’s message came to Jeremiah saying,
\v 13 “Go and tell Hananyah this: ‘Yahweh says that you’ve broken wooden yoke bars, but in their place, you’ll make yoke bars out of iron,’
\v 14 because Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says, ‘I’ve put an iron yoke bar on the neck of all these nations to serve the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar, and they will \add indeed\add* serve him. I’ve also given him the animals from the countryside.’ ”
\p
\v 15 Then the prophet Jeremiah told the prophet Hananyah, “Listen, please, Hananyah. Yahweh hasn’t sent you, and you yourself have made this people \add group\add* put their trust in things that aren’t true.
\v 16 Because of that, Yahweh says this: ‘Listen, I’m removing you from this world. You’ll die this very year, because you have said things that would cause the people to rebel against Yahweh.’ ”
\p
\v 17 The prophet Hananyah then died in the seventh month of that year.
\c 29
\s1 Jeremiah’s letter to the exiled Jews
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 The prophet Jeremiah sent a letter from Jerusalem to the remainder of the elders of the exile, and to the priests, and to the prophets, and to all the people that Nebuchadnezzar had exiled from Jerusalem to Babylon
\v 2 after the removal from Jerusalem of King Jeconiah and the queen mother, the court officials and the leaders of Judah and Jerusalem, plus the craftsmen and the metalsmiths.
\v 3 \add He sent it\add* with Shafan’s son Eleasah and Hilkiyah’s son Gemaryah, who Judah’s King Zedekiah sent to the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar in Babylon. \add The letter\add* said,
\pi1
\v 4 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this to all of you that I’ve exiled from Jerusalem to Babylon:
\v 5 Build houses and live in them, and plant gardens and eat their fruit.
\v 6 Marry and have children. Find wives for your sons and give your daughters in marriage so that they \add also\add* have children. Increase in number there—don’t become fewer.
\v 7 In addition, the city that I’ve sent you all into exile to, work to make it more prosperous and pray to Yahweh for it, because if it prospers, its prosperity will belong to all of you.
\v 8 That’s because Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: Don’t let your prophets and diviners who are among you deceive you, and don’t listen to your dreams that you \add wishfully\add* dream,
\v 9 because those prophets are prophesying using my name but not being truthful—I didn’t send them. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 10 “Because this is what Yahweh says: When seventy years have passed for Babylon, I’ll visit take notice of you all \add again\add*, and I’ll implement my good message about you—to bring you all back to this place,
\v 11 because I know the plans that I have for you all: plans for peaceful well-being—not to bring you all harm, \add but rather\add* a future with hope. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 12 Then you’ll all call out to me \add for help\add*, and you’ll come and pray to me, and I’ll listen to you all.
\v 13 You’ll search for me, and you’ll find me, because you’ll all want to follow me with complete sincerity.
\v 14 Then, Yahweh has declared, I’ll let myself be found by you all, and I’ll bring you back from captivity. I’ll gather you all from all the nations and all the places that I banished you to, and I’ll bring you all back to the place that I sent you into exile from. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\pi1
\v 15 Because you’ve all said that Yahweh has appointed prophets for you \add there\add* in Babylon,
\v 16 then Yahweh has something to say about the king sitting on David’s throne and about all the people living in this city—your relatives who didn’t go into exile with you.
\v 17 So Commander-in-chief Yahweh says: Listen, I’m about to send war, famine and deadly diseases against them. I’ll make them like rotten figs that are so bad they’re inedible.
\v 18 Yes, I’ll chase after them with war, famine, and deadly diseases, and I’ll make them become a horror to all the world’s kingdoms—a curse and a desolation, and a reproach and something to be hissed at by all the nations where I’ve driven them,
\v 19 because they didn’t listen to my messages that I sent to them over and over again via my servants the prophets, but all you \add exiles\add* didn’t listen. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 20 But all you exiles that I sent away from Jerusalem to Babylon, listen to Yahweh’s message:
\pi1
\v 21 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, says this about Kolayah’s son Ahab and Ma’aseyah’s son Zedekiah, who are \add both\add* prophesying untruths to you in my name: Listen, I’m handing them over to the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar, and he’ll kill them right in front of you.
\v 22 Then because of them, a \add new\add* curse \add phrase\add* will be taken on by all the Jewish exiles in Babylon, which goes, “May Yahweh make you like Zedekiah and Ahab, who were roasted in the fire by the Babylonian king,”
\v 23 because they did something shameful in Israel and they have committed adultery with the wives of their neighbours, and they spoke untrue messages in my name that I hadn’t commanded them to. I am the one who knows that and I’m a witness. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 Message to false prophet Shemayah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 24 \add Yahweh told me to\add* tell Shemayah (the Nehelami) this:
\v 25 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: Because you sent letters in your name to all the people in Jerusalem, and to the priest Zephaniah Ma’aseyah’s son), and to all the priests, to tell Zephaniah:
\pi1
\v 26 Yahweh has made you, \add Zephaniah,\add* priest in place of the priest Yehoyada, to be overseer in Yahweh’s temple over every man who raves \add out loud\add*, making himself a prophet, and you must put him in the stocks with an iron collar around his neck.
\v 27 So why haven’t you rebuked Jeremiah the Antoti who’s prophesying to you all,
\v 28 because he’s sent \add a letter\add* to us \add here\add* in Babylon saying, “Your exile will last a long time. Build houses and live in them. Plant gardens and eat their fruit.”
\p
\v 29 The priest Zephaniah read that letter to the prophet Jeremiah,
\v 30 then Yahweh gave this message to Jeremiah:
\v 31 Send to all the exiles to tell them: This is what Yahweh says about Shemayah the Nehelami: Because Shemayah has prophesied to you all, even though I didn’t send him, and he made you trust what’s not true,
\v 32 therefore Yahweh says this: Listen, I will punish Shemayah the Nehelami and his descendants. None of his family will live among this people, and he won’t experience the good things that I’ll do for my people, because he’s encouraged rebellion against Yahweh. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\c 30
\s1 Yahweh’s promises to his people
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave Jeremiah a message, saying:
\v 2 Israel’s god, Yahweh says this: Write down on a scroll all the messages that I’ve given you,
\v 3 because listen to Yahweh’s declaration: The time is coming when I’ll restore my people of Israel and Judah from captivity, and I’ll bring them back to the land that I have to their ancestors, and they’ll take possession of it \add again\add*.
\p
\v 4 Now these are the messages that Yahweh spoke concerning Israel and Judah:
\q1
\v 5 Yes, Yahweh says this:
\q1 A cry of panic and terror has been heard,
\q1 and there’s no peace.
\q1
\v 6 Please ask and find out: can males give birth?
\q1 Why do I see every strong man with his hands on his hips like a woman in labour?
\q1 And why has everyone’s faces gone so pale?
\q1
\v 7 Oh dear, that will be a terrible day like nothing that’s come before.
\q1 It’ll be a distressing time for Jacob,
\q1 →but he’ll be rescued from it.
\p
\v 8 Then Commander-in-chief Yahweh declares that at that time I’ll break his yoke off your neck, and I’ll pull your chains apart, and foreigners will no longer enslave your country.
\v 9 Then they’ll serve their god Yahweh, and their king \add on David’s throne\add* who I’ll appoint for them.
\p
\v 10 Then Yahweh declares:
\q1 Then you, my servant Jacob, don’t be afraid,
\q1 and don’t be dismayed, Israel,
\q1 because listen, I’m rescuing you from afar,
\q1 and your descendants from the land where they were held captive.
\q1 Israel will return and be left alone and at ease,
\q1 and there won’t be anyone making it afraid.
\q1
\v 11 Yahweh declares:
\q1 Yes, I’m with you to rescue you,
\q1 because I’ll completely destroy all the nations that I’ve scattered you to,
\q1 ^but I won’t completely destroy you.
\q1 I’ll discipline you according to justice,
\q1 but I won’t just let you off what you deserve.
\p
\v 12 Yes, Yahweh says this:
\q1 Your fracture is incurable.
\q1 Your wound is severe.
\q1
\v 13 There’s no one pleading your cause.
\q1 There’s no medicines for your wound.
\q1 There’s no healing for you.
\q1
\v 14 All your lovers have forgotten you—
\q1 they don’t care about you.
\q1 Yes, I’ve struck you like an enemy would,
\q1 with the discipline of a cruel person,
\q1 due to the severity of your wickedness.
\q1 You’ve disobeyed so frequently.
\q1
\v 15 Why do you cry out about your fracture—
\q1 about your incurable pain?
\q1 Your wickedness has been severe.
\q1 You’ve disobeyed so often
\q1 →\add so\add* I’ve done those things to you.
\q1
\v 16 Because of that, all those who devoured you will be devoured,
\q1 and all of your adversaries, every one of them, will go into captivity.
\q1 →Then the ones plundering you will become like plunder,
\q1 and all those preying on you, I’ll give to you as spoil.
\q1
\v 17 Yes, I’ll bring healing to you,
\q1 and I’ll heal you from your wounds
\q1 because they’ve called you an outcast.
\q1 ‘She is Zion—no one cares about her.’
\q1 That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 18 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, I’m restoring the fortune of Jacob’s descendants,
\q1 and I will have compassion on the places where they live.
\q1 Each city will be rebuilt on its mound,
\q1 and each fortress will sit on its proper place.
\q1
\v 19 Then thanksgiving will go out from them,
\q1 and the sound of those laughing.
\q1 I’ll multiply them, and they won’t diminish \add in number\add*,
\q1 →and I’ll make them honoured—
\q1 they won’t become insignificant.
\q1
\v 20 Then its children will be as in former times,
\q1 and its community will be established under my supervision,
\q1 and I’ll punish all those who oppress it.
\q1
\v 21 Its king will be from among it,
\q1 and its ruler will come from its middle.
\q1 Then I’ll bring it near, and it will approach me,
\q1 because who is this that has pledged its heart to approach me?
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 22 You’ll be my people
\q1 and I’ll become your god.
\p
\v 23 Listen, Yahweh’s storm of rage has gone out—
\q1 a storm whirling around the heads of wicked \add people\add*.
\q1
\v 24 Yahweh’s burning anger won’t turn back
\q1 until he’s established and completed what he’s planned.
\q1 You’ll all understand it in the days to come.
\c 31
\s1 Israel’s return home
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 1 Yahweh declares that at that time, I’ll be \add the\add* god to all of Israel’s families, and they’ll be my people.
\v 2 Yahweh says this:
\q1 A people, the survivors after being attacked in war,
\q1 found favour in the wilderness,
\q1 Israel going to find a peaceful place.
\q1
\v 3 From far off, Yahweh appeared to me: I’ve always loved you
\q1 →therefore I’ve displayed loyal commitment to help you endure.
\q1
\v 4 I’ll rebuild you, and you’ll be restored, Israel’s pretty daughter.
\q1 Again you’ll adorn yourself with your tambourines,
\q1 and will go out in the dance of those celebrating.
\q1
\v 5 You’ll plant vineyards again on the Samaria hills.
\q1 The planters will plant and make them productive.
\q1
\v 6 Yes, there’ll be a day when the watchers in the Ephraim hill country will call out,
\q1 ‘Get ready and let’s go up to our god Yahweh at Zion,’
\q1
\v 7 because Yahweh says this:
\q1 “Sing for \add Israel\add* Jacob with gladness,
\q1 and shout for this leader of the nations.
\q1 Cause it to be heard, praise, and say,
\q1 ‘Yahweh, save your people, the remnant of Israel.’
\q1
\v 8 Listen, I’m bringing them \add back\add* from the land in the north,
\q1 and I’ll gather them from the remote parts of the world.
\q1 Among them are the blind and the lame,
\q1 and pregnant women together with those giving birth.
\q1 A large number of people will return here.
\q1
\v 9 They’ll be weeping as they come,
\q1 and as they request me, I’ll bring them \add out\add*.
\q1 I’ll cause them to walk to streams of water on a straight road.
\q1 They won’t stumble on it.
\q1 because I’ve become a father for Israel,
\q1 and Israel, he’s my firstborn.
\p
\v 10 Listen to Yahweh’s message, \add you\add* nations,
\q1 and declare it in the coastlands from far off, and say,
\q1 ‘The one scattering Israel will gather him \add back\add*,
\q1 and he’ll protect him like a shepherd of his flock,
\q1
\v 11 because Yahweh has ransomed \add Israel\add* (Jacob
\q1 and paid the price to free it from the power of a \add nation that’s\add* stronger than it.
\q1
\v 12 Then they’ll come and sing on Zion’s heights,
\q1 and they’ll be radiant about Yahweh’s goodness:
\q1 about the grain, about the new wine and the fresh oil,
\q1 and about the young from the flock and the herd.
\q1 Their soul will be like a watered garden,
\q1 and they won’t be weakened again.
\q1
\v 13 Then the young women will celebrate with dancing,
\q1 along with the young and old men together.
\q1 I’ll turn their mourning into happiness and I’ll comfort them,
\q1 and I’ll give them reason for celebration rather than sorrow.
\q1
\v 14 I’ll satisfy the priests’ appetites for good food
\q1 and my people will be pleased by my goodness.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 Yahweh’s mercy towards Israel
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 15 Yahweh says this,
\q1 “A voice is heard in Ramah, wailing,
\q1 bitter weeping.
\q1 Rachel is weeping over her sons.
\q1 She refuses to be comforted over her sons,
\q1 because he is no more.”
\p
\v 16 Yahweh says this,
\q1 “Don’t cry any more,
\q1 and keep the tears out of your eyes,
\q1 →because there’s a reward for your work,
\q1 →and they’ll return from the enemy’s country.”
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 17 There’s hope for your future,
\q1 and your children will return to their border.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 18 I’ve heard Israel upset at itself, \add saying\add*,
\q1 You disciplined me,
\q1 and I was disciplined like an untrained calf.
\q1 Restore me so I can return
\q1 because you’re my god Yahweh.
\q1
\v 19 Yes, after I turned away, I repented,
\q1 and after I was instructed, I slapped my thigh.
\q1 I was ashamed and also humiliated
\q1 because I \add still\add* bore the reproach from my youthful \add rebellion\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 20 Isn’t Israel a precious son to me,
\q1 or a child that I really liked?
\q1 Yes, as often as I speak against him,
\q1 I still continually remember him.
\q1 →Therefore I groan deep down for him.
\q1 I’ll always feel compassionate towards him.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 21 Set signposts up for yourself.
\q1 Place markers where you can see them.
\q1 Set the highway as your goal—
\q1 the path that you went on.
\q1 Israel’s young daughter, return—
\q1 return to those cities of yours.
\q1
\v 22 How much longer will you waver, \add you\add* apostate daughter?
\q1 Yes, Yahweh has created something new in the land—
\q1 a woman protects\f + \fr 31:22 \ft Heb. ‘surrounds’—the intended meaning of this last line isn’t certain.\f* a man.
\s1 Israel’s future peace/prosperity
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 23 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 When I restore the prosperity of Judah and its cities,
\q1 again they’ll \add restore\add* this saying,
\q1 ‘May Yahweh bless you—
\q1 Yahweh who’s the repository of righteousness—
\q1 mountain of holiness.’
\q1
\v 24 Then Judah and all its cities will live in the land together—
\q1 farmers, and \add also\add* shepherds who roam around with their flocks,
\q1
\v 25 because I satisfy those who are weary,
\q1 and I refresh everyone who’s become faint.
\p
\v 26 After that, I woke up and looked around,
\q1 and I’d had a good sleep.
\p
\v 27 Yahweh declares:
\q1 Listen, there’s a time coming when I’ll sow the Israel and Judah regions with the seeds of people and animals \add so they’ll multiply all around\add*.
\q1
\v 28 Just like how I supervised them to uproot and tear down,
\q1 and to overthrow and destroy and do harm,
\q1 now I’ll keep watch as they build and plant.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 29 Then at that time, they’ll no longer say:
\q1 ‘The parents ate sour grapes,
\q1 →but it’s the teeth of the children that are set on edge.’
\q1
\v 30 because everyone will die for their own disobedience,
\q1 i.e., whoever eats sour grapes will have their own teeth set on edge.
\p
\v 31 Listen, Yahweh declares that the time is coming when I’ll make a new agreement with the people of Israel and Judah.
\v 32 It won’t be like the agreement that I made with their ancestors at the time when I took hold of their hand to bring them out from Egypt, when they broke my agreement even though I was a husband to them. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 33 Yes, Yahweh declares that this is the agreement that I’ll make with the Israelite people after that time: I’ll put my law inside them—writing it on their heart, and I will be their god, and they will be a people for me.
\v 34 Then each man will no longer teach his neighbour or his brother, saying, ‘Know Yahweh’, because all of them will know me—from the smallest of them to the greatest, because I’ll forgive their disobedience, and I won’t remember their wickedness any longer.
\p
\v 35 Yahweh says this:
\q1 \add I’m\add* the one who gives the sun for light during the day,
\q1 and the moon and the stars for light at night.
\q1 He stirs up the ocean, so that its waves roar,
\q1 His name is Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\q1
\v 36 Yahweh declares that if these elements stopped working,
\q1 →then Israel would also permanently cease to exist as a nation.
\p
\v 37 Yahweh says this:
\q1 If the skies above could be measured and be searched out,
\q1 as well as the foundations of the earth below,
\q1 →then I would also reject all of Israel’s descendants
\q2 for everything that they’ve done.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 38 Listen, Yahweh declares that the time is coming when the city will be \add re\add*built for Yahweh—from Hananel’s tower \add all the way\add* to the Corner gate.
\v 39 After that, the measuring line will be stretched over Garev hill and around to Go’ah.
\v 40 Then all the valley of the corpses and ashes, and all the fields as far as the Kidron brook, to the corner of the Horses gate toward the east, will be holiness to Yahweh. It won’t be uprooted, and it’ll never be overthrown again.
\c 32
\s1 Jeremiah buys a field
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 This was the message that came to Jeremiah from Yahweh in the tenth year of \add the reign of\add* Judah’s King Zedekiah, the eighteenth year of Nebuchadnezzar’s \add reign\add*.
\v 2 The army of the Babylonian king had besieged Jerusalem, and the prophet Jeremiah was imprisoned in the guards’ courtyard at the palace of Judah’s king.
\v 3 Judah’s king Zedekiah had imprisoned him \add there after asking\add*, “Why do you prophesy and say that Yahweh says: Listen, I’m about to hand this city over to the Babylonian king and he’ll capture it.
\v 4 Judah’s King Zedekiah definitely won’t escape from the power of the Chaldeans, because he’ll be handed over to the Babylonian king and they’ll speak face-to-face to each other.
\v 5 He’ll take Zedekiah to Babylon where he’ll remain until I’ve dealt with him. Thebes, you all won’t succeed in fighting against the Babylonians. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 6 Then Jeremiah said: Yahweh gave me a message, saying:
\v 7 Listen, your \add cousin\add* Hanamel (your uncle Shalum’s son) is coming to \add see\add* you and will ask you to buy his field in Anatot, because you have the right to buy it.
\v 8 Then just as Yahweh had said, my cousin Hanamel came to me in the guards’ courtyard and he asked me, “Buy my field that is in Anatot in the Benjamin region, because you have the right to inherit it so the right to buy it belongs to you. Buy it for yourself.” Then I knew that this was Yahweh’s message.
\v 9 So I bought the field in Anatot from my cousin Hanamel, and I weighed out seventeen shekels of silver for him.
\v 10 Then I wrote in a scroll and sealed it, and had witnesses witness it and the weight of the silver on the scales.
\v 11 Next I took that sealed purchase deed with the price and conditions, as well as an unsealed copy.
\v 12 I gave the \add sealed\add* purchase document to Baruk (son of Neriyah, son of Mahseyah) in front of my cousin Hanamel and the witnesses who’d signed the purchase agreement, as well as all the Jews who were sitting in the guards’ courtyard.
\v 13 Then \add still\add* in front of them, I instructed Baruk,
\v 14 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: Take these documents, both this receipt of purchase that’s sealed and the unsealed copies, and put them into a clay jar so they’ll last for a long time,
\v 15 because Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: Houses, fields, and vineyards will again be bought in this land.
\s1 Jeremiah’s prayer
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 16 After I’d given the purchase deed to Neriyah’s son Baruk, I prayed to Yahweh, saying:
\v 17 Oh no, master Yahweh. Listen, you alone made the heavens and the earth by taking action and using your incredible strength. Nothing you say is too difficult for you to do.
\v 18 You show loyal commitment to thousands, and pour people’s guilt into their children’s laps after them. You are the strong and powerful god—your name is Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\v 19 You are very wise and do incredible things, because your eyes are open to all the people’s ways—to give each person what their conduct and actions deserve.
\v 20 You did miracles and amazing things in Egypt, \add remembered\add* to this day, and you’ve made a name for yourself in Israel and among humankind.
\v 21 You brought your people Israel out from Egypt using miracles and amazing things and by taking action and using your incredible strength, along with great terror.
\v 22 Then you gave them this land which you had promised their ancestors that you’d give them—a land that’s very fertile.
\v 23 So they entered and took possession of it, but they didn’t listen to your voice or follow your instructions. They didn’t do anything that you’d ordered them to do so you’ve brought all this calamity onto them.
\p
\v 24 Look, the siege mounds have reached up to the city to capture it, and it’s been handed over to the Chaldeans who’ve been fighting it. Everything you said would happen has, including war, famine, and deadly diseases, and you’re there watching it.
\v 25 Then you yourself told me, ‘Use silver to buy yourself a field, and have witnesses witness it, even though this city is being handed over to the Chaldeans.’
\p
\v 26 Then Yahweh gave Jeremiah this message:
\v 27 Listen, I’m Yahweh, the god of all mankind. Is there anything that’s too difficult for me to do?
\v 28 Therefore Yahweh says this, ‘Listen, I’m about to hand this city over to the Babylonians—the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar will capture it.
\v 29 The soldiers who are fighting against this city will come and set fire to this city and burn it. Those people who worship Baal on their \add flat\add* roofs, and who poured out drink offerings to other gods in order to provoke me, will \add now\add* have their houses burnt down
\v 30 because the people of Israel and Judah have certainly been people who’ve been doing evil in front of me since their youth. The people of Israel have certainly offended me with the practices and activities. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 31 Yahweh declares that \add the people of\add* this city has provoked my anger and fury since the day that they built it, and then right up to the present day, so I’ll will remove it \add right\add* out of my sight,
\v 32 because of all the wicked things that the people of Israel and Judah have done to provoke me—their kings, princes, priests, prophets, and every person in Judah including Jerusalem’s inhabitants.
\v 33 They turned their backs to me instead of their faces, even though I had eagerly taught them. I tried to teach them, but they wouldn’t listen or receive correction.
\v 34 They set up their detestable idols in the house that is called by my name, \add in order\add* to defile it,
\v 35 and they built hilltop shrines for Baal along the Ben-Hinom valley in order to pass their sons and daughters through the fire for Molech. I didn’t command them \add to do that\add*—it never entered my mind that they should do that detestable thing and so cause Judah to sin.’
\s1 Promised restoration
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 36 So now Israel’s god Yahweh has something to say about this city that you’ve all been talking about, saying, ‘It’s been handed over to the Babylonian king \add due to\add* war, famine, and deadly diseases.’
\v 37 Listen, I’ll gather them \add back\add* from every land where I had banished them to in my rage, fury, and great anger. I’m about to bring them back to this place and enable them to live in security,
\v 38 and then they’ll be my people and I’ll be their god.
\v 39 I’ll give them a united vision and a single way to honour me every day so that it’ll \add turn out\add* good for them and for their descendants after them.
\v 40 Then I’ll make a permanent agreement with them, that I won’t turn away from doing good to them. I’ll set honour for me in their minds, so that they’ll never turn away from me.
\v 41 Then I’ll take pleasure in doing good to them. I’ll faithfully plant them in this land with all my heart and all my life
\v 42 because Yahweh says this, ‘Just as I have brought all this great disaster on this people, so too I’ll bring on them all the good things that I’ve said I’d do for them.
\v 43 Then \add people\add* will buy fields in this land, about which you’ve been saying, “This land is ruined, without people or \add even\add* animals \add living\add* on it. It’s been handed over to the Babylonians.”
\v 44 They’ll use silver to buy fields and will write in sealed scrolls. They’ll assemble witnesses in the Benjamin region, all around Jerusalem and Judah’s \add other\add* cities of Judah, in the cities in the hill country and in the lowlands, and in the cities of the Negev, because I’ll bring back their fortunes. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\c 33
\s1 Promised blessings
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then while Jeremiah was still locked in the guards’ courtyard, Yahweh gave him a second message, saying,
\v 2 Yahweh, the maker \add of the earth\add*, who formed and established it and \add who’s known by\add* his name Yahweh, says:
\v 3 Call \add out\add* to me, and I’ll answer you. I’ll tell you amazing things that you haven’t \add previously\add* known or understood.
\v 4 Yes, Israel’s god, Yahweh, says this about the houses in this city and the palaces of Judah’s king that were torn down \add to be used against\add* the siege ramps and the attackers:
\v 5 The Babylonians are coming to fight, and to fill the houses with the corpses of the people that I’ll kill in my rage and fury when I turn my back on this city because of all their wickedness.
\v 6 Listen, I’m about to bring a cure and healing, because I’ll heal them and will bring bountiful peace and prosperity to them, and justice.
\v 7 I’ll bring back the fortunes of Judah and Israel—I’ll build them up as they were before.
\v 8 Then I’ll purify them from all the evil that they’ve committed against me—I’ll pardon all the times that they disobeyed me, and all the ways that they rebelled against me.
\v 9 Then this city will become \add an object\add* of joy for me—a song of praise and honour for all the nations of the earth who’ll hear about all the good things that I’m going to do for it. Then the people in the city will be respectful and tremble because of all the good things and the peace and prosperity that I’ll give to it.
\p
\v 10 Yahweh says this: In this place that you’re all talking about, saying, “It’s desolate—a place where neither people or animals \add live\add*,” in Judah’s cities including Jerusalem’s streets that are \add indeed\add* desolate with no people or animals around, there’ll be heard again
\v 11 the sounds of happiness and celebration. You’ll all hear the sounds of the bride and groom, and those who bring thank offerings to Yahweh’s temple will be heard saying,
\q1 “Give thanks to Commander-in-chief Yahweh,
\q1 because Yahweh is good,
\q1 and his loyal commitment lasts forever!”
\m Yes, I’ll restore the fortunes of the land to what they were before, says Yahweh.
\p
\v 12 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says: ‘In this desolate place where no people or animals \add are living\add*, around all its cities there’ll again be pastures where shepherds can rest their flocks.
\v 13 In the cities in the hill country, the lowlands, and the Negev, in the Benjamin \add region\add*, and all around Jerusalem and in Judah’s \add other\add* cities, the flocks will again pass be counted by their shepherds,’ says Yahweh.
\p
\v 14 Yahweh declares: Listen, the time is coming when I’ll do what I’ve promised for the people of Israel and Judah.
\v 15 In those days and at that time, I’ll make a righteous branch grow for David, and he’ll do what’s fair and right throughout the land.
\v 16 Judah will be rescued in those days, and Jerusalem will live in security, because it’ll be called, ‘Yahweh is the one who does what’s right for us,’
\v 17 because Yahweh says this: ‘There’ll always be one of David’s descendants reigning over Israel,
\v 18 and there’ll always be a Levitical priest to stand in front of me to sacrifice burnt offerings, to burn grain offerings, and to offer other sacrifices.’
\p
\v 19 Then Yahweh gave Jeremiah this message:
\v 20 Yahweh says this: ‘If you all can break my contract with day and night so that there’ll no longer be day or night at their proper times,
\v 21 then my agreement with my servant David my servant could \add also\add* be broken, so that he would no longer have a descendant to sit on his throne. The same is true for my agreement with my servants, the priests from Levi tribe.
\v 22 As the stars in the skies can’t be counted, and as the sand \add grains\add* on the seashores can’t be measured, so I’ll increase the descendants of my servant David, as well as the Levites who work for me.’
\p
\v 23 Yahweh gave Jeremiah this message:
\v 24 Haven’t you noticed what this people \add group\add* has declared when they said, ‘The two clans that Yahweh chose, now he’s rejected them’? In that way they despise my people, saying that they no longer deserve to be a nation as far as they’re concerned.
\v 25 \add But\add* I, Yahweh say this: Didn’t I establish the \add permanent\add* contract with day and night, and determine the laws of the universe and this planet?
\v 26 In the same way, would I \add now\add* reject Jacob’s descendants and not bring a ruler for the descendants of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob out of them. \add Thebes,\add* because I’ll restore their fortunes and show mercy towards them.
\c 34
\s1 The warning for Zedekiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave this message to Jeremiah when the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar, along with all the world’s kingdoms, domains, and people \add groups\add* under his control, was attacking Jerusalem and Judah’s other cities, saying:
\v 2 Israel’s god Yahweh says to go and speak to Judah’s King Zedekiah and tell him that Yahweh says this: Listen, I’m about to hand this city over to the Babylonian king, and he’ll set it on fire.
\v 3 You won’t escape from his power, because you’ll definitely be captured and handed over to him and you’ll talk face-to-face with the king of Babylon as you’re taken to Babylon.
\v 4 However King Zedekiah, listen to Yahweh’s message—you won’t be killed by being run through with a sword
\v 5 \add but actually\add* you’ll die in peace. They’ll cremate your body as was done for your ancestors, the previous kings, and they’ll mourn for you \add saying\add*, ‘Oh no, master!’ That’s the promise that Yahweh has declared.
\v 6 So the prophet Jeremiah passed on all those messages to Judah’s King Zedekiah in Jerusalem
\v 7 when the Babylonian king’s army had attacked Jerusalem and Judah’s remaining fortified cities of Lakish and Azekah.
\s1 The restoration of slaves
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 8 Yahweh gave a message to Jeremiah after King Zedekiah had made an agreement with all the people in Jerusalem that they’d free
\v 9 their male and female Hebrew slaves—no one should force a fellow Jew to work for them.
\v 10 So all the leaders and people formally agreed that each person would free their male and female slaves, and they put it into practice—taking them from their work and setting them free.
\v 11 However, after that they changed their minds and retrieved the male and female slaves that they’d freed, then forced them to work as slaves again.
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh gave me this message to tell them:
\v 13 Israel’s god Yahweh says that I personally made an agreement with your ancestors on the day that I brought them out from their lives of slavery in Egypt. That was when I said that
\v 14 at the end of every seven years, you all must release any fellow Hebrews who had sold themselves to you and served you for six years, releasing them as free people. However, your ancestors did not listen to me or show any desire to obey me.
\v 15 Now \add recently,\add* you yourselves repented and began to do what I’ve said is right. You all made a solemn agreement at my temple that carries my name, that you’d monitor each other \add to free your slaves\add*.
\v 16 \add But\add* then you all turned and profaned my name by retrieving your male and female slaves which you’d released as free people, and then forced them to work as slaves again,
\v 17 therefore Yahweh says this: You yourselves haven’t listened to me—you all should have declared your fellow Israelites to be free. \add So\add* listen, I’m about to declare ‘freedom’ for you: freedom to experience war, deadly diseases, and famine, because I’m going to make you a horrible thing in the sight of every kingdom in the world. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 18 Then I’ll deal with the people who’ve broken my agreement, who didn’t respect the intent of the agreement that they established in front of me when they cut a bull in two and walked between the two halves,
\v 19 when the leaders of Judah and Jerusalem, the officials and the priests, and all the people of the land walked between the \add two\add* sections of the bull.
\v 20 I’ll hand them over to their enemies and to those who want to see them dead. Their bodies will be food for the birds in the skies and the animals on the ground.
\v 21 I’ll hand Judah’s King Zedekiah and his leaders over to their enemies and to those who want to see them dead, and to the army of the Babylonian king who’s attacking you.
\v 22 This is Yahweh’s declaration: Listen, I’m about to order them back to battle against this city, and they’ll capture it and set it on fire. Then I’ll make Judah’s \add other\add* cities into deserted wastelands.
\c 35
\s1 Praising the faithful Rekavites
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add Several years earlier,\add* when Yehoyakim son of Josiah was the king of Judah, Yahweh had given Jeremiah this message:
\v 2 Go to the Rekavi clan and talk with them, then bring them into one of the rooms in my temple, and give them wine to drink.
\v 3 So I took Ya’azanyah (son of Jeremiah, son of Havatsanyah) and his brothers and his sons, and all the \add rest of\add* the Rekavi clan,
\v 4 and I took them to the rooms of the sons of Hanan (son of Yigdalyah, man of God) at the temple. Those rooms were beside the leaders’ room, which was above the room of Ma’aseyah (Shalum’s son), the gatekeeper.
\v 5 Then I placed bowls and cups of wine in front of the Rekavites and told them to drink some.
\p
\v 6 But they responded, “We don’t drink wine, because our ancestor, Rekav’s son Yonadav, commanded \add all of\add* us and our descendants not to ever drink any wine.
\v 7 He also told us not to build any houses, or to plant any crops or vineyards, \add saying\add* that we must always live in tents, so that we’d have long lives in the land that we’re staying in \add as foreigners\add*.
\v 8 We’ve obeyed the voice of our ancestor, Rekav’s son Yonadav in everything that he commanded us \add and\add* we, our wives, and our children have never drunk wine in our lives.
\v 9 We’ll never build houses to live in, or own any vineyards, fields, or seed.
\v 10 We’ve lived in tents, and we’ve followed everything that our ancestor Yonadav told us,
\v 11 but when the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar attacked the land, we decided that we must move to Jerusalem to escape from the Babylonian and Aramean armies, so now we’re \add all\add* living in Jerusalem.
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh gave this message to Jeremiah, saying that
\v 13 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says to go and ask the people in Judah, including those who live in Jerusalem: Won’t you all accept correction and listen to my messages? This is Yahweh’s declaration:
\v 14 Rekav’s son Yonadav told his descendants not to drink any wine and they’ve followed that to this \add very\add* day. In contrast, I’ve personally and consistently spoken to all of you but you haven’t taken any notice of what I’ve said.
\v 15 I persistently sent all my servants, the prophets, out to you all to tell each person to turn from their wicked behaviour and start doing good instead, and to stop following after other gods and serving them. Then they’d live on the land that I gave to your ancestors and to all of you, however, you all won’t listen to me or pay attention to me.
\v 16 Yes, Yonadav’s descendants have followed the instructions that their ancestor gave them, but this people, refuses to obey me,
\v 17 so Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, says this: Listen, I’m sending all the disasters that I pronounced for Judah, including everyone living in Jerusalem. I’m sending them against them because I spoke to them, but they wouldn’t listen. I called to them, but they wouldn’t answer.
\p
\v 18 Then Jeremiah told the Rekavi clan that Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: You’ve all listened to the commands of your ancestor Yonadav and have kept them all. You’ve obeyed everything that he commanded you all to do,
\v 19 so Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: There’ll always be someone descended from Rekav’s son Yonadav to serve me.
\c 36
\s1 Baruk reads out Yahweh’s messages at the temple
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 In the fourth year \add of the reign\add* Judah’s King Yehoyakim (son of Josiah, Yahweh gave this message to Jeremiah:
\v 2 Get a scroll for yourself and write all the messages on it that I’ve told you concerning Israel and Judah, and all the other nations. Do this for everything I have said, \add back\add* from the time of Josiah until the present time.
\v 3 Perhaps Judah’s people will hear about all the disasters that I intend to bring on them and then turn away from their wicked ways, so I can forgive them for their wickedness and their disobedience.
\p
\v 4 Then Jeremiah called Neriyah’s son Baruk, and Jeremiah dictated all the messages that Yahweh had given to him, for Baruk to record on a scroll.
\v 5 Then Jeremiah instructed Baruk saying, “I’m restrained from entering Yahweh’s residence
\v 6 so you must go and read from the scroll that you wrote at my dictation. On the day of the fast, you must read Yahweh’s messages so that all the people in the temple will hear them as well as the \add people from\add* the rest of Judah’s cities. Declare these messages to them.
\v 7 Perhaps Yahweh will take notice of their pleas for mercy. Perhaps everyone will turn from their wicked ways, since the anger and fury that Yahweh has declared against this nation is severe.
\v 8 So Neriyah’s son Baruk did everything that the prophet Jeremiah had told him to—he read Yahweh’s messages aloud at Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 9 In the ninth month of the next year, all the people in Jerusalem and the visitors to the city from the rest of Judah, announced a fast to honour Yahweh.
\v 10 Again Baruk read Jeremiah’s messages aloud at Yahweh’s temple, from the room of Gemaryah (son of the scribe Shafan) in the upper courtyard, by the entrance gate to Yahweh’s residence. He did that so that all the people could hear it.
\s1 The messages get read to the officials
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 11 When Mikayah (son of Gemaryah son of Shafan) heard all Yahweh’s messages from the scroll,
\v 12 he went down to the secretary’s room in the palace, and wow, all the officials were sitting there: Elishama the secretary, Shemayah’s son Delayah, Akbor’s son Elnatan, Shafan’s son Gemaryah, and Hananyah’s son Zedekiah, along with all the \add other\add* officials.
\v 13 When Mikayah reported to them all the messages that he’d heard Baruk read aloud for the people to hear,
\v 14 the officials sent Jew son of Netanyah son of Shelemyah son of Ethiopian) to Baruk. Jew ordered Baruk, “Take the scroll in your hand, the scroll from which you were reading in the hearing of the people, and come.” So Neriyah’s son Baruk took the scroll and went to the officials
\v 15 where they told him to sit down and read it to them, so Baruk read out \add the messages\add*.
\v 16 When they heard it all, they were afraid and decided together that they needed to report it all to the king,
\v 17 and they asked Baruk, “How come you wrote down all these messages from Jeremiah?”
\p
\v 18 “He dictated them to me,” he replied, “and I just wrote them down on the scroll.”
\p
\v 19 Then the officials told Baruk, “Go \add and\add* hide, and Jeremiah \add as well\add*. Don’t let anyone know where you two are.”
\s1 King Yehoyakim burns the scroll
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 20 Then they left the scroll in the secretary Elishama’s room and went out to the king in the courtyard and told him everything \add they’d heard\add*.
\v 21 The king sent Jew to get the scroll, and he went and got it from the secretary Elishama’s room. Then Jew read it aloud for the king and all the officials who were standing beside him to hear.
\v 22 That month, the king was in his winter house and a brazier was burning in front of him.
\v 23 Whenever Jew had read a couple of columns, the king would slice it off with a knife and toss it into the fire until the entire scroll was gone,
\v 24 but neither the king or any of his servants were afraid of the messages (and they didn’t tear their clothes \add to indicate repentance\add*—
\v 25 Elnatan, Delayah, and Gemaryah had urged the king not to burn the scroll, but he hadn’t taken any notice of them).
\v 26 Then the king ordered a relative Yerahme’el, Azriel’s son Serayah, and Avdeel’s son Shelemyah to arrest the scribe Baruk and the prophet Jeremiah, but Yahweh had hidden them.
\s1 Jeremiah rewrites the scroll
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 27 After the king had burnt the scroll with the messages on it that Jeremiah had dictated to Baruk, Yahweh gave this message to Jeremiah:
\v 28 Get yourself another scroll and write down all the messages that were on the original scroll that Judah’s King Yehoyakim burnt.
\v 29 Then you must tell the king that Yahweh says: You burnt that scroll, asking why it was written that the Babylonian king would come and destroy this land and leave it without people or animals.
\v 30 Now Yahweh says about King Yehoyakim: Thebes descendant of his will ever sit on David’s throne. As for him, his corpse will be thrown out into the heat of the day and the frost at night.
\v 31 I’ll punish him, his descendants, and his servants for their wickedness. The disasters that I’ve threatened them with, but which they ignored, I’ll send onto them and all Jerusalem’s inhabitants, and onto every person in \add the rest of\add* Judah.
\p
\v 32 So Jeremiah got another scroll and gave it to Baruk, son of Neriyah the scribe. Jeremiah \add again\add* dictated all the messages that had been on the scroll burnt by Judah’s King Yehoyakim, and Baruk wrote them onto the scroll, along with several other similar messages.
\c 37
\s1 Jeremiah’s response to Zedekiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 By now Josiah’s son Zedekiah reigned as king instead of Yehoyakim’s son Yehoyakin. The Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar had made Zedekiah king over the Judah region.
\v 2 However, Zedekiah, his servants, and his people wouldn’t listen to Yahweh’s messages that he’d given via the prophet Jeremiah.
\p
\v 3 Eventually, King Zedekiah, Shelemyah’s son Yehukal, and Zephaniah son of Ma’aseyah the priest, sent a message to the prophet Jeremiah requesting him, “Pray on our behalf to our god, Yahweh.
\v 4 At that time, Jeremiah was \add still\add* coming and going among the people, because he hadn’t yet been put in prison.
\v 5 Pharaoh’s army had come out of Egypt, and the Babylonians who were besieging Jerusalem heard the news about them and retreated from Jerusalem.
\p
\v 6 Then Yahweh gave Jeremiah this message to declare:
\v 7 Israel’s god, Yahweh told me that you people should say this to Judah’s king who sent you all to consult me: Listen Pharaoh’s army which was coming out to help you all is about to return back to its own country, Egypt.
\v 8 Then the Babylonians will return and attack this city, then they’ll capture it and burn it down.
\v 9 Yahweh says not to deceive yourselves that the Babylonians have left, because they won’t leave,
\v 10 because even if you all defeated the entire Babylonian army that’s attacking you so that only wounded men were left in their tents, they would get up and burn down this city.
\s1 Jeremiah is arrested and imprisoned
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 11 Then when the Babylonian army had left Jerusalem to avoid Pharaoh’s army,
\v 12 Jeremiah left the city to go to the Benjamin region to take possession of a section \add of land\add* there among his people.
\v 13 As he went through the Benjamin gate, a chief guard named Yiriyah (son of Shelemyah, son of Hananyah) was there. He seized the prophet Jeremiah and claimed that he was deserting to the Babylonians.
\p
\v 14 But Jeremiah disputed, “That’s a lie—I’m not deserting to the Babylonians.” However Yiriyah wouldn’t listen to him and he arrested him and took him to the officials.
\v 15 The officials were angry at Jeremiah and they \add had\add* him beaten and put in prison. (\add The place\add* had been the house of the scribe Jonathan, but it had been converted into a prison.)
\v 16 So Jeremiah was kept there in an underground cell for several days.
\p
\v 17 Then King Zedekiah sent someone to fetch Jeremiah, and in his house, the king asked him privately, “Is there any message from Yahweh?”
\p “There is a message,” Jeremiah answered. “You’ll be handed over to the Babylonian king.”
\v 18 Then Jeremiah asked the king, “What crime have I committed against you or your servants or your people, that you’ve had me put in prison?
\v 19 Where are all the other prophets who prophesied to you all that the Babylonian king wouldn’t come here?
\v 20 But now, my master the king, please listen to this appeal: Don’t return me to the scribe Jonathan’s house, or I’ll die there.”
\p
\v 21 So King Zedekiah ordered that Jeremiah be held in the guards’ courtyard, and he was given a loaf of bread every day from the bakers’ street, until \add the time\add* when all the bread in the city ran out. But Jeremiah remained \add confined\add* in the guards’ courtyard.
\c 38
\s1 Jeremiah is placed down a shaft
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add Four officials,\add* Matan’s son Shefatyah, Pashhur’s son Gedalyah, Shelemyah’s son Yehukal, and Malkiyah’s son Pashhur heard the messages that Jeremiah was telling all the people, saying:
\v 2 Yahweh says this: Anyone who stays in this city will be killed by fighting, famine, or deadly diseases, but anyone who goes out to the Babylonians will survive—they won’t be killed.
\v 3 Yahweh says that this city will definitely be taken over and captured by the Babylonian king.
\p
\v 4 So those officials went to King Zedekiah and requested that Jeremiah be executed because \add (they claimed)\add* his messages were discouraging their warriors defending the city and discouraging all the people. They claimed that he wasn’t advancing the people’s safety, but \add causing\add* harm.
\p
\v 5 “All right,” King Zedekiah replied. “Do whatever you want with him, because no king can argue against all of you.”
\v 6 So they took Jeremiah and put him into the \add water storage\add* cistern \add carved out by\add* the king’s son Malkiyah. It was \add right there\add* in the guards’ courtyard, and they lowered Jeremiah down with ropes. There wasn’t any water \add left\add* in the cistern, but it was \add still\add* muddy \add at the bottom\add* and he sank down into the mud.
\p
\v 7 Now the Ethiopian Eved-Melek was one of the trusted servants in the king’s house and he heard that they had put Jeremiah down into the cistern. Meanwhile, the king was sitting at the Benjamin gate.
\v 8 So Eved-Melek left the palace and went and spoke with the king,
\v 9 “My master the king, those men have been evil in the way they’ve mistreated the prophet Jeremiah. They threw him into a cistern so he’d die in it from hunger, since there’s no food left in the city.”
\v 10 Then the king ordered Eved-Melek to take command of thirty of \add the king’s men\add* to go and get the prophet Jeremiah out of the cistern so he wouldn’t die \add down there\add*.
\v 11 So Eved-Melek led the men to a storeroom for clothing under the king’s house where they got some rags and worn-out clothes. Then they let them down by ropes to Jeremiah in the cistern
\v 12 and told him to use the rags and old clothes under his armpits to protect him from being hurt by the ropes. Jeremiah did that,
\v 13 then they pulled him up with the ropes—that’s how they got him up out from the \add mud in\add* the cistern. Then he stayed in the guards’ courtyard.
\s1 King Zedekiah’s advice to Jeremiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 14 One day King Zedekiah summoned the prophet Jeremiah to meet him at the third entrance to the temple and told him, “I want to ask you something. Don’t refuse to answer honestly and truthfully.”
\p
\v 15 “\add Ha,\add* if I tell you the truth, you’ll order me to be executed,” Jeremiah replied. “And if I give you good advice, you won’t take any notice of what I say.”
\p
\v 16 But King Zedekiah privately promised Jeremiah, “As our creator Yahweh lives, I won’t execute you or hand you over to those men who want to see you dead.”
\p
\v 17 “\add Okay,\add*” Jeremiah answered the king, “Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: If you indeed go out to the Babylonian king’s officials, then you’ll live, and this city won’t be burnt \add down\add*. You and your family would live.
\v 18 But if you don’t go out to his officials, then this city will be handed over to the Babylonians. They’ll burn it \add down\add*, and you wouldn’t \add be able to\add* escape from their power.”
\p
\v 19 “\add But\add* I’m afraid of the Jews who’ve \add already\add* deserted to the Babylonians,” the king confided to Jeremiah. “You see, I might be handed over to them and they might treat me cruelly.”
\p
\v 20 “The Babylonians won’t hand you over to those Jews,” Jeremiah responded. “Obey Yahweh’s message from Yahweh that I’m passing on to you, so that things will go well for you, and so that you’ll live \add through this\add*.
\v 21 However, if you refuse to surrender, then this is what Yahweh has shown me:
\v 22 Listen king of Judah, all the women who are left in your palace will be taken out to the Babylonian king’s commanders. Those women will tell you,
\q1 ‘You were deceived by your friends—they’ve ruined you.
\q1 \add Now\add* your feet are stuck in the mud, and your friends have turned away.’
\p
\v 23 Yes, all your wives and children will be taken out to the Babylonians and you yourself won’t escape from them \add either\add*. You’ll be taken captive by the Babylonian king, and this city will be burnt \add down\add*.”
\p
\v 24 Then Zedekiah told Jeremiah, “Don’t tell anyone else what we spoke about or you might die.
\v 25 If the officials hear that I’ve have talked with you, and if they come and demand, ‘Tell us what you said to the king—don’t hide it from us or we’ll kill you,’
\v 26 then you must tell them, ‘I was just pleading to the king not to return me to \add the dungeon in\add* Jonathan’s house or I’d die there.’
\v 27 Then \add sure enough\add* the officials came to Jeremiah and questioned him and he answered them as the king had instructed him. They couldn’t say anything else because they hadn’t heard the conversation,
\v 28 so Jeremiah remained in the guards’ courtyard until the day that Jerusalem was captured.
\c 39
\s1 The fall of Jerusalem
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 In the tenth month of the ninth year \add of the reign\add* of Judah’s King Zedekiah, the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar came against Jerusalem with his large army and besieged it.
\v 2 On the ninth day of the fourth month of Zedekiah’s eleventh year,\f + \fr 39:2 \ft i.e., about eighteen months later (in 586 B.C.).\f* the city was broken into.
\v 3 Then all of the Babylonian king’s officials came and sat at the middle gate, \add including\add* the top official Nevu-Sar-Sekim, and Samgar-Nevu and Sarsekim.
\p
\v 4 When Judah’s King Zedekiah and all his warriors saw \add what was happening\add*, they \add left\add* at nighttime through the gate between the two walls beside the king’s garden and fled towards the Jordan river plains.
\v 5 However the Babylonian army chased after them and overtook the king down in the plains near Jericho. They took him back up to the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar at Rivlah in the Hamat region, and he pronounced his judgement on him.
\v 6 The king had Zedekiah’s sons slaughtered at Rivlah as their father \add was forced to\add* watch, and Judah’s officials were also executed.
\v 7 Then they gouged out Zedekiah’s eyes and put him in chains to be taken to Babylon.
\v 8 Then the Babylonians burnt down the palace and the other houses, and tore down Jerusalem’s walls.
\v 9 The rest of the people who’d stayed in the city, including those who’d previously deserted, were exiled to Babylon \add under the supervision of\add* the chief bodyguard Nevuzar-Adan.
\v 10 However, he allowed the poorest people who didn’t own anything to remain in Judah—allocating vineyards and fields \add for them to work\add*.
\s1 Jeremiah is released
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 11 Now the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar had given an order to his chief bodyguard Nevuzar-Adan about \add finding\add* Jeremiah
\v 12 and keeping him safe and unharmed—even to care for him and do anything that he requested.
\v 13 So Nevuzar-Adan, the top servant Nevu-Shazban, the high official Nergal-Shar-Etser, and all the Babylonian king’s most important officials
\v 14 sent out men to fetch Jeremiah from the guards’ courtyard. They handed him over to Gedalyah (son of Ahikam, son of Shafan) to take him home, so Jeremiah stayed \add there\add* among the people.
\s1 Hope for Eved-Melek
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 15 While Jeremiah had still been held in the guards’ courtyard, Yahweh had given him this message:
\v 16 Go and tell Eved-Melek the Ethiopian that Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: Listen, I’m about to fulfill my messages against this city for disaster and not for good, and they’ll all come true right in front of your eyes on that day.
\v 17 But Yahweh declares that I’ll rescue you from those people that you’re afraid of
\v 18 because I’ll definitely save your life. You won’t be killed by the soldiers, but Yahweh declares that because you put your trust in me, your life will be saved.
\c 40
\s1 Jeremiah returns to Gedalyah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 The chief bodyguard Nevuzar-Adan had found Jeremiah chained up among all the exiles from Jerusalem and \add the rest of\add* Judah to be taken to Babylon, and had released him at Ramah.
\p
\v 2 The chief bodyguard took Jeremiah \add aside\add* and told him, “Your god Yahweh decreed this disaster for this place,
\v 3 then he caused it to happen. Yahweh did just as he’d decreed, since you people sinned against him and didn’t obey him. That’s why \add all\add* this has happened to you people.
\v 4 But now listen, I’ve released you today from your chains. If you choose to come with me to Babylon, I’ll look after you, but if you don’t want to, then look around and go wherever you think best.
\v 5 Or why don’t you return to Gedalyah (son of Ahikam, son of Shafan) who the Babylonian king has put in charge of Judah’s cities. Stay among the people with him, or go wherever you think best.” Then the king’s chief bodyguard gave him food and a gift, and sent him away.
\v 6 So Jeremiah went to Gedalyah’s \add place\add* and stayed with him among the people who were left behind in the land.
\s1 Judah’s governor Gedalyah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 25:22-24)
\p
\v 7 Now some commanders of \add Jew\add* soldiers who were still in the countryside, along with their men heard that the Babylonian king had made Ahikam’s son Gedalyah governor over the land. They also heard that he’d put him in charge of the men, women, and children who were the poorest people in the land—those who hadn’t been exiled to Babylon.
\v 8 So they went to Gedalyah at Mitspah. Those who went were Netanyah’s son Ishmael, Kareah’s sons Yohanan and Jonathan, Tanhumet’s son Serayah, the sons of Ofay the Netofati, and Ma’akati’s son Yezanyah, and their men.
\v 9 Gedalyah (son of Ahikam, son of Shafan) \add made a promise\add* to them and their men, saying, “Don’t be afraid to serve the Babylonian officials. Live in the land and serve the Babylonian king, and it will go well with you.
\v 10 See, I’m living in Mitspah to meet with the Babylonians who came to us. So harvest wine, summer fruit, and oil and store them in your containers. Live in the cities that you’ve occupied.”
\v 11 Then all the Jews in Moab, among the people of Amon, and in Edom, and in every land heard that the Babylonian king had allowed a small number of people to stay in Judah, and that he’d appointed Gedalyah (son of Ahikam, son of Shafan) over them.
\v 12 So all the Jews from every place that they’d be driven into, came \add back\add* to Judah to Gedalyah at Mitspah, and they harvested a lot of wine and summer fruit.
\s1 The murder of Gedalyah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 25:25-26)
\p
\v 13 \add Some time later,\add* Kareah’s son Jonathan and all the army commanders from the countryside, came to Gedalyah at Mitspah,
\v 14 and asked him, “Do you realise that the Ammonite King Ba’alis has sent Netanyah’s son Ishmael to assassinate you?” However Ahikam’s son Gedalyah didn’t believe them
\v 15 so Kareah’s son Yohanan spoke privately to Gedalyah at Mitspah, “Allow me to go \add and\add* kill Netanyah’s Ishmael. Thebes one will suspect me. Why should he kill you? Why allow all of Judah that has been gathered to you to be scattered and the remnant destroyed?”
\p
\v 16 However Ahikam’s son Gedalyah replied to Kareah’s son Yohanan, “\add Thebes,\add* don’t do that, because you’re lying about Ishmael.”
\c 41
\p
\v 1 However, in the seventh month, Ishmael (son of Netanyah, son of Elishama from the royal family) and some officers of the king came—he had ten men with him. They came to Ahikam’s son Gedalyah at Mitspah and had a meal together.
\v 2 Then Ishmael and the ten men who were with him, stood up and attacked with their swords. Ishmael killed Gedalyah, who the Babylonian king had put in charge of the region,
\v 3 and they killed all the Jews who were with Gedalyah \add there\add* at Mitspah, as well as the Babylonian soldiers who were there.
\p
\v 4 The next day before anyone found out that Gedalyah had been murdered,
\v 5 eighty men from Shechem, Shiloh, and from Samaria came who had shaved off their beards, torn their clothes, and cut themselves \add in mourning\add*. They were carrying grain offerings and incense to be offered at Yahweh’s residence.
\v 6 Netanyah’s son Ishmael went out of Mitspah to meet them, weeping as he went. When he reached them, he told them to come and see what had happened to Ahikam’s son Gedalyah.
\v 7 Then as they reached the middle of the town, Ishmael and his men slaughtered them \add and threw their bodies\add* into a cistern.
\p
\v 8 However, ten of them bargained with Ishmael about some wheat, barley, oil, and honey that they’d left out in the countryside, so he didn’t kill them along with the others.
\v 9 The \add water storage\add* cistern when Ishmael had thrown all the bodies of those he’d killed including Gedalyah, had been dug by King Asa in defence against Israel’s King Baesha. Now Netanyah’s son Ishmael had filled it with dead bodies.
\v 10 Next Ishmael captured the remaining people in Mitspah, including the king’s daughters and all the others who the chief bodyguard Nevuzar-Adan had assigned to Ahikam’s son Gedalyah. So Netanyah’s son Ishmael captured them to hand over to the Ammonites.
\p
\v 11 Meanwhile Kareah’s son Yohanan and all the army commanders with him, heard about all the evil things that Netanyah’s son Ishmael had done,
\v 12 so they took all their men to fight against Ishmael. They came across him at the large pool near Gibeon \add city\add*
\v 13 When all the people who’d been captured by Ishmael saw Yohanan and all the army commanders they were very relieved.
\v 14 So all those people that Ishmael had captured were freed and went back with Kareah’s son Yohanan.
\v 15 Unfortunately, Netanyah’s son Ishmael escaped from Yohanan, along with eight of his men, and they went to the Ammonites.
\p
\v 16 Then Kareah’s son Yohanan and all the army commanders with him, took all the people from Mitspah who’d been rescued from Netanyah’s son Ishmael after he’d killed Ahikam’s son Gedalyah. They took those warriors, and women and children and court-officials that they’d saved at Gibeon,
\v 17 and went and stayed at Gerut-Kimham near Bethlehem, getting ready to go to Egypt
\v 18 because of the Babylonians. (They worried about their response because Netanyah’s son Ishmael had assassinated Ahikam’s son Gedalyah who’d been appointed in the region by the Babylonian king.)
\c 42
\s1 The people ask Jeremiah to pray
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then all the army commanders, as well as Kareah’s son Yohanan, Hoshayah’s son Yezanyah, and all the people from the less prominent to the most important officials approached the prophet Jeremiah
\v 2 and asked him, “Please listen to our request. Please ask your god Yahweh our on behalf of all of us who are left here, because there’s not very many of us left as you can see.
\v 3 Ask your god Yahweh to tell us what we should do and where we should go.”
\p
\v 4 “\add Sure,\add* I’ve heard \add your concerns\add*,” the prophet Jeremiah responded. “Listen, Ill pray to your god Yahweh as you’ve all requested. Whatever Yahweh answers, I’ll tell you. I’ll keep nothing back from you all.”
\p
\v 5 “We \add promise\add* to do everything that your god Yahweh tells us to do,” they told Jeremiah. “If we don’t, then Yahweh can be a true and faithful witness against us.
\v 6 Whether it’s good or bad, we’ll obey the instructions from our god Yahweh who we’re asking you to speak to, so that it may be well with us when we obey his response.”
\s1 Yahweh’s answer to Jeremiah’s prayer
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 7 After ten days, Yahweh’s response came to Jeremiah,
\v 8 so he called Kareah’s son Yohanan and all the army commanders, as well as all the people from the less prominent to the most important officials,
\v 9 then he told them, “You all sent me to Israel’s god Yahweh to pass on your requests and here’s his reply:
\v 10 If you all stay in this region, then I’ll build you up and not tear you down. I’ll plant you and not pull you all out, because I’m upset about the harm that I’ve done to you all.
\v 11 Don’t be afraid of the Babylonian king any more. This is Yahweh’s declaration: Don’t be afraid of him because I’m with you all to save you and to rescue you from his clutches,
\v 12 because I’ll give you all mercy. I’ll have compassion on you, and I’ll restore you all back to your country.
\p
\v 13 However, suppose that you all decide not to listen to the words of your god Yahweh, and decide not to stay in this region,
\v 14 \add and\add* if you all say that you’ll go to Egypt to live there where there’s no war, and no trumpets signalling, and no shortage of food,
\v 15 Now \add you\add* remaining people listen to Yahweh’s message: Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says that if you all do decide to go and stay \add down\add* in Egypt,
\v 16 then the war that you all fear will overtake you in Egypt. The famine that you’re anxious about will follow you all to Egypt, and then you’ll die there,
\v 17 i.e., anyone who decides to go to live as foreigners in Egypt, will die from war, famine, or deadly diseases. There won’t be any survivors from the calamities that I’d send onto them.
\p
\v 18 Yes, Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: Just as my anger and rage was poured out on Jerusalem’s inhabitants, in the same way my anger would be poured out on all of you if you go to Egypt. You’d become an object of cursing and a horror, something to be used as a curse word, and something dishonourable, and you’d never see this place again.
\p
\v 19 \add Then Jeremiah\add* \add continued:\add* Yahweh has spoken to you all, the remaining people of Judah. Don’t go \add down\add* to Egypt as you know that I’ve definitely warned you all \add against that\add* today.
\v 20 Yes, you made a life and death mistake when you sent me to your god Yahweh, and asked me to ask him for instructions for you and when you all said that you’d do everything that he says,
\v 21 because I’ve reported all that to you today. However, you haven’t listened to your god Yahweh’s message, or to anything that he sent me \add to tell\add* you all about.
\v 22 So now, you should definitely be aware that you’ll die from war, famine, and deadly diseases where you all wanted to go and live as foreigners.
\c 43
\s1 Jeremiah gets taken to Egypt
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 When Jeremiah had finished passing on Yahweh’s messages that their god Yahweh had told him to say,
\v 2 Hoshayah’s son Azaryah, Kareah’s son Yohanan, and all the arrogant men told Jeremiah, “You’re lying \add to us\add*. Our god Yahweh didn’t send you to tell us not to go \add and\add* live in Egypt.
\v 3 \add It’s\add* because Neriyah’s son Baruk is inciting you against us \add so that\add* we’ll \add stay here and\add* get captured by the Babylonians to be \add either\add* put to death or be taken as exiles to Babylon.”
\p
\v 4 So Kareah’s son Yohanan, all the army officers, and all \add the rest of\add* the people, refused to take notice of Yahweh’s instructions to \add remain\add* living in the Judah region.
\v 5 Then Yohanan and all the army commanders gathered all those remaining Jew people together, who’d come from all the other countries that they’d previously been driven away to and returned to live in Judah.
\v 6 \add That included\add* men and women, children, the king’s daughters, and all the people that Nevuzar-Adan, the king’s chief bodyguard Nevuzar-Adan had left with Gedalyah (son of Ahikam, son of Shafan), and including the prophet Jeremiah and Neriyah’s son Baruk.
\v 7 Then they went to Egypt because they took no notice of Yahweh’s instructions and they arrived at Tahfanhes.
\p
\v 8 In Tahfanhes, Yahweh gave Jeremiah a message:
\v 9 Get some large stones and while the people from Judah are watching, push them into the mortar in the pavement at the entrance to Pharaoh’s palace at Tahfanhes.
\v 10 Then tell the Jews that Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief says: Listen, I’m about to send messengers to my servant the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar, to summon him \add here\add*. I’ll place his throne over the top of those hidden stones, and he’ll spread his royal tent over them.
\v 11 He’ll come here to attack Egypt and he’ll kill those that I’ve assigned to die, and similarly for those to be taken captive or run through with a sword.
\v 12 Then he’ll light a fire in the temples of Egypt’s gods. Nebuchadnezzar will \add either\add* burn them or carry them away. He’ll clean Egypt out just like shepherds pick lice off their clothes, and then he’ll quietly leave
\v 13 \add after\add* smashing the \add stone\add* pillars at Beyt-Shemesh and burning the temples of Egypt’s gods.
\c 44
\s1 Yahweh’s message to the Jews in Egypt
\p
\v 1 Yahweh spoke to Jeremiah about all the Jews who lived in Migdol, Tahfanhes, and Memphis in \add northern/lower\add* Egypt, as well as those in Patros \add in southern/upper Egypt\add*:
\v 2 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says that you yourselves have seen all the disasters that I sent to Jerusalem and Judah’s other cities. See, they’re \add just\add* ruins today without anyone living in them.
\v 3 That’s because of the wicked things they did to provoke me to anger by going to burn incense and to worship other gods that neither they themselves, nor you, nor your ancestors had ever heard about before.
\v 4 I repeatedly sent all my servants, the prophets, to them to tell the people to stop doing those detestable things that I hate,
\v 5 but they didn’t listen. They refused to take any notice of it, or to stop their wicked activities like burning incense to other gods.
\v 6 So my anger and rage was poured out and burnt in Judah’s cities and Jerusalem’s streets \add like a fire\add* and they’re \add still\add* desolate ruins to this day.
\p
\v 7 So now Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh asks why you all \add insist on\add* bringing this terrible harm onto yourselves \add that’ll see\add* men and women, children and babies from Judah, eliminated without even leaving a remnant.
\v 8 You’ve provoked me to anger by what you’ve all done with your \add own\add* two hands, by burning incense to other gods in Egypt where you’ve all gone to live as foreigners. You’ve cut yourselves off so as to become a curse \add word\add* and a reproach among all the nations in the world.
\v 9 Have you all forgotten the wicked things done by your ancestors and \add also\add* by Judah’s kings and their wives? Have you forgotten the evil committed by yourselves and your wives in Judah and on Jerusalem’s streets?
\v 10 To this day, they still haven’t humbled themselves. They don’t respect the instructions or regulations that I gave them and their ancestors, and so they don’t follow them.
\p
\v 11 Therefore Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief says this: Listen, I’m about to start working against you all to bring disaster and destroy all Judah.
\v 12 I’ll eliminate the small group of Jews that has made up their minds to go to live as foreigners in Egypt. I’ll do that so that they’ll all die there Egypt—from either war or famine, \add and\add* from the least to the very prominent people they’ll die and will become a curse word, a horror story, and something to ridicule and mock.
\v 13 I will punish those living in Egypt just like I punished Jerusalem with war, famine, and deadly diseases
\v 14 so that none of the remaining people from Judah who’ve gone to live in Egypt will escape or survive or \add be able to\add* return to Judah when they want to return and live there. None of them will return except a few who escaped from there.
\p
\v 15 Then all the men who knew that their wives were burning incense to other gods, and all the women who were in the great assembly, and all the people who were living in Lower and Upper Egypt, answered Jeremiah:
\v 16 \add Concerning\add* that message that you told us in Yahweh’s name, we’re not listening to you
\v 17 because we’ll certainly do everything that we said we’d do. We’ll burn incense to the queen of the skies and pour out drink offerings to her just as we, our ancestors, our kings, and our leaders did in Judah’s cities and on Jerusalem’s streets. Then we’ll have plenty of food and will prosper, and won’t experience distress.
\v 18 Whenever we didn’t do those things—not offering incense to the queen of the skies and not pouring out drink offerings to her—we were all suffering from poverty and dying from by war and famine.
\p
\v 19 The women said, “When we were offering incense to the queen of the skies and pouring out drink offerings to her, and when we were making cakes in her image and pouring out drink offerings to her, did you think we were doing it behind our husbands’ backs?”
\p
\v 20 Then Jeremiah responded to all those who’d answered him including both men and women. He told them all this message:
\v 21 Didn’t Yahweh remember the incense that you all burned in Judah’s cities and on Jerusalem’s streets—you and your ancestors, your kings and leaders, and the people of the land, and didn’t it come \add back\add* to his mind?
\v 22 Eventually, he could no longer bear it because of your wicked behaviour, \add i.e.,\add* because of the detestable things that you all did. Then your land became a deserted wasteland and a curse, without inhabitants as it is today.
\v 23 Because you all burnt incense \add to other gods\add* and sinned against Yahweh, and because you wouldn’t listen to his voice, his instructions, his regulations, or his promises, this disaster has come against you as \add you all can see\add* today.
\p
\v 24 Then Jeremiah told all the people, including all those women, “Listen to Yahweh’s message, all of \add you from\add* Judah who are \add now here\add* in Egypt.
\v 25 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says that both you and your wives have said out loud and confirmed with your actions that you’ll all certainly carry out your vows to worship the queen of the skies and to pour out drink offerings to her. So then, go ahead and do those things to fulfill your wows.
\v 26 So then all of Judah who’re staying Egypt, listen to Yahweh’s message: Yahweh says, listen, I’ve promised by my powerful name that none of the men from Judah who’re living \add here\add* in Egypt will call on my name any more, i.e., you’ll all no longer say, ‘As our master Yahweh lives.’
\v 27 Listen, I’m watching over them for disaster and not for good. Every Jew person in Egypt will meet their end through war or famine until they’re all eliminated.
\v 28 Then \add a few\add* survivors \add from the battles\add* will return from Egypt to Judah region—only a small number of them. In that way, all the remaining Jews who went to live in Egypt will know whose word will stand—mine or theirs.
\v 29 Yahweh declares that this will be the sign for you all: I’m going to punish you all in this place so that you’ll know that what I’ve said will come to pass, resulting in harm for all of you.
\v 30 Yahweh says: Listen, I’m about to hand the Egyptian king, Pharaoh Hofra over to his enemies and to those who want to kill him. It’ll be the same as when I handed Judah’s King Zedekiah king of Judah over to the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar—his enemy who wanted him dead.
\c 45
\s1 Yahweh’s promise to Baruk
\p
\v 1 This is the message that the prophet Jeremiah dictated to Neriyah’s son Baruk to have it written on a scroll in the fourth year \add of the reign\add* of Judah’s King Yehoyakim (son of Josiah:
\v 2 Israel’s god Yahweh says this to you Baruk:
\v 3 You’ve said, ‘I’m not doing well because Yahweh has piled sorrow on top of my pain. I’m exhausted from groaning and I can’t find \add any\add* peace.’
\p
\v 4 Yahweh says to tell Baruk, Listen, I’m about to tear down what I’ve built throughout the world, and to pull out what I’ve planted.
\v 5 But maybe you’re hoping for great things for yourself? Don’t hope for that, because listen, disaster is coming for all humanity, but wherever you go, I’m giving you your life as your ‘spoil of war’. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\c 46
\s1 Judgement for Egypt
\p
\v 1 These are the messages that Yahweh gave the prophet Jeremiah about \add other\add* nations:
\v 2 This is about the army of the Egyptian king Pharaoh Nekoh who was at Karkemish by the Euphrates river. That was the army that the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar defeated in the fourth year \add of the reign\add* of Judah’s King Yehoyakim, son of Josiah:
\q1
\v 3 Get the small shield and the large shields ready,
\q1 and go out for battle.
\q1
\v 4 Harness on the horses \add to the chariots\add*
\q1 Mount up on the horses and take your stand with your helmets \add on\add*.
\q1 Polish the spears and put on your armour.
\b
\q1
\v 5 What am I seeing here?
\q1 They’re filled with terror and they’re retreating,
\q1 because their warriors will be crushed.
\q1 They’re fleeing for safety without looking back.
\q1 Terror is all around.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 6 Don’t let the swift one flee,
\q1 and don’t let the warrior escape.
\q1 They’ll stumble and fall \add there\add*
\q1 in the north by the Euphrates river,
\q1
\v 7 Who’s this \add coming\add* who rises like the Nile,
\q1 whose waters surge up and down like the rivers?
\q1
\v 8 Egypt rises like the Nile,
\q1 like rivers of water that rise and fall, saying,
\q1 ‘I’ll go up and I will cover the earth.
\q1 I’ll destroy cities and their inhabitants.
\q1
\v 9 Go up, \add you\add* horses.
\q1 Be angry, \add you\add* chariots.
\q1 Let the soldiers go out,
\q1 Ethiopia and Libya,
\q1 men skillful with a shield,
\q1 and Ludia, men skillful at using bows \add and arrows\add*.’
\b
\q1
\v 10 That day will be the day of vengeance for the master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, when he’ll avenge himself on his enemies.
\q1 The sword will devour and be satisfied—it’ll drink its fill of their blood
\q1 because there’ll be a sacrifice to the master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh
\q1 in the northern land by the Euphrates river.
\q1
\v 11 Go up to Gilead and obtain medicine, \add you\add* virgin daughter of Egypt.
\q1 It’s vain to put that much medicine on yourself—there’s no cure for you.
\q1
\v 12 The nations have heard about your disgrace.
\q1 The world is filled with your wailing,
\q1 because warrior stumbles against warrior—
\q1 both of them fall together.
\s1 Nebuchadnezzar’s arrival
\p
\v 13 When the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar was coming to attack Egypt, Yahweh gave the prophet Jeremiah this message:
\q1
\v 14 Announce in Egypt, and declare it in Migdol, Memphis, and Tahfanhes:
\q1 ‘Take your station and prepare \add to defend\add* yourself,
\q1 because everyone around you will be killed.’
\q1
\v 15 Why are your powerful \add warriors\add* lying face down on the ground?
\q1 They can’t stand \add against the enemy\add*,
\q1 because I Yahweh, have pushed them to the ground.
\q1
\v 16 He increases the numbers of those who stumble.
\q1 Each soldier falls against the next one.
\q1 They’re saying, ‘Get ready.
\q1 Let’s go back to our own people, to our native land.
\q1 Let’s leave this oppressive war.
\b
\q1
\v 17 There \add in Egypt\add* they’ll call out,
\q1 ‘Our King Pharaoh is all noise—
\q1 he’s let his opportunity slip away.
\q1
\v 18 The king whose name is Commander-in-chief Yahweh declares:
\q1 ‘As \add surely\add* as I live
\q1 an invader is coming
\q1 \add who’s as tall\add* as \add Mt.\add* Tavor among the mountains,
\q1 and like \add Mt.\add* Carmel by the sea.
\q1
\v 19 You inhabitants of Egypt,
\q1 pack your own baggage to carry into exile,
\q1 because Memphis will become a wasteland—
\q1 it will lie in ruins and no one will live there.
\q1
\v 20 Egypt is a very beautiful young cow,
\q1 ^but a stinging insect is coming from the north.
\q1 It’s on its way.
\q1
\v 21 Its hired soldiers are like calves \add that have been protected and well-fed\add*,
\q1 ^but they’ll also turn away and run away.
\q1 They won’t stand together, because the day of their disaster is coming against them—
\q1 it’s time for their punishment.
\q1
\v 22 Egypt hisses like a snake and crawls away,
\q1 because her enemies are coming with \add a show of\add* strength.
\q1 They’re coming toward her like woodcutters with axes.
\q1
\v 23 Yahweh declares that they’ll cut down the forest
\q1 →so there’ll be nowhere to hide
\q1 because their soldiers will be more numerous than locusts
\q1 and no one will even be able to count them.
\q1
\v 24 Egypt’s inhabitants will be humiliated—
\q1 they’ll be handed over to those northern people.
\p
\v 25 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says, “Listen, I’m about to punish Amon \add the god\add* of Thebes \add city\add*, Egypt and her \add other\add* gods, its kings the Pharaohs, and \add all\add* those who trust in them.
\v 26 I’ll hand them over to those who want them dead, and over to Babylon’s King Nebuchadnezzar and his servants. But after that, Egypt will be inhabited as in past times. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 Yahweh’s rescue of Jacob’s descendants
\q1
\v 27 But you, my servant Jacob \add meaning Israel\add* \add )\add*, don’t be afraid.
\q1 Don’t be dismayed, Israel, because listen,
\q1 I’m about to bring you back from far away,
\q1 and your descendants from the land of their captivity.
\q1 →Then Jacob will return, find peace, and be secure,
\q1 and there’ll be no one to terrify him.
\q1
\v 28 This is Yahweh’s declaration:
\q1 You, my servant Jacob, don’t be afraid,
\q1 because I’m with you,
\q1 so I’ll send complete destruction to all the nations that I drove you into.
\q1 But I won’t completely destroy you,
\q1 ^although I’ll discipline you fairly,
\q1 and I certainly won’t leave you unpunished.”
\c 47
\s1 Yahweh’s message to the Philistines
\p
\v 1 This is Yahweh’s message that he gave to the prophet Jeremiah concerning the Philistines before Pharaoh attacked Gaza.
\v 2 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, \add armies\add* are rising in the north \add like\add* floods of water.
\q1 They’ll be like an overflowing river.
\q1 Then they’ll overflow the land and everything in it—
\q1 its cities and its inhabitants.
\q1 So everyone will shout out \add for help\add*,
\q1 and all the land’s inhabitants will wail.
\q1
\v 3 At the sound of the stamping of their powerful horses’ hooves,
\q1 at the clattering of their chariots and the noise of their wheels,
\q1 fathers won’t help their children because of their own helplessness.
\q1
\v 4 The day is coming that will devastate all of the Philistines—
\q1 to cut every survivor off from Tyre and Sidon who wants to help them,
\q1 because Yahweh is devastating the Philistines—
\q1 the remnant who \add originally\add* came from Kaftor island.
\q1
\v 5 \add The people of\add* Gaza will shave their heads in mourning.
\q1 The remnant in Ashkelon’s valley will be silenced.
\q1 How much longer will you cut yourself \add in mourning\add* for?
\q1
\v 6 Oh no, sword of Yahweh, how long will it be until you stop?
\q1 Get back to your scabbard. Stop and be silent.
\q1
\v 7 How can it stop when Yahweh has commanded it,
\q1 when he has ordered it to attack Ashkelon and the \add other\add* cities along the coast?
\c 48
\s1 Moab’s punishment
\p
\v 1 Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says to Moab:
\q1 Nevo won’t end well because it’ll be devastated.
\q1 Kiryatayim has been humiliated and captured.
\q1 Its fortress has been disgraced and shattered.
\q1
\v 2 Thebes one will praise Moab again.
\q1 \add Their enemies\add* in Heshbon plotted disaster against them, \add saying,\add*
\q1 Let’s go and destroy it as a nation.
\q1 Madmen will be silenced after the sword finishes with you.
\q1
\v 3 Listen, a cry of distress \add is coming\add* from Horonayim,
\q1 where there’s ruin and terrible destruction.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Moab has been destroyed.
\q1 Its workers have made their cries heard.
\q1
\v 5 They weeps as they climb Luhit hill,
\q1 because on the way down to Horonayim,
\q1 shouts of distress will heard \add because of the\add* destruction.
\q1
\v 6 Flee, save your lives
\q1 →so you’ll be like a juniper bush in the wilderness.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Because of your trust in your practices and your wealth,
\q1 you too will be captured.
\q1 Then Chemosh will go away into captivity, along with its priests and leaders.
\q1
\v 8 The destroyer will come to every city—no city will escape.
\q1 The valley will perish and the plain will be devastated, as Yahweh has said.
\q1
\v 9 Give wings to Moab, because it must certainly fly away.
\q1 Its cities will become a wasteland without anyone living in them.
\p
\v 10 Anyone who’s slack doing Yahweh’s work should be cursed.
\q1 Anyone who’s afraid to use his sword to kill should be cursed.
\s1 Moab’s cities destroyed
\p
\v 11 Moab has felt secure since it was young.
\q1 It’s like its wine that’s never been poured from container to container.
\q1 It’s never gone into captivity.
\q1 →so it \add still\add* tastes as good as ever—
\q1 its \add nice\add* aroma hasn’t changed.
\p
\v 12 But Yahweh declares that the time is coming
\q1 when I’ll send along those who’ll tip it over
\q1 and empty out all its pots and smash its jars.
\q1
\v 13 Then Moab will be ashamed of \add their god\add* Chemosh
\q1 just as Israel’s people were ashamed of \add the golden calf\add* that they put their trust in at Bethel.
\q1
\v 14 How can you \add Moab soldiers\add* say, ‘We are warriors,
\q1 powerful fighting men’?
\q1
\v 15 Moab will be devastated and its cities attacked.
\q1 Its finest young men have gone down to the place of slaughter.
\q1 That is the king’s declaration—his name is Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\q1
\v 16 Moab’s disaster is coming soon.
\q1 Calamity is rushing towards it.
\q1
\v 17 You surrounding \add nations\add*, wail,
\q1 and all you who know Moab’s fame, shout
\q1 ‘Oh no, the strong staff, the splendid rod, has been broken.’
\q1
\v 18 Come down from your place of honour
\q1 and sit on the ground that’s \add dry and\add* thirsty,
\q1 you daughter living in Divon \add city\add*,
\q1 because the one who’ll destroy Moab will attack you—
\q1 the one who’ll destroy your fortifications.
\q1
\v 19 You people who live in Aroer \add city\add*,
\q1 stand on the road and watch.
\q1 Ask the escaping men and women what happened \add there\add*.
\q1
\v 20 Moab has been humiliated because it’s been shattered.
\q1 Wail and cry out.
\q1 Tell the people at the Arnon \add river\add* that Moab’s been devastated.
\p
\v 21 Now punishment has come to Moab’s high plateau:
\q1 to Holon, Yahats, Mofa’at,
\v 22 Divon, Nevo, Beyt-Divlatayim,
\v 23 Kiryatayim, Beyt-Gamul, Beyt-Me’on,
\v 24 Keriyot, and Batserah, and all of Moab’s towns, \add both\add* near and far.
\v 25 Moab’s power will be terminated and its dominance will be broken.
\s1 Moab’s humiliation
\p
\v 26 Make Moab drunk, because it acted proudly against Yahweh.
\q1 Let Moab wallow in its vomit,
\q1 and let it be an object of ridicule.
\q1
\v 27 Didn’t Moab make Israel an object of ridicule?
\q1 Weren’t they thought of as thieves,
\q1 such that you shook your head when you spoke about them.
\p
\v 28 Abandon the cities and camp on the cliffs, \add you\add* inhabitants of Moab,
\q1 and become like a dove that’s nesting over the mouth of a hole in the rocks.
\q1
\v 29 We’ve heard about Moab’s pride,
\q1 about its arrogance, haughtiness, pride, boastfulness,
\q1 and the conceit in its heart.
\m
\v 30 This is Yahweh’s declaration:
\q1 I myself know its arrogance
\q1 and their hollow boasting about things they haven’t \add even\add* done.
\q1
\v 31 So I’ll wail for Moab,
\q1 and I’ll cry out for all of Moab.
\q1 I’ll lament for the people of Kir-Heres.
\q1
\v 32 I’ll weep for you more than I did for Yazer, \add you\add* vine of Sivmah.
\q1 Your tendrils passed across the Dead sea and reached \add as far as\add* Yazer.
\q1 A destroyer has attacked your summer fruit and your grape harvest,
\q1
\v 33 so celebration and happiness have been taken away from the fruit trees and from Moab \add itself\add*.
\q1 I’ve put an end to the wine from their wine-pressing pits.
\q1 They won’t tread \add the grapes\add* with \add happy\add* shouts—
\q1 any shouts won’t be shouts of celebration.
\p
\v 34 From the shouts at Heshbon as far as El’aleh, their sound is heard at Yahats, from Tsoar to Horonayim \add and\add* Eglat-Shelishiyah, since even the water at Nimrim has dried up.
\v 35 I will put an end to \add anyone\add* in Moab who makes sacrifices on the hilltop shrines and burns incense to his gods. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 36 Because of all that, I groan inwardly for Moab and Kir-Hareset, like a flute \add at a funeral, knowing that\add* all the wealth that they acquired will be lost.
\v 37 Yes, every head will be shaved and all their beards will be clipped. They’ll all have cuts on their hands and \add be wearing\add* \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth around their waists.
\v 38 There’s wailing \add coming from\add* every \add flat\add* roof and from all the plazas in Moab because I’ve destroyed Moab like pottery that no one wants. That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 39 How it’s been shattered. How they \add keep\add* wailing. Moab turns its back in shame—it will become an object of derision and a terror to all those who are around it
\v 40 because Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, the enemy will swoop down like an eagle and spread out his wings against Moab.
\q1
\v 41 Keriyot has been captured, and its fortresses have been seized,
\q1 because on that day, Moab’s soldiers will be apprehensive like women in labour.
\q1
\v 42 Then Moab will be destroyed and no longer be a people \add group\add*,
\q1 because they thought they were better than Yahweh.
\q1
\v 43 Terror, and pits and traps, are coming to you inhabitants of Moab.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 44 Anyone who flees because of terror will fall into a pit,
\q1 and anyone who climbs out of the pit will be caught in a trap,
\q1 because I will bring this on them in the year when I punish them.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 45 Those who flee will stand in the shadow of Heshbon \add city\add* without any strength,
\q1 because will go out from Heshbon—flame from the middle of Sihon.
\q1 It’ll devour Moab’s forehead and the top of the heads of the rebellious people.
\q1
\v 46 You won’t end well Moab. You worshippers of Chemosh will be destroyed
\q1 because your sons and daughters will be captured and taken away.
\p
\v 47 However, in future years, Moab’s judgement will end \add and\add* I will restore Moab from captivity.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\c 49
\s1 Yahweh’s judgement for the Ammonites
\p
\v 1 Yahweh says this about the Ammonites:
\q1 Doesn’t Israel have any children \add of its own\add*?
\q1 \add Is there no one to inherit anything in Israel\add*?
\q1 Why does \add the god\add* Molech occupy Gad,
\q1 and its people live in their cities?
\v 2 So listen, Yahweh declares that the time is coming
\q1 when I’ll sound the signal for battle against Amon’s \add capital city\add* Rabah,
\q1 →so it’ll become a deserted heap and its villages will be set on fire,
\q1 because Yahweh says that Israel will drive out those who \add initially\add* drove them out.
\v 3 Wail, Heshbon, because Ai will be devastated.
\q1 Cry out \add and\add* put on \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth, \add you\add* daughters of Rabah.
\q1 Mourn and run back and forth \add trying to figure out what to do\add*
\q1 because Molech is going into captivity along with its priests and leaders.
\q1
\v 4 Why do you brag about your fertile valleys, \add you\add* faithless daughter?
\q1 You who trust in your wealth and think that no one would ever attack you.
\q1
\v 5 Listen, this is the declaration of my master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh:
\q1 I’m about to bring terror to you from all those who surround you.
\q1 You’ll all be driven away ahead of it
\q1 and there won’t be anyone to gather those running away.
\q1
\v 6 However in future years, I’ll restore Amon’s people from captivity.
\m That is Amon’s captivity.
\s1 Yahweh’s judgement for Edom
\p
\v 7 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this about Edom:
\q1 Isn’t there any wisdom left in Teyman \add district\add*?
\q1 Has \add good\add* advice disappeared from those who have understanding?
\q1 Has their wisdom become corrupted?
\q1
\v 8 Turn \add and\add* run away.
\q1 Stay in deep \add caves\add*, \add you\add* inhabitants of Dedan,
\q1 because I’m bringing Esau’s disaster onto Edom when I punish it.
\q1
\v 9 If grape harvesters came to you, wouldn’t they \add at least\add* leave a little bit behind?
\q1 If thieves came in the night, wouldn’t they steal only as much as they needed?
\q1
\v 10 However, I’ll strip Esau \add (i.e., Edom)\add* bare.
\q1 I’ve uncovered his secret places so he can’t hide himself.
\q1 His descendants and \add other\add* relatives and neighbours will be devastated and destroyed.
\q1
\v 11 Leave your orphans behind.
\q1 I’ll take care of their lives,
\q1 and your widows can rely on me.
\p
\v 12 Yes, Yahweh says this: Listen, \add even\add* those who didn’t deserve it, must certainly drink some of the cup \add of suffering\add*. Did you think you yourself would escape being punished? You won’t, because you’ll certainly be included in it
\v 13 because Yahweh declares that I’ve solemnly promised that Batserah will become a horror, a disgrace, a devastation, and an object for cursing. All its cities will be forever ruined.
\p
\v 14 I’ve heard a report from Yahweh,
\q1 and a messenger has been sent out to the nations,
\q1 ‘Gather together and attack Edom.
\q1 Get ready for battle.’
\v 15 Yes, listen, I’ve made you minor compared to the other nations,
\q1 despised among humankind.
\q1
\v 16 As for your fearsomeness, your arrogance has deceived you, inhabitants of places on the cliff—
\q1 you who’ve occupied the highest hills so that you may make your nest high like an eagle.
\q1 I’ll bring you down from there.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 17 Edom will become a horror to everyone passing by it.
\q1 Those who do will be appalled and hiss about all its disasters.
\q1
\v 18 Yahweh says that just like the overthrow of Sodom and Gomorrah and their neighbours,
\q1 no one will live there—no human would want to stay there.
\q1
\v 19 Listen, he’ll go up like a lion from the forests along the Jordan \add river\add* to the green pasturelands,
\q1 because I’ll quickly make Edom flee from it,
\q1 and I’ll chose someone to be charge of it.
\q1 Indeed, no one else is like me, and who can challenge my decision?
\q1 What shepherd can stand against me?
\q1
\v 20 So listen to the plans that Yahweh has decided against Edom—
\q1 the intentions that he’s formed against Teyman’s inhabitants.
\q1 They’ll be dragged away, even the smallest flock.
\q1 →Their pasturelands will be turned into ruined places.
\q1
\v 21 The ground will shake at the sound of their falling.
\q1 The sound of distressed shouts will be heard \add right across\add* at the Red sea.
\q1
\v 22 Listen, someone will ascend like an eagle, and swoop down and spread his wings over Batserah.
\q1 →Then on that day, Edom’s soldiers will be apprehensive like women in labour.”
\s1 Yahweh’s judgement for Damascus
\p
\v 23 Concerning Damascus:
\q1 Hamat and Arpad will be ashamed, because they’ve heard bad news.
\q1 They’ll melt away—
\q1 they’ll become as troubled as an ocean that cannot stay calm.
\q1
\v 24 Damascus will be disheartened.
\q1 It turns away to flee in panic.
\q1 Distress and pangs of pain seize it like a woman giving birth.
\q1
\v 25 How has that \add widely\add* praised city been abandoned—
\q1 the town that gave me happiness.
\q1
\v 26 Therefore its young men will fall in its plazas,
\q1 and all the fighting men will perish on that day.
\p That is the declaration of Commander-in-chief Yahweh,
\q1
\v 27 because I’ll light a fire on the wall around Damascus,
\q1 and it will burn up Ben-Hadad’s fortresses.
\s1 Yahweh’s judgement for Kidar and Hatsor
\p
\v 28 Concerning Kedar \add tribe\add* and Hatsor’s kingdoms (which were about to be attacked by Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar Yahweh says this:
\q1 Take action \add and\add* attack Kedar and destroy those eastern people.
\q1
\v 29 Their tents and their flocks will be taken,
\q1 along with their tent curtains and all of their equipment.
\q1 Their camels will be led away from them,
\q1 and warriors surrounding them will call out things that terrify them.
\p
\v 30 Yahweh says to you inhabitants of Hatsor:
\q1 Quickly, flee, hide in deep caves,
\q1 because the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar has made a plan
\q1 and plotted out his destiny for you all.
\p
\v 31 Yahweh says \add to the Babylonians\add*:
\q1 Take action \add and\add* attack that nation at ease—
\q1 that lives in safety.
\q1 They don’t have gates with bars across them,
\q1 and its people live by themselves.
\q1
\v 32 Their camels will become plunder,
\q1 and their many livestock will become \add war\add* plunder.
\q1 Then those who cut the corners \add of their hair\add*, I’ll scatter to every wind
\q1 and I’ll bring disaster on them from every direction.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 33 Hatsor will become a home for jackals—a permanent wasteland.
\q1 Thebes one will live there—no human would want to stay there.
\s1 Yahweh’s judgement for Eylam
\p
\v 34 At the beginning of the reign of Judah’s King Zedekiah, Yahweh gave this message about Eylam to the prophet Jeremiah.
\v 35 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, I’m about to break the \add power of\add* Eylam’s archers—
\q1 their main initial attack force.
\q1
\v 36 Then I’ll bring four winds against Eylam from four different directions,
\q1 and I will scatter them to all of those winds.
\q1 There’s no nation that those banished from Eylam won’t go to.
\q1
\v 37 So I’ll shatter Eylam in front of their enemies and those who want to see them dead.
\q1 In my furious anger, I’ll send disaster against them,
\q1 and then I’ll send war to follow them until I’ve annihilated them.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 38 Yahweh declares that I’ll put my throne in Eylam,
\q1 and will destroy its king and leaders from there,
\v 39 however in future years, I’ll restore Eylam’s \add people\add* from captivity.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\c 50
\s1 Yahweh’s judgement for Babylon
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave the prophet Jeremiah this message concerning Babylon, the country of the Chaldeans:
\q1
\v 2 Make a report to the nations and get them to listen to it.
\q1 Raise a signal flag and cause them to listen—don’t conceal it.
\q1 Tell them that Babylon will be captured \add and their ‘god’\add* Merodak \add (also called ‘Bel’)\add* will be humiliated.
\q1 Merodak will be completely disgraced—
\q1 its replicas will be humiliated \add as\add* its idols will be shattered.
\p
\v 3 A nation from the north will come against it, and make their land into ruins.
\q1 Thebes one will live in it—people and animals will all flee away.
\s1 The return of Israel and Judah
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 4 Yahweh declares that at a \add future\add* time, the people of Israel and the people of Judah,
\q1 will join together to go to their god Yahweh with weeping and a desire to worship him.
\v 5 They’ll ask the way to Zion and will set off toward it
\q1 to join themselves to Yahweh with a permanent commitment that will never be forgotten.
\p
\v 6 My people have been a lost flock.
\q1 Their shepherds have led them astray in the mountains.
\q1 They’ve gone from hill to hill.
\q1 They went—they’d forgotten the place where they’d lived.
\q1
\v 7 Everyone who found them devoured them
\q1 and their enemies told themselves that they weren’t guilty
\q1 because the people \add they attacked\add* had sinned against Yahweh who was their true home—
\q1 Yahweh, who their ancestors had placed their hope in.
\p
\v 8 \add You Jews\add*, leave Babylon.
\q1 Get out from the land of the Chaldeans.
\q1 Be like male goats that leave ahead of the rest of the flock,
\q1
\v 9 because listen, I’m about to set in motion and raise up a group of powerful nations from the north against Babylon.
\q1 They’ll line up against her—Babylon will be captured from there.
\q1 Their arrows will be like a skilled warrior who always returns and never empty-handed.
\s1 Babylon’s fall
\p
\v 10 The land of the Chaldeans will become plunder.
\q1 All those who plunder it will take everything they want.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration
\q1
\v 11 because you’re so happy \add Babylon\add*—your people celebrate the plundering of my inheritance.
\q1 You skip around like a calf stamping in its pasture.
\q1 You neigh like a powerful horse.
\q1
\v 12 Your mother will be terribly ashamed—
\q1 the one who bore you will be embarrassed.
\q1 Listen, she’ll become the least of nations—
\q1 a wilderness, a dry land, and a desert.
\q1
\v 13 Due to Yahweh’s severe anger, Babylon won’t remain inhabited, but will become a complete ruin.
\q1 Everyone who passes by will be appalled, and will hiss because of all its wounds.
\p
\v 14 \add You armies\add*, line yourselves up all around Babylon—
\q1 everyone who uses a bow \add and arrows\add* must shoot at it.
\q1 Don’t hold back any of your arrows, because it has disobeyed Yahweh.
\q1
\v 15 Shout \add together\add* all of you who are around the city.
\q1 It has surrendered—its towers have been taken over.
\q1 Its walls are torn down, because that is Yahweh’s vengeance.
\q1 Avenge yourselves on Babylon—
\q1 do to it just like what it’s done \add to others\add*.
\q1
\v 16 Destroy both the Babylonian farmer who sows seed
\q1 and the one who uses a sickle at harvest time.
\q1 Instead of the oppressor’s sword, let each person return to their own people—
\q1 let everyone flee \add back\add* to their own region.
\s1 Israel’s return
\p
\v 17 Israel is like sheep that were scattered and driven away by lions.
\q1 Firstly, the Assyrian king devoured its \add meat\add*,
\q1 then after that, the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar chomped on its bones.
\q1
\v 18 Therefore Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, says this:
\q1 Listen, I’m about to punish the Babylonian king and his country,
\q1 just as I punished the Assyrian king.
\q1
\v 19 Then I’ll restore Israel to its homeland—
\q1 it will graze on \add Mt.\add* Carmel and in the Bashan \add region\add*.
\q1 then it will be satisfied in the Ephraim hill country and Gilead.
\q1
\v 20 Yahweh says that at that time,
\q1 Israel’s disobedience won’t exist
\q1 and no wickedness will be found in Judah
\q1 because I’ll forgive those that I leave as a remnant.
\s1 Yahweh’s judgement for Babylon
\p
\v 21 Go up against the Meratayim region \add in Babylon\add*
\q1 as well as Pekod’s inhabitants.
\q1 Attack them and ensure that they’re eliminated.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration—\add make sure to\add* do everything that I’m commanding you \add to do\add*.
\q1
\v 22 \add There’s\add* a sound of battle and terrible destruction across the land.
\q1
\v 23 How the ‘hammer’ of all the lands has \add itself\add* been cut apart and destroyed.
\q1 How Babylon has become an \add object of\add* horror among the nations.
\q1
\v 24 I set a trap for you and you were caught, Babylon, and you didn’t \add even\add* know it.
\q1 You were found and captured, because you went against Yahweh.
\q1
\v 25 Yahweh has opened his armoury and is bringing out the weapons for carrying out his anger.
\q1 There’s work for the master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, in \add Babylon\add*—the land of the Chaldeans.
\q1
\v 26 Come to it from far away.
\q1 Open its store-houses \add full\add* of grain.
\q1 Pile \add everything\add* up in heaps.
\q1 Completely destroy everything—don’t leave anyone alive.
\q1
\v 27 Kill all its young bulls—send them off for slaughter.
\q1 They won’t end well because their moment has come—the time for their punishment.
\p
\v 28 \add There’s\add* the sound of those fleeing—escaping from Babylon
\q1 to announce in Zion that our god Yahweh is taking vengeance—
\q1 vengeance for \add what was done to\add* his temple.
\p
\v 29 Summon the archers against Babylon—all those who use bows and arrows.
\q1 Surround it completely and don’t let anyone escape.
\q1 Repay it for what it’s done.
\q1 Do to it what it did to others
\q1 because it had defied Yahweh, Israel’s holy one,
\q1
\v 30 so their young men will fall in the \add town\add* squares,
\q1 and all their fighting men will be killed on that day.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 31 Listen, Commander-in-chief Yahweh declares:
\q1 I’m against you, you proud \add nation\add*
\q1 because your time is up, you proud one—
\q1 the time has come for me to punish you.
\q1
\v 32 So the proud one will stumble and fall,
\q1 and no one will be there to help it up.
\q1 I’ll light a fire in its cities and it will devour everything around it.
\p
\v 33 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 The Israel’s people are oppressed,
\q1 as are the people from Judah.
\q1 All those who captured them have kept hold of them—
\q1 they’ve refused to let them go.
\q1
\v 34 The one who paid the price to free them is strong.
\q1 His name is Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\q1 He’ll successfully plead their case,
\q1 in order to bring peace and stability to the land,
\q1 and to cause turmoil for Babylon’s inhabitants.
\p
\v 35 Yahweh declares that war is coming to the Chaldeans
\q1 including its inhabitants, leaders, and wise men.
\q1
\v 36 War is coming for empty talkers and they’ll become fools.
\q1 Battles are coming against its warriors, and they’ll be terrified.
\q1
\v 37 Attacks are coming against their horses,
\q1 their chariots and all of Babylon’s foreign mercenaries,
\q1 →so they’ll become like women.
\q1 Armed raids are coming against its storerooms,
\q1 →and they’ll \add all\add* be looted.
\q1
\v 38 A drought is coming on its water supplies so they’ll dry up,
\q1 because it’s a land \add full\add* of idols,
\q1 and the people do mad things trying to appease them.
\p
\v 39 So jackals and \add other\add* desert animals will live there,
\q1 along with ostriches that will breed there.
\q1 People will never live there again—
\q1 future generations won’t live there.
\q1
\v 40 Just like the overthrow of Sodom and Gomorrah and its neighbours,
\q1 Yahweh declares that no one will live there—no human would want to stay there.
\q1
\v 41 Listen, a people \add group\add* is coming from the north—
\q1 a powerful nation and many kings are being stirred up from the farthest parts of the world.
\q1
\v 42 They’ll be carrying bows and spears.
\q1 They’re cruel and will show no mercy.
\q1 The sound \add of them coming\add* will be like the roar of the sea
\q1 They’ll be riding horses in formation,
\q1 organised for battle, against you, daughter of Babylon.
\q1
\v 43 The Babylonian king will hear their report
\q2 and his hands will fall limp in distress—
\q1 he’ll be in anguish like a woman giving birth.
\p
\v 44 Listen, \add there’ll be one who\add* comes like a lion
\q1 from the thickets along the Jordan, up to the watered pastureland
\q1 because I’ll act quickly to cause them to run away from there,
\q1 and I’ll appoint someone \add specially\add* chosen for it,
\q1 because who is like me, and who can summon me \add to come\add*?
\q1 What shepherd is able to resist me?
\q1
\v 45 So listen to the plans that Yahweh has decided against Babylon—
\q1 the plans that he has planned against the land of the Chaldeans.
\q1 They’ll definitely be dragged away—even the smallest flock.
\q1 Their pasturelands will be ruined.
\q1
\v 46 The sound of Babylon being conquered Babylon will make the earth quake,
\q1 and their cry of distress will be heard right across the continent.
\c 51
\s1 Yahweh says more against Babylon
\p
\v 1 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, I’m about to stir up a destructive wind against Babylon
\q1 and against the Babylonian people.
\q1
\v 2 I’ll send foreigners into Babylon, and they’ll shake it down and devastate its land,
\q1 because they’ll come against it from all sides on that disastrous day.
\q1
\v 3 Don’t let the archers shoot their arrows
\q1 Don’t let their soldiers put on their armour.
\q1 Don’t spare their young men—
\q1 Destroy their army completely.
\q1
\v 4 Their wounded will fall in the land of the Chaldeans.
\q1 Those who’re stabbed will fall in its streets,
\q1
\v 5 because Israel and Judah haven’t been abandoned by their god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh,
\q1 even though their\f + \fr 51:5 \ft This pronoun could refer either to Israel and Judah, or to Babylon, so other translations might take the alternative rendering here.\f* \add own\add* land is filled with offenses committed against Israel’s holy one.
\q1
\v 6 Escape quickly from out of Babylon—each person must save themself.
\q1 Don’t die \add there\add* because of their wickedness
\q1 because \add now\add* is the time of Yahweh’s vengeance.
\q1 He’ll repay Babylon for everything they’ve done.
\q1
\v 7 Babylon was \add like\add* a golden cup in Yahweh’s hand that made all the world drunk—
\q1 nations drank its wine and went mad.
\q1
\v 8 Suddenly Babylon will fall and be destroyed.
\q1 Wail for it—give it medicine for its pain—perhaps it can be healed?
\q1
\v 9 We \add tried to\add* heal Babylon, but it never recovered,
\q1 →\add so\add* leave it and let everyone go back to their own region,
\q1 because its guilt has reached up to the heavens—
\q1 it’s piled up to the clouds.
\p
\v 10 Yahweh has \add now\add* decided that we’ve served our time.
\q1 Let’s go and tell \add everyone\add* in Zion what our god Yahweh has done.
\p
\v 11 Sharpen your arrows and strap on your shields.
\q1 Yahweh is stirring up the spirit of the kings of the Medes in his plan to destroy Babylon
\q1 because of Yahweh’s vengeance—revenge for \add their destruction of\add* his temple.
\q1
\v 12 Lift up your banner over Babylon’s walls.
\q1 Reinforce the guards. Assign watchmen.
\p Prepare your ambushes because Yahweh will do what he has said concerning Babylon’s inhabitants.
\q1
\v 13 \add You people\add* who live by many water \add supplies\add* and who are rich with treasures, your end has come.
\q1 The thread of your life is \add now\add* cut short.
\v 14 Commander-in-chief Yahweh has made an oath by his own life:
\q1 ‘I’ll fill you with men, like a swarm of locusts,
\q1 and they’ll shout out against you.’
\s1 A song praising Yahweh
\q1
\v 15 He has made the earth by his power.
\q1 He set the world in place by his wisdom.
\q1 By his understanding he stretched out the skies.
\q1
\v 16 When he makes thunder, there’s the roar of waters in the skies,
\q1 because he brings the mist up from the ends of the earth.
\q1 He makes lightning to go with the rain
\q1 and sends out wind from his storehouses.
\q1
\v 17 All people have become ignorant—without knowledge.
\q1 Every metalworker is put to shame by his idols.
\q1 →because his cast images are lifeless frauds.
\q1
\v 18 They are the worthless work of mockers
\q1 who’ll perish at the time of their punishment.
\q1
\v 19 Israel’s god is not like those,
\q1 because he’s the one who formed everything.
\q1 \add Israel is\add* the tribe of his inheritance.
\q1 Commander-in-chief Yahweh is his name.
\s1 Yahweh’s hammer
\p
\v 20 You are my sledge-hammer—my weapon for battle—
\q1 →With you I’ll smash nations and destroy kingdoms.
\q1
\v 21 With you I’ll smash horses and their riders.
\q1 With you I’ll smash chariots and their drivers.
\q1
\v 22 With you I’ll smash men and women.
\q1 With you I’ll smash the old and the young.
\q1 With you I’ll smash the young men and young women.
\q1
\v 23 With you I’ll smash the shepherds and their flocks.
\q1 With you I will smash the plowmen and their teams \add of cattle\add*.
\q1 With you I’ll smash the governors and officials.
\s1 Babylon’s punishment
\p
\v 24 I’ll repay Babylon and all the Chaldeans
\q1 for all the evil they did in Zion as you watched.\f + \fr 51:24 \ft The segment ‘as you watch(ed)’ could also apply to Babylon getting repaid, so other translations might choose differently here.\f*
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 25 Listen, Yahweh declares that I’m against you,
\q1 \add you\add* mountain of destruction that destroys the whole earth.
\q1 I’ll stretch my hand out against you, and roll you \add down\add* from the cliffs,
\q1 and turn you into a charred mountain
\q1
\v 26 so they won’t quarry any stone from you to construct a building’s corner or foundation.
\q1 because you’ll become a permanent wasteland.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 27 Lift up a banner over the earth.
\q1 Blow the trumpet across the nations.
\q1 Call the nations to attack it: Ararat, Mini, and Ashkenaz.
\q1 Appoint a commander to attack it.
\q1 Bring in horses like swarming locusts.
\q1
\v 28 Prepare the nations to attack it:
\q1 The kings of the Medes and their governors,
\q1 all of their officials and all the land under their control.
\q1
\v 29 The land will shake and writhe \add in agony\add*,
\q1 since Yahweh’s plans against Babylon will be fulfilled:
\q1 →to make Babylon into a deserted wasteland.
\q1
\v 30 The Babylonian soldiers have given up the fight—they stay in their fortresses.
\q1 Their strength has run out—they’ve become \add like\add* women.
\q1 The \add locking\add* bars across its gates are broken \add and\add* its homes are on fire.
\q1
\v 31 A messenger runs \add across\add* to pass the news onto another messenger,
\q1 and a runner tells another runner to report to the Babylonian king
\q1 that his city has been taken over from one end to the other.
\q1
\v 32 The fords \add over the river\add* have been seized
\q1 the reed marshes have been burnt \add off\add*,
\q1 and Babylon’s soldiers are terrified
\q1
\v 33 Yes, Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh, says this:
\q1 The daughter of Babylon is like a threshing floor—it’s time to trample it down.
\q1 In a little while, the time will come for it to be harvested.
\q1
\v 34 The Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar has devoured me.
\q1 He’s driven me into confusion and made me into an empty pot.
\q1 Like a monster, he’s swallowed me—filled his stomach with my delicacies,
\q1 →and then he spat me out.
\q1
\v 35 The one who lives in Zion will say,
\q1 ‘May the violence done to me and to my flesh be on Babylon.’
\q1 Jerusalem will say,
\q1 ‘May my blood be on Chaldeans’s inhabitants.’
\s1 Yahweh’s assistance to Israel
\p
\v 36 Therefore Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, I’m about to plead your case and bring about vengeance for you
\q1 because I’ll dry up Babylon’s river and make its springs run dry.
\q1
\v 37 Babylon will become heaps of rubble without any inhabitants—
\q1 a den of jackals, a horror, and something to whistle at.
\q1
\v 38 The Babylonians will roar together like young lions.
\q1 They will growl like lion cubs.
\q1
\v 39 When they become hot with greed, I’ll make a feast for them
\q1 and I’ll get them drunk so they can celebrate.
\q1 →Then they’ll enter into permanent sleep and not wake up.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 40 I’ll send them down to the slaughter,
\q1 like lambs, rams, \add and\add* male goats.
\s1 Punishing Babylon and its god
\p
\v 41 Wow, Babylon \add Heb. Sheshak)\add* has been captured.
\q1 \add The city\add* praised by the whole world has been seized.
\q1 Wow, Babylon has become a horror among the nations.
\q1
\v 42 The sea has come up over Babylon.
\q1 It’s been covered by its violent waves.
\q1
\v 43 Its cities have become a wasteland, a dry, wilderness region—
\q1 an area that no one lives in, and no human being \add even\add* passes through.
\q1
\v 44 I’ll punish Babylon’s \add god\add* Bel
\q1 and I’ll pull what he swallowed \add back\add* out of his mouth,
\q1 →Then the nations will no longer go streaming to him \add to placate him\add*.
\q1 What’s more, Babylon’s walls will fall.
\q1
\v 45 Come out from Babylon, my people.
\q1 Each of you \add should\add* save their own life from Yahweh’s furious anger.
\q1
\v 46 Don’t be discouraged or afraid due to the report that’s heard in the land,
\q1 as there’ll be a report one year and the again the year after.
\q1 There’ll be violence will in the world—ruler will be against ruler.
\q1
\v 47 Therefore, listen, the time is coming when I’ll punish Babylon’s carved idols.
\q1 \add People\add* across their country will be ashamed,
\q1 and their slaughtered soldiers will all fall right there in their country.
\q1
\v 48 Then the heavens and the earth, and everyone in them will celebrate over Babylon’s \add fall\add*
\q1 because destroyers from the north will come for it
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\v 49 As Babylon has made Israel’s slain warriors fall,
\q1 so too their slain will fall in Babylon.
\s1 Yahweh’s message to Israelites in Babylon
\p
\v 50 \add You all\add* who escaped from being killed in war, go away—don’t stay still.
\q1 Remember Yahweh even though you’re all far away.
\q1 Start bringing Jerusalem to mind.
\q1
\v 51 We’re ashamed because we’ve heard insults—
\q1 our feeling of being disgraced can be seen on our faces,
\q1 because foreigners have entered the sacred places in Yahweh’s house.
\q1
\v 52 So Yahweh declares:
\q1 Listen, the time is coming when I’ll punish Babylon’s carved idols,
\q1 and their wounded people will groan across that entire region.
\q1
\v 53 Yes, even if Babylon built tall towers or fortified their highest fortresses,
\q1 destroyers that I’ve sent would \add still\add* go there.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\s1 More on Babylon’s destruction
\p
\v 54 A cry of distress came from Babylon—
\q1 terrible destruction from the land of the Chaldeans
\q1
\v 55 because Yahweh is about to destroy Babylon.
\q1 He’s causing its loud voice to die away.
\q1 Their enemies roar like violent ocean waves—
\q1 their noise becomes very prominent
\q1
\v 56 because a destroyer has come against it, against Babylon,
\q1 and their soldiers have been captured.
\q1 Their weapons are shattered because Yahweh is a god of vengeance—
\q1 he’ll definitely repay them fully.
\q1
\v 57 I’ll make its princes, advisors, officials, and its soldiers drunk,
\q1 and they will sleep in a permanent stupor and never wake up.
\q1 That is the king’s declaration—Commander-in-chief Yahweh is his name.
\q1
\v 58 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 Babylon’s thick walls will be completely demolished,
\q1 and its tall gates will be set on fire.
\q1 The peoples \add coming to their aid\add* will waste their time trying—
\q1 everything that the nations try to do for it will be burnt up.
\s1 Jeremiah’s message to be read out in Babylon
\p
\v 59 This is the message that the prophet Jeremiah commanded Serayah (son of Neriyah, son of Mahseyah)
\q1 when he went with Judah’s King Zedekiah to Babylon in the fourth year of his reign.
\q1 Now Serayah was a chief officer,
\q1
\v 60 and Jeremiah had written on a scroll about all the disaster that was going to come to Babylon—
\q1 all these messages that were written about Babylon.
\q1
\v 61 Then Jeremiah told Serayah, “When you go to Babylon, ensure that you read these messages aloud
\v 62 and then pray, ‘Yahweh, you yourself have declared that you would destroy this place, and then that no man or animal will live in it and it’ll be desolate forever.’
\v 63 Then when you’ve finished reading out this scroll, tie a stone to it and throw it into the middle of the Euphrates \add river\add*.
\v 64 Say, ‘Babylon will sink like that. It won’t \add ever\add* recover from the disaster that I’m sending against it, and they’ll become irrelevant’
\m Jeremiah’s messages end here.
\c 52
\s1 Jerusalem’s fall and Zedekiah’s end
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 24:18–25:7)
\p
\v 1 Zedekiah was twenty-one when he began to reign \add and\add* he reigned from Jerusalem for eleven years. His mother’s name was Hamutal. (She was the daughter of Jeremiah from Livnah.)
\v 2 He did what was evil in Yahweh’s sight just as \add his father\add* Yehoyakim had done.
\v 3 All those events happened in Jerusalem and \add across the rest of\add* Judah because of Yahweh’s anger, until he drove them out of his sight.
\p Then Zedekiah rebelled against the Babylonian king,
\v 4 so on the 10th of the tenth month of the ninth year of King Zedekiah’s reign, the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar came and surrounded Jerusalem with all his army. They took action to put the city under siege
\v 5 and the it remained under siege until the eleventh year of King Zedekiah’s \add reign\add*.
\v 6 By the 9th of the fourth month, the famine was so severe in the city that there was no \add more\add* food for the residents.
\v 7 Then the city was broken into, and all the fighting men fled out of the city at night by the way of the gate that was between the two walls near the king’s garden, even though the Chaldeans were all around the city. So they went in the direction of the Aravah \add plain along the Jordan\add* \add river\add*.
\v 8 But the Chaldean army chased after the king, and overtook Zedekiah in the Jordan river plains near Jericho. All his army was then scattered away from him.
\v 9 They captured the king and brought him up to the Babylonian king at Rivlah in Hamat region, where he passed sentence on him.
\v 10 Then the Babylonian king slaughtered Zedekiah’s sons right there in front of his eyes, and he also slaughtered all of Judah’s leaders there at Rivlah.
\v 11 Then he had Zedekiah’s eyes gouged out, shackled him with bronze chains, and took him to Babylon where the Babylonian king put him in prison until he died there.
\s1 The Babylonians destroy the temple
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 25:8-17)
\p
\v 12 Then on the 10th of the fifth month in the nineteenth year of the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar’s reign, Nevuzar-Adan came to Jerusalem. He was the commander of the king’s bodyguards and a servant of the Babylonian king.
\v 13 He burnt \add down\add* Yahweh’s house, the king’s palace, and all the homes in Jerusalem, as well as every important building \add in the city\add*.
\v 14 As for the walls all around Jerusalem, they were pulled down by the Babylonian army who were with the commander of the bodyguards.
\v 15 Some of the poorer people and the rest of the people who remained in the city, as well as those who’d defected to the Babylonian king, and the rest of the craftsmen were taken into exile by Nevuzar-Adan,
\v 16 However, Nevuzar-Adan, the commander of the bodyguards, left some of the poorest people to work the vineyards and the fields.
\p
\v 17 As for the bronze pillars that were part of Yahweh’s temple, and the \add large\add* bronze \add basin called\add* ‘The Sea’, and the stands that were in it, the Chaldeans broke them into pieces and carried all the bronze back to Babylon.
\v 18 The pots, shovels, lamp trimmers, bowls, and all the bronze utensils that \add the priests\add* had used when serving \add in the temple\add*, were all taken away by the Chaldeans.
\v 19 Also, the basins and incense burners, the bowls, pots, lampstands, pans, and basins that were made of gold or silver, were taken away by the commander of the king’s bodyguards.
\v 20 The two pillars, \add the large bronze basin known as\add* ‘The Sea’, and the twelve bronze bulls that were under the stands, things that Solomon had made for Yahweh’s temple, contained too much bronze to weigh.
\v 21 (Each pillar was eight metres high with a circumference of nearly six metres. Each pillar was hollow with a 8cm thick \add wall\add*
\v 22 with a bronze capital on the top. Each capital was over two metres high, with bronze latticework and bronze pomegranates all around.
\v 23 There were ninety-six pomegranates on the sides of each capital, and another hundred pomegranates above the surrounding latticework.)
\s1 The people of Judah are exiled to Babylon
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 25:18-21, 27-30)
\p
\v 24 The commander of the bodyguards took the high priest Serayah prisoner, along with the second-level priest Zephaniah, and the three gatekeepers.
\v 25 From the city, he took an army commander prisoner, as well as seven of the king’s advisers who were \add still\add* in the city. He also took prisoner the king’s army recruitment officer, along with sixty important men from the country who were in the city.
\v 26 Then Nevuzar-Adan, the commander of the bodyguards, took them to the Babylonian king at Rivlah,
\v 27 and the king put them to death \add there\add* at Rivlah in the Hamat region.
\p So Judah’s \add people\add* were taken out of their country \add and\add* into exile.
\v 28 These were the people who Nebuchadnezzar exiled:
\li1 In Nebuchadnezzar’s seventh year, 3,023 Jews.
\li1
\v 29 In his eighteenth year, he took 832 people from Jerusalem.
\li1
\v 30 In his twenty-third year, Nevuzar-Adan, the commander of the king’s bodyguards, exiled 745 Jew people.
\m All those exiles totalled 4,600 people.
\p
\v 31 \add Some time later\add* on the 25th of the twelfth month of the thirty-seventh year of the exile of Judah’s King Yehoyakin, Babylon’s King Evil-Merodak (in the first year of his reign) released King Yehoyakin from prison.
\v 32 He spoke kindly to him and gave him a seat more honourable than that of the other \add exiled\add* kings who were with him in Babylon.
\v 33 Evil-Merodak supplied new clothes for Yehoyakin \add to wear\add*, and he ate regularly at the king’s table for the rest of his life.
\v 34 He was \add also\add* given a daily allowance by the king for the rest of his life.
